const e={1:{name:"Hudson Bay",area:"Hudson Bay and approaches, enclosed by northern Ontario, Manitoba, the Keewatin/Nunavut coast and northern Quebec (Ungava); Hudson Strait leads out east to zone 2.",lat:"55°N to 71°N",lon:"91°W to 67°W",borders:[2],history:"No naval battles, sinkings or U-boat operations are recorded inside Hudson Bay during the Second World War; its importance lay in its remoteness and in the northern air routes along its shores. The port of Churchill, Manitoba, had shipped prairie grain since 1931, but its short ice-free season, roughly late July to early November, and the long passage through Hudson Strait made it marginal in wartime. Shipping statistics show six grain ships in 1939, none in 1940, 1941 or 1942, a single ship, SS Tintagel, in August 1943, and none in 1944 or 1945. Instead the bay became part of the Crimson Route, a planned northern ferry route for aircraft to Britain ordered in May 1942. American forces built an airfield and base at Churchill in 1942-43, which became Fort Churchill, another airfield at Coral Harbour on Southampton Island, and a station at Fort Chimo (Crystal I) near Ungava Bay. Construction equipment for these sites was lost when a U-boat sank a supply ship off Labrador in August 1942. After Allied aircraft gained bases in the Azores in 1943 the route was abandoned, and the Churchill and Southampton Island airfields passed to Canada in 1944.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crimson_Route","https://mhs.mb.ca/docs/sites/churchillelevator.shtml","https://mhs.mb.ca/docs/sites/fortchurchill.shtml","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_of_Churchill"]},2:{name:"Baffin Bay & Davis Strait",area:"Between Baffin Island/Labrador's northern tip and west Greenland; covers Baffin Bay, Davis Strait and the Labrador Sea's northern part.",lat:"57°N to 72°N",lon:"71°W to 47°W",borders:[1,3,8],history:"These Arctic waters mattered because of Greenland, which the United States took under protection by an agreement of 9 April 1941 after Germany occupied Denmark. Airfields such as Bluie West Eight at Søndre Strømfjord were built in 1941, an airfield and weather station, Crystal II, was begun at Frobisher Bay on Baffin Island, and the cryolite mine at Ivigtut, essential for aluminium production, was guarded and supplied by sea. The US Coast Guard's Greenland Patrol escorted the small convoys to these bases. The worst loss came on 3 February 1943, when U-223 torpedoed the Army transport Dorchester of convoy SG-19 about 150 miles west of Cape Farewell; about 675 of the 904 aboard died, among them the 'Four Chaplains' who gave away their life jackets. The cutters Escanaba and Comanche rescued about 230 survivors, Escanaba using rescue swimmers. Escanaba herself exploded and sank off Ivigtut on 13 June 1943 while escorting convoy GS-24; only two of the roughly 103-105 aboard survived, and the cause, torpedo or drifting mine, remains uncertain. On 22 October 1943 U-537 landed an automatic German weather station, Weather Station Kurt, at Martin Bay near Cape Chidley, Labrador's northern tip; it was rediscovered in 1977.",sources:["https://www.uboat.net/allies/merchants/2616.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USCGC_Escanaba_(WPG-77)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenland_in_World_War_II","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weather_Station_Kurt"]},3:{name:"Denmark Strait & Iceland",area:"Waters around Iceland: the Denmark Strait between Greenland's east coast and Iceland, south-east toward the Faroes and north of Scotland.",lat:"56°N to 69°N",lon:"41°W to 5°W",borders:[2,4,8,9,10,11],history:"The Denmark Strait saw one of the war's most famous actions. On 24 May 1941 the battleship Bismarck and heavy cruiser Prinz Eugen, breaking out into the Atlantic, met HMS Hood and HMS Prince of Wales; Hood blew up within minutes and only three of the more than 1,400 men aboard survived. Prince of Wales was damaged and withdrew, but her hits cost Bismarck fuel and forced her to abandon the sortie; she was sunk three days later far to the south. Earlier, on 23 November 1939, the armed merchant cruiser Rawalpindi of the Northern Patrol was sunk north of the Faroes by Scharnhorst and Gneisenau, with 238 men killed. Britain occupied the Faroes in April 1940 (Operation Valentine) and landed troops at Reykjavik on 10 May 1940 (Operation Fork) to deny Iceland to Germany; American forces later relieved most of the garrison. Hvalfjörður became a convoy anchorage: Operation Dervish sailed from there for Russia in August 1941 and PQ 17 on 27 June 1942. In September 1941 the Markgraf wolfpack attacked slow convoy SC 42 off south-east Greenland, near Cape Farewell, sinking 16 merchant ships; the Canadian corvettes Chambly and Moose Jaw sank U-501.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Denmark_Strait","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMS_Rawalpindi","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Invasion_of_Iceland","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoy_SC_42"]},4:{name:"Norwegian Sea (north) / Bear Island approaches",area:"Open Arctic water north of Norway between Jan Mayen and the North Cape; the convoy lane to Murmansk crosses it.",lat:"63°N to 71°N",lon:"3°W to 20°E",borders:[3,5,11,12],history:"These waters, from the Norwegian Sea to the coast of northern Norway, were the setting for major actions in 1940 and later the western leg of the Arctic convoy route to Russia. At Narvik, German destroyers sank the Norwegian coast-defence ships Eidsvold and Norge on 9 April 1940; in the First Battle of Narvik on 10 April British destroyers sank two German destroyers but lost Hardy and Hunter, and on 13 April HMS Warspite and nine destroyers destroyed the remaining eight. During the Allied evacuation, Scharnhorst and Gneisenau caught the carrier HMS Glorious and destroyers Ardent and Acasta in the open Norwegian Sea on 8 June 1940 and sank all three, killing about 1,519 men. On 4 March 1941 Operation Claymore landed commandos in the Lofoten Islands, destroying fish-oil plants and some ten ships and capturing Enigma rotors from the trawler Krebs. Convoy traffic crossed this sea from 1941; U-255 sank the freighter Greylock of returning convoy RA 52 here on 3 February 1943. The battleship Tirpitz ended her career at Tromsø, capsized by RAF Lancasters dropping Tallboy bombs on 12 November 1944 with heavy loss of life.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battles_of_Narvik","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMS_Glorious","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Claymore","https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/ship/2621.html"]},5:{name:"Barents Sea",area:"Off northern Norway (Finnmark) and the Kola Peninsula, including the approaches to Murmansk and Kola Inlet.",lat:"70°N to 73°N",lon:"21°E to 39°E",borders:[4,6],history:"The Barents Sea, the final approach to Murmansk and the Kola Inlet, was the last and most exposed stretch of the Arctic convoy route. On 30 April 1942 U-456 torpedoed the cruiser HMS Edinburgh, escorting returning convoy QP 11 and carrying about 4.5 tons of gold; after a further fight with German destroyers she was scuttled on 2 May with about 60 men lost, and most of the gold was salvaged in 1981. On 31 December 1942, in the Battle of the Barents Sea, Admiral Hipper and Lützow attacked convoy JW 51B bound for the Kola Inlet; the destroyer Achates and minesweeper Bramble were sunk, as was the German destroyer Friedrich Eckoldt, but the convoy was untouched, and Hitler's fury led to Admiral Raeder's resignation. In September 1943 British X-craft midget submarines penetrated Kåfjord in the Altafjord and badly damaged Tirpitz (Operation Source), winning Victoria Crosses for Donald Cameron and Godfrey Place. On 26 December 1943, in the Battle of the North Cape, Scharnhorst sortied against convoy JW 55B and was sunk by HMS Duke of York, cruisers and destroyers under Admiral Fraser; only 36 of her roughly 1,968 crew survived.",sources:["https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/1579.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Barents_Sea","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Source","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_North_Cape"]},6:{name:"White Sea",area:"The White Sea and its mouth (Gorlo), including Arkhangelsk (Archangel), Molotovsk/Severodvinsk and the Kanin Peninsula coast.",lat:"67°N to 68°N",lon:"32°E to 44°E",borders:[5],history:"The White Sea and its port of Arkhangelsk were one of the two Soviet terminals for the Arctic convoys, usable in summer and autumn because ice closed the ports from about late November to late May, when traffic switched to Murmansk. The first Allied convoy to Russia, Operation Dervish, reached Arkhangelsk on 31 August 1941 with six merchant ships and an oiler carrying raw materials and crated Hurricane fighters, escorted through the White Sea from Kanin Nos by Soviet destroyers. After the disaster of PQ 17 in July 1942, nine of its surviving merchant ships eventually reached Arkhangelsk, the last group arriving under corvette escort on 25 July. Convoy PQ 18, which lost 13 ships on passage, reached the anchorage off Arkhangelsk in September 1942; a north-westerly gale against a lee shore, with unlit beacons and missing pilot boats, left five ships aground by dawn on 20 September, and Ju 88 bombers attacked on 20 and 21 September, scoring only near misses before the ships moved upriver and bombs fell on the town instead. The Germans also planned to mine the White Sea entrance with U-boats, destroyers and the minelayer Ulm (Operation Zar).",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Dervish_(1941)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoy_PQ_17","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoy_PQ_18"]},7:{name:"Arctic Ocean (East Siberian/Chukchi/Beaufort)",area:"Long Arctic strip along the top of the map from the East Siberian Sea across the Bering Strait's north side (Chukchi Sea) to the Beaufort Sea off northern Alaska and Canada.",lat:"63°N to 75°N",lon:"164°E to 122°W",borders:[18,19],history:"No naval battles are recorded in this remote Arctic strip, which stretches from the East Siberian Sea through the Chukchi Sea to the Beaufort Sea; the German cruiser Admiral Scheer's raid into the Soviet Arctic in August 1942 (Operation Wunderland) got no farther east than the Vilkitsky Strait, far to the west. It mattered as a route, not a battleground. In summer 1942 the Soviet Union moved warships from the Pacific to the Northern Fleet by the Northern Sea Route: Expedition EON-18, led by the destroyer leader Baku with destroyers Razumny and Razyaryonny, icebreakers, tankers and transports, left Vladivostok on 15 July, rested at Provideniya Bay, met ice from mid-August, and passed Pevek, Ambarchik Bay and the East Siberian Sea before reaching Vaenga in Kola Bay on 14 October. On the Canadian side, the RCMP schooner St. Roch under Henry Larsen completed the first west-to-east transit of the Northwest Passage between 1940 and 1942, partly to demonstrate Canadian sovereignty, and in 1944 made the return voyage to Vancouver in 86 days. Lend-Lease aircraft flown from Alaska to Siberia from late 1942, with Nome as a final stop, crossed the Bering Strait region to the south; some 7,983 were delivered.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soviet_destroyer_Baku","https://www.thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/henry-larsen","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Wunderland","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northwest_Staging_Route"]},8:{name:"Labrador Sea",area:"Off the Labrador coast south of Davis Strait and south-west of Greenland's Cape Farewell; northern edge of the main North Atlantic convoy routes.",lat:"51°N to 59°N",lon:"61°W to 41°W",borders:[2,3,9,21,22],history:"The Labrador Sea lay on the northern edge of the main North Atlantic convoy routes and the sea lanes from Newfoundland to Greenland. In late August 1942 U-517 and U-165 attacked the Greenland supply convoy SG-6 in the Strait of Belle Isle; on 27 August U-517 sank the American transport Chatham, carrying 428 passengers, mostly construction workers bound for Greenland, though only 14 of the 562 aboard died; U-517 also sank the freighter Arlyn. The zone's largest battle was slow convoy SC 107, sighted by the Veilchen wolfpack on 29 October 1942 and attacked from the night of 1 November. On 2 November, near 52°N 45°W, U-522 sank Maritima, Mount Pelion and Parthenon. Over the whole battle, which continued eastward, fifteen freighters were lost, about 150 seamen died, and RCAF aircraft sank U-520 and U-658 east of Newfoundland, while U-132 is believed destroyed by the explosion of an ammunition ship it had torpedoed. The troop transport Dorchester was sunk in February 1943 just north of this zone, west of Cape Farewell. Conditions here are harsh, with gales, fog and drifting ice, and in winter the Labrador Current carries icebergs south toward the convoy lanes.",sources:["https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/2093.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoys_SG-6/LN-6","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoy_SC_107","https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/2350.html"]},9:{name:"Central North Atlantic (northern)",area:"Open ocean roughly midway between Newfoundland and Ireland, south of Greenland/Iceland - the heart of the wartime 'air gap'.",lat:"49°N to 56°N",lon:"40°W to 25°W",borders:[3,8,10,22,23],history:"This stretch of open ocean, beyond the reach of most land-based aircraft from Newfoundland, Iceland and Britain until 1943, formed the heart of the mid-Atlantic 'air gap' where U-boat wolfpacks fought their greatest convoy battles. On 5 November 1940 the armed merchant cruiser HMS Jervis Bay, sole escort of convoy HX 84, steamed straight at the pocket battleship Admiral Scheer about 755 nautical miles south-south-west of Reykjavik; she was sunk with most of her crew, but her sacrifice let the convoy scatter and only five merchant ships were lost. Captain Edward Fegen received a posthumous Victoria Cross, and the badly damaged tanker San Demetrio survived. On 16-19 March 1943 nearly 40 U-boats of the Raubgraf, Stürmer and Dränger groups attacked convoys HX 229 and SC 122 in the largest convoy battle of the war, sinking 22 merchant ships for the loss of one U-boat; Kingsbury and Fort Cedar Lake were among those sunk near 52 degrees North, 32 degrees West on 17 March. Earlier, on 4 November 1942, the SC 107 straggler Hatimura was sunk on the zone's western edge.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMS_Jervis_Bay","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoys_HX_229/SC_122","https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/2786.html","https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/2373.html"]},10:{name:"Western Approaches & Ireland",area:"Waters west and south of Ireland, the North Channel/Irish Sea and the approaches to the Clyde and Liverpool.",lat:"50°N to 56°N",lon:"23°W to 2°W",borders:[3,9,23,24],history:"The Western Approaches were the gateway through which almost all Atlantic convoys reached Britain, and the scene of the war's first sinkings. On 3 September 1939, hours after Britain declared war, U-30 torpedoed the liner Athenia about 250 miles west of Inishtrahull, killing more than 110. On 17 September U-29 sank the aircraft carrier HMS Courageous about 350 miles west of Land's End with the loss of 518 men. On 2 July 1940 U-47 torpedoed the Arandora Star, carrying Italian and German internees and prisoners to Canada, 75 miles west of Bloody Foreland; 805 died. After the fall of France, convoys were routed north of Ireland, and Western Approaches Command moved from Plymouth to Derby House, Liverpool, opening there on 7 February 1941, with escort groups based at Liverpool, Greenock on the Clyde and Londonderry. Admiral Sir Percy Noble and, from November 1942, Admiral Sir Max Horton directed the convoy war from there. Liverpool's docks were heavily bombed in the May Blitz of 1941; on 4 May the munitions ship Malakand caught fire and exploded in Huskisson Dock, destroying the No. 2 dock.",sources:["https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/ship/1.html","https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/32.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Arandora_Star","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Approaches_Command"]},11:{name:"North Sea (north) & Scapa Flow",area:"Northern North Sea from the Orkneys and Shetlands east toward Norway, including Scapa Flow and the east coast of Scotland.",lat:"54°N to 62°N",lon:"1°W to 7°E",borders:[3,4,12,13,25],history:"Scapa Flow in Orkney was the Home Fleet's main base, and its most famous wartime event was a disaster: in the early hours of 14 October 1939 Günther Prien's U-47 slipped in through Kirk Sound and torpedoed the battleship HMS Royal Oak at anchor; 835 men and boys died. Churchill ordered the eastern entrances sealed, and the Churchill Barriers, largely built by Italian prisoners of war, were essentially complete by September 1944. On 10 April 1940 sixteen Skua dive bombers flew from RNAS Hatston in Orkney to sink the German cruiser Königsberg at Bergen. Earlier, on 16-17 February 1940, the destroyer HMS Cossack under Captain Philip Vian boarded the German supply ship Altmark in Jøssingfjord, on Norway's south-west coast, freeing 299 British prisoners. From 1941 to 1945 the Norwegian 'Shetland Bus', based at Lunna and later Scalloway, made 198 trips to occupied Norway, landing 192 agents and some 380 tons of weapons and supplies and bringing out 373 refugees, at a cost of 44 men.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMS_Royal_Oak_(08)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shetland_bus","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Altmark_incident","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_cruiser_K%C3%B6nigsberg"]},12:{name:"Norwegian coast",area:"Off the west coast of southern/central Norway (Bergen to Trondheim area) and the outer Skagerrak.",lat:"59°N to 65°N",lon:"7°E to 15°E",borders:[4,11,13],history:"The coast from Bergen to Trondheim was a key objective of the German invasion of Norway on 9 April 1940. On 8 April 1940 the destroyer HMS Glowworm met the cruiser Admiral Hipper, part of the force carrying troops to Trondheim, about 70 miles north-west of Frøya; she rammed Hipper before sinking with heavy loss, and her captain, Gerard Roope, received a posthumous Victoria Cross for the war's first VC action, though it was gazetted only in 1945. On 10 April Fleet Air Arm Skuas flying from Orkney sank the German cruiser Königsberg at Bergen. On 27 December 1941 Operation Archery, a commando raid on Vågsøy and Måløy supported by the cruiser HMS Kenya, sank about ten ships and destroyed fish-oil plants and stores; it helped persuade Hitler to reinforce Norway with some 30,000 more troops. By 1942 the battleship Tirpitz was based near Trondheim, threatening the Arctic convoys. In late October 1942 Operation Title sent Leif Larsen's boat Arthur, carrying two Chariot human torpedoes, into Trondheimsfjord to attack her at Fættenfjord; both Chariots broke loose in bad weather and most of the crew escaped to Sweden. Shetland Bus boats regularly landed agents and arms along this coast.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMS_Glowworm_(H92)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_cruiser_K%C3%B6nigsberg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Archery","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Title"]},13:{name:"Skagerrak, Kattegat & German Bight",area:"Around Denmark: the Skagerrak, Kattegat and Danish straits plus the German Bight off Heligoland, Wilhelmshaven and the Kiel Canal mouth.",lat:"53°N to 59°N",lon:"6°E to 14°E",borders:[11,12,15,25],history:"The German Bight saw one of the war's first air-sea battles on 18 December 1939, when 22 RAF Wellington bombers hunting warships off Wilhelmshaven were intercepted by German fighters and 12 were shot down. The Skagerrak became a front line in Operation Weserübung: on 8 April 1940 the Polish submarine Orzeł sank the German troopship Rio de Janeiro off Lillesand, an early sign of the coming invasion, and on 9 April HMS Truant torpedoed the cruiser Karlsruhe outside Kristiansand; the torpedo boat Greif took off her crew and scuttled her. In January 1941 Operation Rubble saw five Norwegian merchant ships break out of Gothenburg through the German blockade, carrying about 18,600 tonnes of Swedish ball bearings, machine tools and steel to Kirkwall. On 29 August 1943 the Danish navy scuttled 32 of its 52 vessels rather than let them be seized, while a few escaped to Sweden. Throughout the war Wilhelmshaven and the Kiel Canal mouth were home waters for the Kriegsmarine: after the Channel Dash of February 1942 Scharnhorst reached Wilhelmshaven, while Gneisenau and Prinz Eugen tied up at Brunsbüttel.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Heligoland_Bight_(1939)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_cruiser_Karlsruhe","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Rubble","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Safari"]},14:{name:"Gulf of Bothnia",area:"Between Sweden and Finland north of the Åland Islands.",lat:"60°N to 67°N",lon:"20°E to 26°E",borders:[15,16],history:"The Gulf of Bothnia lay away from the main naval fronts, but Swedish and Finnish coastal shipping using it was periodically hunted by Soviet submarines. In the Winter War ShCh-311 sank the Finnish steamer Wilpas off Vaasa on 29 December 1939 and, on 5 January 1940, the Swedish steamer Fenris by gunfire near the Sydostbrotten lightship, between Örnsköldsvik and Holmsund; the icebreaker Atle rescued her crew. Winter ice soon ended the campaign. In September 1942 the Soviet submarine S-13, later notorious for sinking the Wilhelm Gustloff, raided the gulf, sinking the Finnish steamers Hera on 11 September and Jussi H. in the Södra Kvarken the next day, and the small Dutch vessel Anna W. by gunfire off Kaskinen on 18 September. The gulf's most significant operation came at its northern tip in the Lapland War. On 1 October 1944 Finnish troops carried from Oulu in three lightly armed cargo ships landed unopposed at Röyttä, the outer port of Tornio, to turn on their former German allies. German Stukas sank the transports Bore IX and Maininki at the pier on 4 October, and the gunboats Hämeenmaa and Uusimaa arrived on 6 October to provide anti-aircraft fire.",sources:["https://www.uboat.net/allies/warships/ship/5058.html","https://uboat.net/allies/warships/ship/5098.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Tornio","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_shipwrecks_in_January_1940"]},15:{name:"Baltic Sea (south & central)",area:"The southern Baltic from the Danish islands to East Prussia: Kiel Bay, Rügen, Bornholm, Danzig/Gdynia, Gotland and the Swedish east coast.",lat:"55°N to 59°N",lon:"12°E to 23°E",borders:[13,14,16],history:"Some of the first shots of the war in Europe were fired here: shortly before 05:00 on 1 September 1939 the German battleship Schleswig-Holstein opened fire on the Polish depot at Westerplatte in Danzig harbour, where about 180 defenders held out for seven days. In 1942 Soviet submarines broke into these waters to attack the Swedish ore trade, S-7 sinking the Swedish steamers Margareta and Luleå off Sweden's east coast in July. The sea's darkest chapter came in 1945. Operation Hannibal (23 January to 8 May) carried some 800,000 to 900,000 civilians and 350,000 soldiers west from Pillau, Gotenhafen (Gdynia), Danzig and Hela. On 30 January the Soviet submarine S-13 torpedoed the liner Wilhelm Gustloff off the Stolpe Bank with more than 10,000 aboard, killing up to 9,500, the largest loss of life in a single ship sinking; on 10 February the same boat sank the General von Steuben. On 16 April the submarine L-3 sank the Goya north of Cape Rozewie, with over 6,000 dead and fewer than 200 survivors. On 3 May 1945 RAF Typhoons attacked ships anchored in the Bay of Lübeck, sinking the Cap Arcona and Thielbek, which were crowded with concentration-camp prisoners; over 7,000 died.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Westerplatte","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Hannibal","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MV_Goya","https://www.kz-gedenkstaette-neuengamme.de/en/history/satellite-camps/satellite-camps/bay-of-luebeck-neustadt-in-holstein-sinking-of-prisoner-ships/"]},16:{name:"Gulf of Finland & Gulf of Riga",area:"Eastern Baltic: the Gulf of Riga, Estonian coast and the Gulf of Finland up to Leningrad/Kronstadt.",lat:"56°N to 61°N",lon:"22°E to 29°E",borders:[14,15],history:"For much of the war the Soviet Baltic Fleet was penned into the Gulf of Finland. In September 1939 the Polish submarine Orzeł, interned at Tallinn, slipped out on the night of 18 September and eventually reached Britain. In the Winter War, Finnish coastal guns at Russarö off Hanko engaged the cruiser Kirov on 1 December 1939, damaging her with near misses and forcing her to withdraw with 17 dead. After Germany invaded the Soviet Union, the evacuation of Tallinn on 27 to 31 August 1941 became a catastrophe: of some 225 ships and vessels, about 60 were sunk, many in the German and Finnish Juminda minefield, with commonly cited deaths of about 15,000. Leningrad and Kronstadt were then besieged, and on 23 September 1941 a Stuka flown by Hans-Ulrich Rudel blew the bow off the battleship Marat at Kronstadt. In 1942 Soviet submarines staged through the island of Lavansaari to break out into the Baltic, but in 1943 a double anti-submarine net from Porkkala to Naissaar, backed by thousands of newly laid German and Finnish mines, sealed the gulf. ShCh-323, ShCh-406, ShCh-408, S-9 and S-12 were lost, and no Soviet submarine sank a ship in the Baltic that year.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naval_warfare_in_the_Winter_War","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soviet_evacuation_of_Tallinn","https://www.historynet.com/hans-ulrich-rudel-eagle-eastern-front.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soviet_Baltic_Sea_submarine_campaign_in_1943"]},17:{name:"Sea of Okhotsk",area:"Enclosed by Kamchatka, the Siberian coast, Sakhalin and the Kuril chain.",lat:"46°N to 60°N",lon:"137°E to 157°E",borders:[38],history:"The Sea of Okhotsk was a relative backwater, because the Soviet Union and Japan stayed at peace until August 1945. Soviet merchant ships on the Pacific Lend-Lease route generally passed Japanese waters unmolested, but some fell victim to American submarines; the Soviet freighter Ob was sunk in the Sea of Okhotsk by USS Sunfish on 6 July 1944. US submarines patrolled the Kuril chain that walls the sea's eastern side. USS Herring, after sinking the escort Ishigaki from a convoy, was reportedly hit by a shore battery and sunk off Matsuwa Island on 1 June 1944. From June 1945 USS Barb worked along the Okhotsk coasts, firing the first rockets used by a US submarine at towns on Karafuto (southern Sakhalin) and Hokkaido, shelling the garrison on Kaihyo (Tyuleny) Island on 2 July, and landing a party on Karafuto that wrecked a train on 22 to 23 July. Aleutian-based US aircraft also raided Shumshu, at the chain's northern end, from 1943. When the Soviet Union entered the war, troops from Kamchatka landed on Shumshu on 18 August 1945; its garrison surrendered on 23 August, and the remaining Kurils were occupied by early September.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacific_Route","https://www.history.navy.mil/research/library/online-reading-room/title-list-alphabetically/u/united-states-submarine-losses/herring-ss-233.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Barb_(SS-220)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shumshu"]},18:{name:"Bering Sea (west)",area:"Western Bering Sea east of Kamchatka and north of the western Aleutians (Attu, Kiska), including the Commander Islands.",lat:"52°N to 61°N",lon:"160°E to 168°W",borders:[7,19,26,27],history:"Japan occupied Attu and Kiska in June 1942. In March 1943 the Battle of the Komandorski Islands, fought in the open Pacific to the south (zone 27), turned back a Japanese reinforcement convoy and ended surface resupply of the two garrisons. US troops landed on Attu at Massacre Bay and on the north coast in May 1943; the fighting ended with a final Japanese charge on the night of 29 May that annihilated the remaining garrison, while about 550 Americans died. Japanese submarines suffered heavily: I-31 was sunk off Chichagof Harbor on Attu in May 1943, and in June I-24 went down north-east of Shemya and I-9 and I-7 off Kiska. More than 5,000 troops were then evacuated from Kiska under cover of fog, so the Allied landing of 15 August met no enemy, yet mines, booby traps and friendly fire caused casualties, and the destroyer Abner Read lost her stern to a mine, with 71 killed.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Komandorski_Islands","https://home.nps.gov/articles/attu-battlefield-and-us-army-and-navy-airfields-on-attu.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Cottage","https://alaskashipwreck.com/?p=1270"]},19:{name:"Bering Sea (east), eastern Aleutians & Kodiak",area:"Eastern Bering Sea, the Alaska Peninsula, Kodiak Island and the eastern Aleutians around Dutch Harbor/Unalaska and Unimak.",lat:"51°N to 61°N",lon:"166°W to 151°W",borders:[7,18,20,27,28],history:"The eastern Aleutians and Kodiak were drawn in by the northern diversion linked to the Battle of Midway. Kodiak was then the principal advanced US naval base in Alaska and the North Pacific, its flying boats patrolling the Gulf of Alaska and the Bering Sea, and on 21 May 1942 a floatplane from the Japanese submarine I-25 scouted its military installations. On 3 and 4 June aircraft from the carriers Ryūjō and Jun'yō under Rear Admiral Kakuji Kakuta bombed Dutch Harbor on Unalaska, hitting barracks at Fort Mears and the beached transport Northwestern, used to house civilian workers; 43 Americans were killed. A Zero damaged by ground fire crash-landed on Akutan Island, and the recovered and repaired fighter gave the United States valuable intelligence on the type. On 26 April 1944 the destroyer escort USS Gilmore sank the Japanese submarine I-180 south-west of Chirikof Island, south-west of Kodiak, with all hands; days earlier she had almost certainly sunk the Soviet Lend-Lease freighter Pavlin Vinogradov somewhere in the Gulf of Alaska. In 1945 Project Hula at Cold Bay on the Alaska Peninsula trained about 12,000 Soviet personnel and transferred 149 American ships to the Soviet Navy.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Dutch_Harbor","https://www.nps.gov/places/kodiak-naval-operating-base-and-forts-greely-and-abercrombie.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_submarine_I-180","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_Hula"]},20:{name:"Gulf of Alaska",area:"Eastern Gulf of Alaska east of Kodiak: Prince William Sound, Yakutat, the Alaska Panhandle (Sitka, Ketchikan) and the Queen Charlotte Islands.",lat:"50°N to 59°N",lon:"150°W to 128°W",borders:[19,28,29],history:"No major naval battles were fought in the eastern Gulf of Alaska, but its coasts carried the supply and air routes to the Aleutian front. Sitka was the US Navy's first air station in Alaska when the war began. The Army built landing fields at Yakutat, Cordova and on Annette Island near Ketchikan to refuel transport aircraft flying between Washington State and Elmendorf. In April 1942 the Royal Canadian Air Force's No. 115 Squadron, described as the first Canadians based on US territory to help defend it, moved its Bolingbrokes to Annette Island to guard the approaches to Prince Rupert, joined in June by No. 118 Squadron's Kittyhawks. On 7 July 1942 a 115 Squadron crew bombed a suspected submarine about 145 miles north-west of the base, and the cutter McLean and patrol vessel YP-251 followed up with depth charges, but a later claim that this sank the Japanese RO-32 is discredited, as no Japanese submarine was then in the area. In Prince William Sound the Army drove two tunnels to link the new port of Whittier to the Alaska Railroad, completed in April 1943 as a shorter supply route less exposed to submarines and bombing.",sources:["https://usnhistory.navylive.dodlive.mil/Recent/Article-View/Article/2686965/the-naval-history-of-alaska","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alaska_World_War_II_Army_airfields","https://legionmagazine.com/the-japanese-attack-air-force-part-31/","https://dot.state.ak.us/creg/whittiertunnel/history.shtml"]},21:{name:"Gulf of St Lawrence & Newfoundland",area:"Gulf of St Lawrence, Newfoundland, Nova Scotia (Halifax), Cape Race and the Grand Banks.",lat:"42°N to 51°N",lon:"64°W to 42°W",borders:[8,22,30,31],history:"The Battle of the St Lawrence began on 11 May 1942, when U-553 sank SS Nicoya off Gaspé; in all 26 ships were lost, and on 9 September 1942 Ottawa closed the St Lawrence to overseas shipping. HMCS Raccoon was sunk with all 38 of her crew by U-165 on 6 September 1942, and the corvette HMCS Charlottetown by U-517 off Cap Chat on 11 September. On 14 October 1942 U-69 torpedoed the ferry Caribou on the North Sydney to Port aux Basques run; 136 of the 237 aboard died, including ten children. In Newfoundland's Conception Bay, U-513 sank the ore carriers Saganaga and Lord Strathcona at Bell Island on 5 September 1942, and on 2 November U-518 sank Rose Castle and the Free French PLM 27 there, one torpedo exploding against the loading pier. HMCS Shawinigan was sunk with all 91 aboard by U-1228 off Port aux Basques on 24 November 1944, and HMCS Esquimalt was torpedoed off Chebucto Head, Halifax, by U-190 on 16 April 1945, with 44 to 52 lives lost. On 9 to 12 August 1941, Churchill and Roosevelt met aboard HMS Prince of Wales and USS Augusta in Placentia Bay, producing the Atlantic Charter.",sources:["https://www.thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/battle-of-the-st-lawrence","https://www.heritage.nf.ca/articles/politics/caribou-sinking.php","https://www.heritage.nf.ca/articles/politics/bell-island-sinkings.php","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMCS_Esquimalt"]},22:{name:"Central North Atlantic (south of Greenland air gap)",area:"Open mid-ocean water south-east of the Grand Banks, mid-way across the convoy routes.",lat:"41°N to 49°N",lon:"40°W to 28°W",borders:[8,9,21,23,31,32],history:"This stretch of open ocean lay in the mid-Atlantic air gap, beyond the reach of most land-based aircraft for much of the war, and it saw heavy convoy fighting. Westbound convoy ON 166 was attacked by U-boats from 21 to 25 February 1943, and ships were torpedoed here at positions running from about 48°N 30°W south-west to 45°N 39°W, among them Empire Trader, N. T. Nielsen Alonso, Expositor, Winkler, Eulima and Ingria. Fourteen merchant ships were lost from the convoy in all. Its escorts accounted for U-606, which was depth-charged by the Polish destroyer Burza, sent to reinforce the escort, and sank after colliding with the US Coast Guard cutter Campbell. By the second half of 1943 American escort carriers were closing the gap. Aircraft from USS Card sank U-525 at 41°29'N 38°55'W on 11 August 1943, killing all 54 crew, then U-460 and U-422 at about 43°N 29°W on 4 October and U-402 at 48°56'N 29°41'W on 13 October. Away from these actions, the area was crossed by the main transatlantic convoy routes and its winter gales were a hazard in their own right.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoy_ON_166","https://www.warsailors.com/convoys/on166.html","https://www.history.navy.mil/research/histories/ship-histories/danfs/c/card.html","https://uboat.net/boats/u525.htm"]},23:{name:"Eastern North Atlantic",area:"Open ocean west of the Bay of Biscay and south-west of Ireland, north of the Azores.",lat:"42°N to 49°N",lon:"26°W to 13°W",borders:[9,10,22,24,32,33],history:"These waters saw Bismarck's end. Heading for Brest after sinking HMS Hood, she was crippled on the evening of 26 May 1941 when a torpedo from one of Ark Royal's Swordfish jammed her rudder. On the morning of 27 May the battleships King George V and Rodney battered her into a wreck about 300 nautical miles west of Brest, near 48°10'N 16°12'W; torpedoes from the cruiser Dorsetshire preceded her sinking, and only about 114 of more than 2,200 crew survived. In December 1941 the Gibraltar to Britain convoy HG 76 fought through these and more southerly waters; its escort, led by Commander Frederic 'Johnnie' Walker, sank five U-boats, but on 21 December U-751 sank the escort carrier Audacity at 43°45'N 19°54'W, about 500 miles west of Cape Finisterre. A year later the westbound convoy ON 154, routed south towards the Azores, was attacked from 26 to 30 December 1942, losing ships around 47°N 25°W and 43°N 27°W; 13 merchantmen and the special service ship Fidelity were sunk. On 28 December 1943, near 45°N 12°W at the edge of the Bay of Biscay, the cruisers Glasgow and Enterprise sank the German destroyer Z27 and torpedo boats T25 and T26.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Last_battle_of_Bismarck","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoy_HG_76","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoy_ON_154","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Bay_of_Biscay"]},24:{name:"Celtic Sea & Channel approaches",area:"Celtic Sea and the western entrance to the English Channel: Cornwall, the Scilly Isles, Brest and Ushant.",lat:"47°N to 51°N",lon:"12°W to 4°W",borders:[10,23,25,33,34],history:"Brest, at the western tip of Brittany, gave these waters their wartime importance. During Operation Aerial about 32,500 Allied personnel, most of them British, were evacuated from Brest on 16 and 17 June 1940. From August 1940 the port served U-boats, becoming home to the 1st and 9th U-boat Flotillas in 1941, with massive concrete pens begun that January. Scharnhorst and Gneisenau arrived after Operation Berlin on 22 March 1941 and drew more than a thousand RAF bombing sorties; on 6 April 1941 Kenneth Campbell's Beaufort torpedoed Gneisenau in the harbour before being shot down with all aboard. The heavy ships, joined by Prinz Eugen after the Bismarck sortie, finally left on the evening of 11 February 1942 for the Channel Dash. After D-Day, early on 9 June 1944, the 10th Destroyer Flotilla, with British, Canadian and Polish ships including Tartar, Ashanti, Haida, Huron and Błyskawica, intercepted German destroyers off Brittany in the Battle of Ushant, sinking ZH1 and driving Z32 ashore on Île de Batz. Brest's last U-boat, U-256, sailed on 4 September 1944, and the shattered port fell to American forces later that month after a month-long battle.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Aerial","https://www.uboat.net/flotillas/bases/brest.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Channel_Dash","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Ushant_(1944)"]},25:{name:"English Channel & southern North Sea",area:"The English Channel and Strait of Dover with the southern North Sea off East Anglia, the Netherlands and Belgium.",lat:"49°N to 54°N",lon:"5°W to 4°E",borders:[11,13,24,34],history:"The Channel and southern North Sea saw some of the war's most famous sea operations. During Operation Dynamo, from 26 May to 4 June 1940, more than 338,000 British and French soldiers were lifted from Dunkirk by over 800 vessels, including more than 300 small craft gathered after an Admiralty appeal. On 12 February 1942 Scharnhorst, Gneisenau and Prinz Eugen dashed from Brest through the Strait of Dover in the Channel Dash; all six Swordfish of Lieutenant-Commander Eugene Esmonde's torpedo attack were shot down, though both battleships were damaged by mines near the Scheldt and off the Dutch island of Terschelling. During the Dieppe Raid on 19 August 1942 the escort destroyer HMS Berkeley was wrecked by bombs while covering the withdrawal and was scuttled. Rehearsing for D-Day, Exercise Tiger in Lyme Bay was attacked on the night of 27 to 28 April 1944 by nine German E-boats from Cherbourg, which sank LST-507 and LST-531 with the loss of several hundred American servicemen. On 6 June 1944 Operation Neptune landed about 150,000 troops on five Normandy beaches from a fleet of nearly 7,000 vessels; over 200 Allied ships were lost before the operation ended on 30 June.",sources:["https://www.iwm.org.uk/history/what-you-need-to-know-about-the-dunkirk-evacuations","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Channel_Dash","https://history.navy.mil/about-us/leadership/director/directors-corner/h-grams/h-gram-029/h-029-1.html","https://admin.cwgc.org/our-work/blog/legacy-of-liberation-the-royal-navy-d-day/"]},26:{name:"North Pacific off southern Kamchatka",area:"Open North Pacific south of the Commander Islands and east of southern Kamchatka.",lat:"49°N to 55°N",lon:"159°E to 165°E",borders:[18,27,39,40],history:"Little naval fighting is recorded in these waters south of the Commander Islands and off the east coast of southern Kamchatka, which lay on the quiet flank of the Aleutian and Kuril campaigns; the Battle of the Komandorski Islands in March 1943 was fought just to the east, in neighbouring waters. From 1943 American bombers based on Attu and Shemya crossed this zone on round trips of some 1,700 miles to raid Paramushiro and the northern Kurils. Damaged or fuel-short aircraft sometimes came down on Soviet Kamchatka, where the neutral Soviets detained their crews; some were later moved across Asia and allowed to 'escape' near the Iranian border. Soviet-flagged Lend-Lease freighters on the Great Circle route from America skirted the Kurils, and in summer some partly unloaded at Petropavlovsk-Kamchatsky so they could use the shallow northern passage through the Amur estuary. On 26 September 1942 the Soviet submarines L-15 and L-16 sailed from Petropavlovsk on a long transfer voyage to the Northern Fleet. On 17 August 1945 the Soviet assault force for Shumshu sailed from Petropavlovsk and ran south along the Kamchatka coast to land the next morning. Fog and gales were constant hazards.",sources:["https://www.adn.com/alaska-life/2016/11/05/the-war-after-attu-anchorage-historian-writes-the-first-history-of-air-battle-launched-from-alaska","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacific_Route","https://uboat.net/allies/warships/ship/4885.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Shumshu"]},27:{name:"North Pacific south of the western Aleutians",area:"Open ocean south of the western Aleutians (around the 180th meridian).",lat:"46°N to 51°N",lon:"166°E to 164°W",borders:[18,19,26,28,40,41],history:"The main event in these waters was the Battle of the Komandorski Islands, fought on 26 March 1943 by American dating (27 March locally) about 100 nautical miles south of the Commander Islands and 180 nautical miles west of Attu. Rear Admiral Charles McMorris, with the heavy cruiser Salt Lake City, the light cruiser Richmond and four destroyers, intercepted Vice Admiral Boshirō Hosogaya's heavy cruisers Nachi and Maya, light cruisers Tama and Abukuma and four destroyers, which were escorting transports bound for Attu. In a gun battle lasting about three and a half hours Salt Lake City was hit repeatedly and lay stopped for a time, but Hosogaya, unaware of how badly he had hurt her and fearing American air attack, withdrew to Paramushiro. Losses were light, seven Americans and fourteen Japanese killed, yet Japan never again tried to supply its Aleutian garrisons by surface ship, leaving the task to submarines, and Hosogaya was retired. In waters just to the north, on 27 July 1943, the battleships Mississippi and Idaho fired 518 fourteen-inch shells at phantom radar contacts west of Kiska, and two days later Rear Admiral Kimura Masatomi's ships slipped through fog to evacuate 5,193 men from Kiska.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Komandorski_Islands","http://www.combinedfleet.com/abukuma_t.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Pips","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_occupation_of_Kiska"]},28:{name:"North Pacific south of the eastern Aleutians",area:"Open ocean south of the Alaska Peninsula/eastern Aleutians.",lat:"45°N to 50°N",lon:"162°W to 145°W",borders:[19,20,27,29,41,42],history:"No significant naval actions are recorded in this stretch of open North Pacific south of the Alaska Peninsula and the eastern Aleutians; the fighting of the Aleutian campaign took place farther north and west. Aircraft from the Japanese carriers Ryūjō and Jun'yō raided Dutch Harbor on Unalaska, to the north-west of this zone, on 3-4 June 1942. Soviet-flagged Lend-Lease freighters bound from American ports for Vladivostok followed the Great Circle route, skirting the Aleutian chain to the north. Japanese submarines did operate in the Gulf of Alaska: on 22 April 1944 I-180 almost certainly sank the Soviet freighter Pavlin Vinogradov, and on 26 April she was herself sunk by the destroyer escort USS Gilmore south-west of Chirikof Island, both well north of this zone. In the winter of 1944-45 some of the roughly 9,300 Fu-Go balloon bombs launched from Japan crossed the North Pacific at high altitude on the jet stream toward North America. This is a stormy region of frequent gales, fog and heavy swell, which made it a hard passage for any ship.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aleutian_Islands_campaign","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_submarine_I-180","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacific_Route","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fire_balloon"]},29:{name:"NE Pacific off British Columbia & Washington",area:"Off Vancouver Island, Washington and northern Oregon; approaches to the Strait of Juan de Fuca, Seattle and Vancouver.",lat:"41°N to 49°N",lon:"144°W to 123°W",borders:[20,28,42,64],history:"These waters off Vancouver Island, Washington and Oregon saw some of the few Japanese attacks on North America's Pacific coast. On 7 June 1942 the submarine I-26 torpedoed the freighter Coast Trader about 35 nautical miles south-west of Cape Flattery; one man died of exposure. Around midnight on 19-20 June I-25 torpedoed the Canadian freighter Fort Camosun some 70 miles south-south-west of Cape Flattery, though her timber and plywood cargo kept her afloat, and on the night of 20 June I-26 shelled the radio station at Estevan Point on Vancouver Island, the first attack on Canadian soil since the Fenian raids of 1870. The next evening I-25 fired 17 shells at Fort Stevens at the mouth of the Columbia River, causing little damage. Returning in the autumn, I-25 launched floatplane incendiary raids on southern Oregon forests in September, torpedoed the tanker Camden on 4 October and sank the tanker Larry Doheny off southern Oregon on 5 October. On 11 October, about 500 miles west of Seattle, she sank the Soviet submarine L-16, bound from Petropavlovsk for the Northern Fleet via the Panama Canal, with all 50 aboard.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_submarine_I-26","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_submarine_I-25","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Fort_Camosun","https://uboat.net/allies/warships/ship/4885.html"]},30:{name:"Off New England & Nova Scotia",area:"Gulf of Maine and the waters off Boston, Cape Cod and south of Nova Scotia; the US-Canadian convoy assembly area.",lat:"38°N to 45°N",lon:"72°W to 55°W",borders:[21,31,43,44],history:"These waters were the western anchor of the North Atlantic convoy system. Halifax, Nova Scotia, launched the HX convoys, beginning with HX 1 on 16 September 1939 escorted by the Canadian destroyers St. Laurent and Saguenay, until the series moved to New York after the United States entered the war; slow SC convoys sailed from Sydney, Nova Scotia. U-boats struck here repeatedly. On 12 January 1942 U-123 sank the British steamer Cyclops about 125 miles south-east of Cape Sable, the first victim of Operation Paukenschlag (Drumbeat), with 88 dead. On 29 November 1944 U-1230 landed the spies William Colepaugh and Erich Gimpel at Hancock Point, Maine; both were caught within weeks. In the war's final weeks U-190 torpedoed HMCS Esquimalt off Chebucto Head near Halifax on 16 April 1945, and most of her dead succumbed to exposure in the water, while U-853 sank USS Eagle 56 off Cape Elizabeth, Maine, on 23 April, killing 49. On 5 May U-853 sank the collier Black Point off Point Judith, Rhode Island, the last US-flagged merchant ship lost in the war, and the next day American warships and blimps destroyed U-853 with all 55 hands.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HX_convoys","https://www.uboat.net/allies/merchants/1243.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMCS_Esquimalt","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Point_Judith"]},31:{name:"Western North Atlantic (south of the Grand Banks)",area:"Open ocean between the Grand Banks and the Azores.",lat:"37°N to 42°N",lon:"54°W to 36°W",borders:[21,22,30,32,44,45],history:"This open stretch of ocean between the Grand Banks and the Azores saw one of the most destructive surface-raider sorties of the war. During Operation Berlin, the German battleships Scharnhorst and Gneisenau fell upon ships of recently dispersed westbound convoys, chiefly OB 294, on 15-16 March 1941, sinking or capturing sixteen vessels in two days. Many of the sinkings, including the tankers British Strength, Simnia and Athelfoam and the freighters Royal Crown, Myson and Rio Dorado, were recorded near 40–42°N, 43°W, inside this zone, and three tankers were seized as prizes. The battleship Rodney, escorting convoy HX 114, raced to the scene and sighted Gneisenau on 16 March, but the faster German ship escaped. In August 1943 aircraft from the escort carrier USS Card, hunting the U-boat refuelling areas west of the Azores, sank U-117 while she was refuelling U-66 on 7 August, U-664 on 9 August, the day after she had fired three torpedoes at Card, and U-525 on 11 August. U-117 and U-525 were lost with all hands. Otherwise the area was a transit zone for merchant ships and for U-boats moving between the Biscay bases and the western Atlantic.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Berlin_(Atlantic)","https://www.naval-history.net/xDKWW2-4103-30MAR02.htm","https://uboat.net/boats/u664.htm","https://uboat.net/boats/u525.htm"]},32:{name:"Azores",area:"The Azores archipelago and surrounding mid-Atlantic waters.",lat:"37°N to 43°N",lon:"35°W to 15°W",borders:[22,23,31,33,45,48],history:"The Azores, neutral Portuguese islands in mid-Atlantic, became a strategic prize in the Battle of the Atlantic because aircraft based there could cover the central ocean and the convoy routes passing through it. In June 1943 Britain invoked its centuries-old alliance with Portugal, and Salazar approved Allied use of ports and airfields on 17 August 1943. A British force of about 3,000 men arrived off Angra do Heroísmo on Terceira on 8 October 1943 and laid a steel-mat runway at Lagens (Lajes) Field; within two weeks anti-submarine aircraft were flying from it, patrolling out to about 500 miles from the islands. On 9 November 1943 a Fortress of No. 220 Squadron RAF sank U-707 east of the Azores with all 51 hands, the first U-boat kill from the new base. American personnel moved into Lajes alongside the British, and the United States later negotiated a second base on Santa Maria, used from 1944. The islands also became a stepping stone for aircraft crossing the Atlantic, the first American B-17 staging through on 9 December 1943. By then the U-boat arm had suffered heavy defeats, and surviving boats had largely withdrawn from the central Atlantic.",sources:["https://skydozer.com/Azores-Crossroads.html","https://novidadesnewsletter.news/2023/10/11/80-years-ago-the-british-opened-the-doors-of-the-azores-to-the-usa","https://uboat.net/boats/u707.htm"]},33:{name:"Bay of Biscay (west) & northern Spain",area:"Western Bay of Biscay and the coast of northern Spain (Cape Ortegal, Ferrol).",lat:"43°N to 47°N",lon:"14°W to 4°W",borders:[23,24,32,34,48],history:"The western Bay of Biscay was the transit lane for U-boats leaving and returning to the French Atlantic bases, and became the scene of an intense Allied air offensive. On 30 July 1943 an RAF Liberator sighted U-461, U-462 and U-504 north-west of Cape Ortegal; an Australian Sunderland of No. 461 Squadron sank U-461, leaving 15 survivors, and the sloops Kite, Woodpecker, Wren and Wild Goose sank U-504 with all hands. On 1 June 1943 Ju 88s of KG 40 shot down BOAC Flight 777, a civilian DC-3, about 200 miles north-west of the Spanish coast near 46°N, 10°W; all 17 aboard, including the actor Leslie Howard, died. On 27 December 1943 the homeward-bound blockade runner Alsterufer, carrying tungsten and rubber, was set ablaze by RAF Liberators, and the next day the cruisers Glasgow and Enterprise fought the German destroyers and torpedo boats sent to escort her, around 45°N, 12°W, sinking Z27, T25 and T26 with several hundred German dead. Just south of the zone, about 30 miles off Vigo, a Hs 293 glide bomb sank the sloop Egret on 27 August 1943, the first Allied warship sunk by a guided missile.",sources:["https://uboat.net/boats/u461.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BOAC_Flight_777","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Bay_of_Biscay","https://uboat.net/allies/warships/ship/3909.html"]},34:{name:"Bay of Biscay (French coast)",area:"The French Atlantic coast: Brittany's south side, Lorient, St-Nazaire, La Pallice/La Rochelle and the Gironde/Bordeaux.",lat:"46°N to 48°N",lon:"5°W to 1°E",borders:[24,25,33],history:"The French Biscay coast was the heart of Germany's Atlantic U-boat war. On 17 June 1940, during Operation Aerial, German bombers sank the overloaded troopship Lancastria off St-Nazaire; at least 3,500 died and some estimates reach 7,000, the largest single-ship loss of life in British maritime history. From July 1940 U-boats operated from Lorient, where Dönitz set up his headquarters at Kernevel and the massive Keroman pens were begun in 1941, and from other Biscay ports, while Bordeaux hosted the Italian BETASOM submarine base from August 1940. On 28 March 1942, in Operation Chariot, the old destroyer HMS Campbeltown rammed the Normandie dock at St-Nazaire, the only Atlantic dock able to take the Tirpitz; her explosives detonated around noon, wrecking it for the rest of the war. The raiders lost 169 killed and 215 captured, and five Victoria Crosses were awarded. In December 1942 Royal Marine canoeists of Operation Frankton, launched from the submarine Tuna, paddled up the Gironde to mine ships at Bordeaux; only Hasler and Sparks survived. Allied bombing flattened Lorient in early 1943, but the pens survived; the U-boats left in August 1944, though the German garrison held out until May 1945.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RMS_Lancastria","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Nazaire_Raid","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Frankton","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lorient_Submarine_Base"]},35:{name:"Black Sea (west)",area:"Western Black Sea: Odessa, western Crimea/Sevastopol, Romania (Constanța), Bulgaria and the Bosporus approaches.",lat:"41°N to 46°N",lon:"29°E to 32°E",borders:[36,52],history:"The western Black Sea saw the Soviet Black Sea Fleet in action from the war's first days. On 26 June 1941 the destroyer leaders Moskva and Kharkov bombarded Constanța, Romania's main port; Moskva struck a mine, probably Romanian, while withdrawing and sank with 268 killed. Besieged Odessa was supplied by sea until the fleet evacuated its garrison to Sevastopol on the night of 14-15 October 1941. Sevastopol was besieged from 30 October 1941 to 4 July 1942. On 7 November 1941 the liner Armenia, carrying wounded and civilians from Sevastopol and Yalta, was torpedoed by a German He 111 about 40 km from Yalta; only eight people were rescued, and an estimated 5,000 to 7,000 died. Fleet supply runs into Sevastopol suffered heavy Luftwaffe losses in June 1942. On 24 February 1942 the refugee ship Struma, towed out from Istanbul, was torpedoed by the Soviet submarine Shch-213 about ten miles north of the Bosporus; some 781 refugees and 10 crew died, with one survivor. When the Red Army retook Crimea in spring 1944, the Axis evacuated over 120,000 men by sea, and on 10 May the transports Totila and Teja were sunk with heavy loss of life.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raid_on_Constanța","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armenia_(ship)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Struma_disaster","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crimean_offensive"]},36:{name:"Black Sea (east)",area:"Eastern Black Sea: the Kerch Strait, Novorossiysk, the Caucasus coast (Tuapse, Poti, Batumi) and northern Turkey's eastern coast.",lat:"41°N to 45°N",lon:"32°E to 41°E",borders:[35],history:"The eastern Black Sea became the Soviet fleet's refuge and the stage for repeated amphibious operations. On 26 December 1941 Soviet troops landed around Kerch, and on 29 December a force sailing from Novorossiysk with two cruisers and eight destroyers put the 44th Army ashore at Feodosia, the cruiser Krasny Kavkaz landing troops straight onto the harbour mole. The bridgehead was crushed by Manstein's Operation Trappenjagd in May 1942, and Kerch fell on 15 May. With Sevastopol lost, the fleet relied on Caucasus ports such as Poti; the destroyer leader Tashkent was bombed and sunk at Novorossiysk on 2 July 1942. On 4 February 1943 Major Tsezar Kunikov's naval infantry seized a foothold at Cape Myskhako near Novorossiysk, the Malaya Zemlya beachhead. In mid-September 1943 Soviet forces broke into Novorossiysk, and the Taman Peninsula was cleared by 9 October as the Germans withdrew across the Kerch Strait. On 6 October 1943 Stukas sank the destroyer leader Kharkov and the destroyers Besposhchadny and Sposobny returning from a Crimean raid, after which Stalin barred the use of destroyers and larger ships without his permission. The Kerch-Eltigen landings of November 1943 won a foothold for the 1944 liberation of Crimea.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Kerch_Peninsula","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malaya_Zemlya","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soviet_destroyer_Kharkov","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kuban_bridgehead"]},37:{name:"Caspian Sea",area:"The Caspian Sea, bordered by the Soviet Caucasus (Baku), Kazakhstan, Turkmenistan and Iran.",lat:"36°N to 47°N",lon:"46°E to 55°E",borders:[],history:"The landlocked Caspian Sea saw no fleet battles, but it was a vital wartime highway. In the Anglo-Soviet invasion of Iran, the Soviet Caspian Flotilla under Rear Admiral Sedelnikov opened the attack on Gilan Province on 25 August 1941, quickly taking the border town of Astara. At Bandar Pahlavi (Anzali), Iranian defenders sank barges in the harbour entrance and brought up guns, preventing a landing; the port fell only when the Soviet 44th Army arrived overland. Three Iranian gunboats faced the flotilla. Afterwards the Caspian became a leg of the Persian Corridor supply route, with Lend-Lease cargo shipped from Iranian ports, chiefly Nowshahr, to Baku and Makhachkala. Oil mattered even more: Baku produced 15.7 million tonnes in 1942, about 72 percent of Soviet output, and tankers and barges carried it north to the Astrakhan roadstead for the Volga. As the German summer offensive of 1942 neared the Volga, the Luftwaffe began raiding the Astrakhan roadstead on the night of 27 June, making about 300 attacks on tanker-fleet vessels before the navigation season closed, and traffic shifted toward Guryev from late summer. The flotilla guarded shipping and troop movements throughout the battles for Stalingrad and the Caucasus.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anglo-Soviet_invasion_of_Iran","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_Corridor","https://kaspy.asu-edu.ru/storage/archive/1(78)/56-63.pdf","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caspian_Flotilla"]},38:{name:"Sea of Japan & Korea Strait",area:"Between Japan and the Asian mainland: the Sea of Japan from Sakhalin/Tatar Strait south to the Korea (Tsushima) Strait and waters west of Kyushu.",lat:"30°N to 52°N",lon:"127°E to 142°E",borders:[17,39,54,55,56,65],history:"The Sea of Japan linked Japan's home islands with Korea and Manchuria, and American submarines repeatedly forced their way in. USS Wahoo entered in August 1943, when faulty torpedoes ruined her patrol, and returned in September; on 5 October she sank the Konron Maru off western Honshū near the Tsushima Strait with 544 lives, before she was lost with all hands to Japanese aircraft and ships in La Pérouse Strait on 11 October 1943. In June 1945 Operation Barney sent nine submarines, using FM sonar to locate mines, through the Tsushima Strait minefields on 4-6 June; between 9 and 20 June they sank 27 merchant ships and the submarine I-122, about 54,786 tons, plus a Soviet freighter by mistake, before leaving through La Pérouse Strait on 24 June. USS Bonefish was lost in Toyama Bay around 18 June. From March 1945 B-29 aerial mining further strangled shipping. In August 1945 the Soviet Pacific Fleet landed in north-east Korea, taking Yuki and Racine on 11-13 August and clearing Seishin (Chongjin) by 15 August. On 24 August the Ukishima Maru, carrying Korean workers home, exploded and sank in Maizuru harbour; Japan counted 549 dead, while other estimates run far higher.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Wahoo_(SS-238)","https://www.history.navy.mil/about-us/leadership/director/directors-corner/h-grams/h-gram-022/h-022-4.html","https://www.usni.org/magazines/proceedings/1995/june/50-years-ago-operation-barney","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seishin_Operation"]},39:{name:"Kuril Islands",area:"The Kuril chain and waters east of Hokkaido toward southern Kamchatka.",lat:"46°N to 53°N",lon:"150°E to 160°E",borders:[26,38,40,56,57],history:"The Kuril chain was Japan's northern bastion. On 26 November 1941 the Pearl Harbor striking force, built around six carriers, sailed from Hitokappu Bay on Etorofu. In the Aleutian campaign Paramushiro served as the forward base; Rear Admiral Kimura's Kiska evacuation force sailed from there and returned on 1 August 1943. After the Aleutians were retaken, US aircraft from Attu and Shemya flew more than 1,500 sorties against the Kurils, and cruisers and destroyers of Task Force 92 bombarded Matsuwa on 21 November 1944 and Paramushiro's Suribachi Wan on 5 January 1945. Submarines hunted here too: S-44 was sunk by the escort Ishigaki on 7 October 1943 while heading for the northern Kurils, and Herring, after sinking Ishigaki and three merchant ships, was lost off Matsuwa on 1 June 1944, apparently to shore batteries. The war's last major battle came on 18 August 1945, when Soviet troops carried from Petropavlovsk stormed Shumshu; five landing ships were destroyed, fighting lasted until 23 August, and the Soviets went on to occupy the whole chain, including Etorofu, Kunashiri and Shikotan, by 5 September.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Shumshu","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Herring_(SS-233)","https://www.history.navy.mil/research/histories/ship-histories/danfs/s/stoddard.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aleutian_Islands_campaign"]},40:{name:"North Pacific (south-east of the Kurils)",area:"Open North Pacific south-east of the Kurils.",lat:"42°N to 48°N",lon:"160°E to 176°E",borders:[26,27,39,41,57,58,61],history:"No naval battles or notable sinkings are recorded in this stretch of open North Pacific south-east of the Kurils, but it lay across one of the war's most consequential routes. After leaving Hitokappu Bay in the Kurils on 26 November 1941, the Pearl Harbor striking force of six carriers and their escorts steamed east through or close to these empty waters toward a standby point that its orders fixed at 42°N, 165°W, refuelling at sea when possible and making every effort to conceal its position, before turning south toward Hawaii for the attack of 7 December. The planned return route ran well to the south, through an assembly point at 30°N, 165°E. Later in the war the area lay between the Japanese bases in the northern Kurils and the American base at Midway, and the direct track from Midway to the northern Kurils crosses it; submarines such as Herring, which left Midway for the Kurils on 21 May 1944, would have passed this way. The weather is dominated by the North Pacific storm track, with frequent gales, heavy seas and fog.",sources:["https://www.ibiblio.org/pha/timeline/411123aijn.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hitokappu_Bay","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Herring_(SS-233)"]},41:{name:"North Pacific (central)",area:"Open ocean around the 180th meridian north of Midway.",lat:"39°N to 45°N",lon:"178°E to 161°W",borders:[27,28,40,42,61,63],history:"No surface battles are recorded in this empty stretch of the North Pacific north of Midway, but one of the war's most famous secret voyages crossed it. Vice-Admiral Chuichi Nagumo's Pearl Harbor striking force of 28 ships left Hitokappu (Tankan) Bay in the Kurils on 26 November 1941 and steamed east at about 14 knots. Its northern route was chosen to pass between the Aleutians and Midway, beyond the range of American air patrols. Planners accepted the risk of heavy weather because few ships used these waters, which made surprise more likely. The stand-by point lay at 42°N: Fuchida's account gives 170°W and the operation order 165°W, both within this zone. Under low cloud and strict radio silence, the fleet first refuelled at sea on 30 November, in seas calmer than feared, and kept its eastward course in secrecy before turning south toward Oahu. The operation order also routed the homeward voyage about 800 nautical miles north of Midway. Apart from that voyage, this was open ocean between the Japanese and American spheres, seldom crossed by merchant shipping.",sources:["https://www.usni.org/magazines/proceedings/1952/september/i-led-air-attack-pearl-harbor","https://www.usni.org/magazines/proceedings/1946/december/climb-mount-niitaka","https://www.ibiblio.org/pha/timeline/411123aijn.html"]},42:{name:"North Pacific (east-central)",area:"Open ocean north-east of Hawaii.",lat:"38°N to 44°N",lon:"159°W to 146°W",borders:[28,29,41,63,64],history:"No major naval actions are recorded in this stretch of open ocean north-east of Hawaii. It lay away from the main wartime routes. Shipping between Puget Sound and Honolulu passed to the south-east, and the Japanese Pearl Harbor striking force in late November and early December 1941 kept to the west, crossing the North Pacific at about 42°N to a stand-by point at 165-170°W before turning south toward Oahu. The nearest notable sinking came on 7 December 1941, when the Japanese submarine I-26 shelled the US Army-chartered lumber freighter Cynthia Olson, bound from Tacoma to Honolulu. She was about 1,200 miles west of Seattle, roughly midway along her route, in waters to the south-east of this zone. No trace of her 33 crew and two Army passengers was ever found. For the rest of the war this cold, empty ocean, between the Aleutians to the north and the Hawaiian chain to the south, saw little more than occasional transits. Its distance from any base, along with the long swells and winter gales of these latitudes, kept it largely outside the fighting.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Cynthia_Olson","https://www.ibiblio.org/pha/timeline/411123aijn.html","https://www.usni.org/magazines/proceedings/1952/september/i-led-air-attack-pearl-harbor"]},43:{name:"US East Coast",area:"The Atlantic coast of the US from New Jersey/Chesapeake past Cape Hatteras to Florida's Atlantic side.",lat:"28°N to 39°N",lon:"81°W to 68°W",borders:[30,44,46,72],history:`The US East Coast saw one of the worst shipping massacres of the war. Germany's Operation Drumbeat (Paukenschlag) brought long-range U-boats to American waters in January 1942. With coastal lights burning and ships sailing alone, sinkings mounted through the "Second Happy Time". The waters off Cape Hatteras became notorious: U-123 sank the freighter Norvana with no survivors on 22 January, and U-552 sank Lancing and the tanker British Splendour there on 7 April. Off the New Jersey coast near Cape May, U-578 sank the destroyer USS Jacob Jones on 28 February 1942, leaving only 11 survivors. USS Roper sank U-85 off the Outer Banks on 14 April, the first U-boat sunk in US waters. The cutter Icarus sank U-352 off North Carolina on 9 May, and a US Army Hudson sank U-701 off Hatteras on 7 July, weeks after its mines had sunk the armed trawler HMT Kingston Ceylonite and the freighter Santore. The first organised US coastal convoy sailed from Hampton Roads on 14 May, and by July most U-boats had shifted south to the Gulf and Caribbean. On 16 June 1942 U-584 landed four Operation Pastorius saboteurs at Ponte Vedra Beach, Florida; all were caught.`,sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Happy_Time","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Jacob_Jones_(DD-130)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_submarine_U-701","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Pastorius"]},44:{name:"Bermuda",area:"Bermuda and surrounding western North Atlantic.",lat:"31°N to 38°N",lon:"67°W to 49°W",borders:[30,31,43,45,46],history:'Bermuda was a key Allied base and convoy hub. From May 1940 to July 1941, 82 BHX convoys sailed from the islands to meet Halifax-to-Britain HX convoys at sea. Under the September 1940 Destroyers for Bases Agreement, Britain granted the United States base rights. American installations, including a naval air station and an army airfield used jointly with the British, joined the Royal Naval Dockyard and the RAF and Fleet Air Arm seaplane stations. At the Hamilton Princess Hotel, some 1,200 British Imperial Censorship staff analysed transatlantic mail, radio and telegraph traffic, helping uncover Axis spies working in the United States, among them the Joe K ring. In 1942 U-boats hunted unescorted ships in the surrounding waters. On 26 June U-107 sank the Dutch passenger-cargo ship Jagersfontein, carrying 86 US Army officers, about 500 miles east of the islands; a Swiss steamer picked up the survivors, and USS Bernadou landed the officers at Bermuda. On 19 June 1944 the captured U-505 was brought into the Great Sound and kept there in secrecy, painted to resemble an American submarine and renamed "USS Nemo" to hide her capture.',sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bermuda","https://www.convoyweb.org.uk/bhx/bhxhome.htm","https://mass.cultureelerfgoed.nl/jagersfontein","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_submarine_U-505"]},45:{name:"Central North Atlantic (west of the Azores)",area:"Open ocean between Bermuda and the Azores.",lat:"32°N to 37°N",lon:"48°W to 25°W",borders:[31,32,44,46,47,48],history:"This central Atlantic zone between Bermuda and the Azores lay across the route of the American UGS convoys from Hampton Roads to North Africa. From November 1942 to May 1945 about 100 of these slow convoys carried roughly 5,800 ships. In March 1943 U-boat groups including Unverzagt attacked convoy UGS 6. Near the zone's northern margin, U-172 sank the straggler Keystone on 13 March after engine trouble left her behind the convoy; 70 of the 72 aboard survived. Three more of the convoy's ships were sunk over the following days. The decisive shift came in summer 1943, when US escort carrier groups went hunting around the convoy route. On 23 July 1943 an Avenger from USS Bogue sank U-527 south of the Azores (40 dead, 13 survivors). The same day the destroyer USS George E. Badger sank U-613 nearby with all 48 men. Later, patrol bombers flying from the Azores joined escort carriers in covering most of the convoys. Beyond these actions this is deep open ocean, crossed mainly by the Gibraltar convoy routes and by U-boats heading to and from distant patrol areas.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UG_convoys","https://www.uboat.net/allies/merchants/ship/2776.html","https://uboat.net/boats/u527.htm","https://uboat.net/boats/u613.htm"]},46:{name:"Sargasso Sea (north)",area:"Open Atlantic south of Bermuda.",lat:"26°N to 30°N",lon:"66°W to 45°W",borders:[43,44,45,47,72,73,74],history:"No major naval actions are recorded in this stretch of open Atlantic south of Bermuda, the northern part of the Sargasso Sea. The Sargasso Sea lies inside the North Atlantic Gyre and is bounded by the Gulf Stream, the North Atlantic Current, the Canary Current and the North Atlantic Equatorial Current. It is known for its floating Sargassum weed and for the light, often calm winds of the horse latitudes. In the war it was mainly a transit area. German and Italian submarines heading from their French bases toward the US coast and the Caribbean in 1942 probably crossed this general area, but the heavy sinkings happened on either side of it. They came off the American coast to the west, around Bermuda to the north, and in the Caribbean, where almost 400 Allied ships were lost. In March 1942 the Italian submarine Enrico Tazzoli, on patrol from Bordeaux toward Florida, sank the Norwegian Tønsbergfjord south-west of Bermuda and the Uruguayan Montevideo at about 29°N, 69°W, both just west of this zone.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sargasso_Sea","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Caribbean","https://regiamarina.net/r-smg-enrico-tazzoli/"]},47:{name:"Central Atlantic (south-west of the Azores)",area:"Open ocean south-west of the Azores.",lat:"28°N to 35°N",lon:"44°W to 23°W",borders:[45,46,48,74],history:`The waters south-west of the Azores became a killing ground for U-boats in mid-1943. American escort carrier groups covering the UGS convoys to Gibraltar and North Africa went on the offensive against U-boats in transit and the supply boats that refuelled them. Aircraft from USS Bogue sank U-217 with all 50 hands on 5 June 1943. On 12 June they sank U-118 west of the Canaries, killing 43 and leaving 16 survivors. Avengers from USS Santee, armed with the new Fido homing torpedo, sank U-160 on 14 July and U-509 on 15 July south of the Azores, both with all hands, and U-43 with all 55 men on 30 July. Just to the south, aircraft from USS Core sank the Type XIV supply boat ("milk cow") U-487 on 13 July; 33 of her crew were rescued by USS Barker. These carrier successes helped break the U-boats' ability to operate far from their bases. Otherwise this is deep subtropical ocean, crossed by the Gibraltar convoy routes.`,sources:["https://uboat.net/boats/u217.htm","https://uboat.net/boats/u118.htm","https://uboat.net/boats/u160.htm","https://uboat.net/boats/u487.htm"]},48:{name:"Off Portugal & Gibraltar approaches",area:"Off Portugal (Lisbon) and the Atlantic approaches to the Strait of Gibraltar, including Madeira.",lat:"31°N to 43°N",lon:"23°W to 8°W",borders:[32,33,45,47,74,78,79],history:`These Atlantic approaches to Gibraltar and neutral Portugal saw one of the first clear Allied convoy victories. Convoy HG 76 left Gibraltar on 14 December 1941, guarded by Commander Frederick "Johnnie" Walker's 36th Escort Group and the small escort carrier HMS Audacity. On 17 December, north-east of Madeira, escorts and a Martlet fighter from Audacity destroyed U-131. On 18 December HMS Blankney and Stanley sank U-434 north of Madeira. Early on 19 December U-574 torpedoed Stanley, which blew up, and the sloop HMS Stork rammed and sank the U-boat. Audacity herself was sunk farther north on 21 December, but five U-boats were lost against the convoy. Lisbon and Porto also served as havens: in August 1941 ten merchant ships of the battered convoy OG 71 sought refuge in Portugal. From November 1942 the UGS convoys to North Africa crossed these waters, and in March 1943 U-boats attacked UGS 6 here. The straggling Liberty ship Molly Pitcher was torpedoed by U-167 on 17 March and finished off by U-521 the next day, with four of 70 aboard lost. Gibraltar-bound traffic passed through these approaches throughout the war.`,sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoy_HG_76","https://uboat.net/boats/u131.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoy_OG_71","https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/2808.html"]},49:{name:"Western Mediterranean (Balearic Sea & Gulf of Lion)",area:"North-western Mediterranean: Gulf of Lion (Toulon, Marseille), Balearic Islands and Catalan coast, west of Corsica and Sardinia.",lat:"40°N to 44°N",lon:"3°E to 9°E",borders:[50,80],history:"The north-western Mediterranean saw the dramatic end of the Vichy French fleet. When German forces entered Toulon on 27 November 1942 to seize it, the French scuttled 77 vessels, including the battleships Strasbourg, Dunkerque and Provence and seven cruisers. The order to scuttle was signalled from Strasbourg, flagship of Admiral Jean de Laborde. Four submarines escaped, three to Algiers and Oran and one to Barcelona. On 9 September 1943, the day after Italy's armistice was announced, the Italian battleship Roma was sunk off north-western Sardinia by Fritz X radio-guided bombs from Luftwaffe Do 217s; between about 1,250 and 1,390 men died, including Admiral Carlo Bergamini. On 15 August 1944 Operation Dragoon landed Allied troops on the Provence coast at Cavalaire, Saint-Tropez and Saint-Raphaël, supported by the battleships Nevada, Texas, Arkansas, Ramillies and Lorraine and about 20 cruisers. USS Somers sank two German warships off Port-Cros that day, and a German glide bomb sank LST-282. Two more German vessels were sunk off La Ciotat on 17 August. The German garrison of Toulon had surrendered by 26 August and Marseille's on 28 August, handing the Allies two major ports.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scuttling_of_the_French_fleet_at_Toulon","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_battleship_Roma_(1940)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Dragoon"]},50:{name:"Tyrrhenian & Ligurian Seas",area:"Between Corsica/Sardinia and the Italian mainland: Genoa, La Spezia, Naples and northern Sicily.",lat:"38°N to 44°N",lon:"9°E to 16°E",borders:[49,51,80],history:"The Tyrrhenian and Ligurian seas lay at the heart of Italy's naval world, with bases at La Spezia, Genoa and Naples. On 9 February 1941, in Operation Grog, Britain's Force H from Gibraltar, with HMS Renown, Malaya, Sheffield and Ark Royal, bombarded Genoa harbour while Ark Royal's aircraft attacked Livorno and La Spezia was mined. An Italian attempt to intercept failed, and 144 civilians were killed in Genoa. The great Allied landings came later. On 9 September 1943 Operation Avalanche put Allied troops ashore at Salerno. HMS Warspite arrived off the beachhead on 15 September and bombarded German positions inland, but on 16 September a Fritz X guided bomb crippled her. On 22 January 1944 Operation Shingle landed troops at Anzio almost unopposed, but the crowded anchorage became a target: a Henschel Hs 293 glide bomb sank the cruiser HMS Spartan there on 29 January, killing 46. On 18 February the cruiser HMS Penelope was torpedoed by U-410 off Naples while returning to Anzio and sank with 417 men.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Grog","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMS_Warspite_(03)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMS_Spartan_(95)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Anzio"]},51:{name:"Adriatic & Ionian Seas",area:"The Adriatic (Venice, Trieste, Yugoslav coast, Albania) and the Ionian Sea between southern Italy (Taranto), eastern Sicily and western Greece.",lat:"36°N to 44°N",lon:"16°E to 21°E",borders:[50,52,80,81],history:"The Ionian Sea and Adriatic were the battleground between Britain's Mediterranean Fleet and Italy's main base at Taranto. On 9 July 1940, in the Battle of Calabria off Punta Stilo, HMS Warspite hit the battleship Giulio Cesare at over 24,000 metres in an indecisive clash. On the night of 11-12 November 1940, 21 Swordfish from HMS Illustrious struck Taranto harbour in Operation Judgement, disabling the battleships Conte di Cavour, Littorio and Duilio. The Japanese studied the raid while planning Pearl Harbor. That same night British cruisers and destroyers sank all four merchant ships of an Italian convoy in the Strait of Otranto. On 27-29 March 1941 Admiral Cunningham's fleet fought the Battle of Cape Matapan west and south-west of the Peloponnese, sinking the heavy cruisers Zara, Pola and Fiume and the destroyers Alfieri and Carducci. About 2,300 Italian sailors died, against three British airmen. On 8-9 November 1941 Malta's Force K, the cruisers Aurora and Penelope with two destroyers, destroyed all seven merchant ships of the Duisburg convoy and the destroyer Fulmine without loss, about 135 miles east of Syracuse.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Calabria","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Taranto","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Cape_Matapan","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Duisburg_Convoy"]},52:{name:"Aegean Sea & Crete",area:"The Aegean between Greece and Turkey, including the Dodecanese, Crete and the Dardanelles approaches.",lat:"34°N to 40°N",lon:"22°E to 29°E",borders:[35,51,53,81],history:"The Aegean was where the Royal Navy suffered some of its heaviest losses to air power. During the Battle of Crete (20 May-1 June 1941), British cruisers and destroyers intercepted a German caïque convoy bound for Maleme on the night of 21 May, sinking eight boats and turning the seaborne invasion back. Axis aircraft then sank the cruisers Gloucester, Fiji and Calcutta and the destroyers Juno, Greyhound, Kelly, Kashmir and Hereward, and wrecked Imperial; about 1,828 Royal Navy men died. The fleet still evacuated about 18,600 troops from Heraklion and Sphakia. In the Dodecanese campaign of September-November 1943, Britain tried to seize Italy's islands after the armistice. German air superiority won again: Kos fell on 3-4 October and Leros on 16 November, and six Allied destroyers were sunk, among them Intrepid, the Greek Vasilissa Olga, Panther, Eclipse, Hurworth and Dulverton. On 12 February 1944 the steamer Oria, carrying more than 4,000 Italian prisoners of war from Rhodes, sank in a storm off Cape Sounion; only 29 people survived. The Battle of Cape Matapan of March 1941 was fought in neighbouring waters to the west.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Crete","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dodecanese_campaign","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Oria_(1920)"]},53:{name:"Eastern Mediterranean (north) & Cyprus",area:"Off southern Turkey and Cyprus, east of Rhodes, toward the Syrian coast.",lat:"34°N to 36°N",lon:"30°E to 36°E",borders:[52,81],history:"The waters off Cyprus and the Syrian-Lebanese coast saw a short naval campaign between British and Vichy French forces during the Syria-Lebanon campaign (Operation Exporter) of June-July 1941. Royal Navy and Australian cruisers and destroyers bombarded coastal positions in support of the advance, and on 15 June bombers damaged the destroyers Ilex and Isis. Before dawn on 16 June, six Swordfish of 815 Squadron torpedoed the French destroyer Chevalier Paul off Latakia as she carried ammunition to Beirut; Valmy and Guépard rescued all but six or seven of her crew. On 21 June the destroyer Jackal intercepted the French hospital ship Canada south-west of Cyprus and took her to Haifa for inspection. On 23 June Naiad, Leander and several destroyers engaged and damaged Guépard north of Beirut. Ships carried Fleet Air Arm torpedoes and stores to Famagusta in Cyprus. Just to the south, the submarine HMS Parthian sank the French submarine Souffleur off Beirut on 25 June. A ceasefire took effect on 12 July 1941, and the Armistice of Saint Jean d'Acre was signed on 14 July, ending the campaign.",sources:["https://www.naval-history.net/xDKWW2-4106-33JUN02.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syria%E2%80%93Lebanon_campaign","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_destroyer_Chevalier_Paul_(1932)"]},54:{name:"Yellow Sea & Bohai",area:"Between China and Korea: Bohai Gulf, Shandong, the Yellow Sea and down toward the Yangtze mouth/Shanghai.",lat:"29°N to 40°N",lon:"122°E to 127°E",borders:[38,55,89],history:"Japan's war at sea in Chinese waters began here in 1937. As the Battle of Shanghai opened in August 1937, Japanese Third Fleet warships on the Huangpu and Yangtze shelled Chinese positions. On 14 August Chinese aircraft attacking the Japanese flagship Izumo, moored off the Bund, dropped stray bombs into the International Settlement, killing hundreds of civilians. Japanese forces landed at Wusong and nearby beaches on 23 August, while the Chinese navy massed cruisers and mines at Jiangyin to block the Yangtze; Japanese aircraft sank ten Chinese ships there by October. On 8 December 1941 Japanese marines seized the US gunboat Wake at Shanghai, and when HMS Peterel's captain refused to surrender, Izumo, the gunboat Toba and shore batteries sank her at her mooring. On 1 October 1942 the submarine USS Grouper torpedoed the Lisbon Maru near the Zhoushan islands; over 800 of the 1,816 British prisoners aboard died, and Chinese fishermen rescued 384, most of whom were recaptured. In March 1943 USS Wahoo sank about eight Japanese ships in the northern Yellow Sea during her fourth war patrol, part of the growing American submarine campaign against Japan's sea lanes.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Shanghai","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMS_Peterel_(1927)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lisbon_Maru","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Wahoo_(SS-238)"]},55:{name:"East China Sea (south) & Ryukyus",area:"South of Kyushu and east of China/Taiwan, including the Ryukyu Islands and Okinawa.",lat:"21°N to 30°N",lon:"126°E to 136°E",borders:[38,54,65,89,91],history:"The Ryukyus saw the last great naval campaign of the Pacific war. After US forces seized the Kerama Islands as a fleet anchorage on 26 March 1945, troops landed on Okinawa on 1 April. The ships offshore then endured waves of kamikaze attacks: seven major attacks between 1 April and 25 May involved more than 1,500 aircraft. Radar-picket destroyers, destroyer escorts and landing ships bore the brunt, and the US Navy lost about 4,900 dead, more than its wounded, before the land battle ended on 22 June. On 7 April the battleship Yamato, on the suicide sortie Operation Ten-Go, was sunk by US carrier aircraft south-west of Kyushu at the northern edge of these waters, along with the cruiser Yahagi and four destroyers; between about 3,700 and 4,250 Japanese sailors died. Earlier, US submarines preyed on Japanese traffic through the island chain. On 22 August 1944 USS Bowfin torpedoed the evacuation ship Tsushima Maru near Akusekijima, killing about 1,500 people, including some 780 schoolchildren.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Okinawa","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Ten-Go","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsushima_Maru"]},56:{name:"Pacific east of Japan",area:"Off the Pacific coast of Honshu and Hokkaido, including the approaches to Tokyo Bay.",lat:"27°N to 47°N",lon:"137°E to 150°E",borders:[38,39,57,59,65],history:"The Pacific approaches to Tokyo and the coasts of Honshu and Hokkaido became, by 1945, a hunting ground for the US Navy. On 29 November 1944 the submarine USS Archerfish torpedoed the giant carrier Shinano, ten days after her commissioning, as she sailed from Yokosuka to Kure; she sank about 65 miles from land with 1,435 dead. In July 1945 Allied battleships shelled Japan's own coast. South Dakota, Indiana and Massachusetts bombarded the Kamaishi ironworks on 14 July; Iowa, Missouri and Wisconsin hit the steelworks at Muroran, Hokkaido, on 15 July; and on 17-18 July a combined US-British force including HMS King George V bombarded the Hitachi area north-east of Tokyo. King George V shelled Hamamatsu on 29 July, and a second strike on Kamaishi on 9 August included HMS Newfoundland and HMNZS Gambia. Carrier aircraft also attacked shipping off Hokkaido and northern Honshu on 14-15 July. From November 1944 Japan launched Fu-Go balloon bombs eastward from the Pacific coast of Honshu. The war ended in these waters: on 2 September 1945 Japan signed the Instrument of Surrender aboard USS Missouri in Tokyo Bay.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_aircraft_carrier_Shinano","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allied_naval_bombardments_of_Japan_during_World_War_II","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_Instrument_of_Surrender","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fu-Go_balloon_bomb"]},57:{name:"North-west Pacific (east of northern Japan)",area:"Open ocean east of Hokkaido/northern Honshu.",lat:"35°N to 46°N",lon:"148°E to 162°E",borders:[39,40,56,58,59],history:"No major battles were fought in this open ocean east of Hokkaido and northern Honshu, but two famous voyages crossed it. Nagumo's Pearl Harbor striking force left Hitokappu (Tankan) Bay in the Kurils on 26 November 1941 and steamed east through these waters on a route chosen to pass between the Aleutians and Midway, out of range of American air patrols. In April 1942 the tables turned: Halsey's Task Force 16, with USS Hornet carrying 16 B-25 bombers and Enterprise providing cover, approached Japan from the east. At 07:38 on 18 April the picket boat No. 23 Nitto Maru, one of a line of Japanese patrol craft far offshore, sighted the force at about 35°N, 154°E, some 650 miles from Japan, and radioed a warning. The cruiser Nashville sank it, and Nashville and Enterprise aircraft sank or damaged several other pickets. Doolittle's bombers launched early, between 08:20 and 09:19, to raid Tokyo and other cities. Later, between November 1944 and April 1945, about 9,300 Fu-Go balloon bombs launched from Honshu drifted east across this ocean on the high-altitude winds toward North America.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doolittle_Raid","https://history.navy.mil/browse-by-topic/wars-conflicts-and-operations/world-war-ii/1942/halsey-doolittle-raid.html","https://www.usni.org/magazines/proceedings/1952/september/i-led-air-attack-pearl-harbor","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fu-Go_balloon_bomb"]},58:{name:"North-west Pacific (central)",area:"Open ocean east of Japan, west of the dateline.",lat:"31°N to 42°N",lon:"163°E to 173°E",borders:[40,57,60,61,62],history:"No major naval actions are recorded in this stretch of the north-west Pacific, east of Japan and west of the International Date Line. It is deep, open ocean far from any island base: Wake Island lies well to the south and the western Aleutians far to the north. The zone mattered mainly as a corridor for long transits. Nagumo's Pearl Harbor striking force crossed the North Pacific eastward from the Kurils at about 42°N in late November 1941, along the northern margin of these waters. Its operation order named an assembly point for the homeward voyage at 30°N, 165°E, just to the south. In April 1942 Halsey's Task Force 16, with the carriers Hornet and Enterprise and Doolittle's 16 B-25 bombers, ran west from its rendezvous north of Hawaii to a launch point about 650 miles east of Japan, crossing these longitudes on the way. Japanese picket boats watched the seaward approaches farther west. In the winter of 1944-45 the high-altitude westerly winds that carried some 9,300 Fu-Go balloon bombs toward North America also blew across this ocean.",sources:["https://www.ibiblio.org/pha/timeline/411123aijn.html","https://www.usni.org/magazines/proceedings/1952/september/i-led-air-attack-pearl-harbor","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doolittle_Raid","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fu-Go_balloon_bomb"]},59:{name:"Bonin Islands (probable)",area:"South-east of Honshu; its island most likely stands for the Bonin (Ogasawara) Islands, e.g. Chichi-jima.",lat:"24°N to 33°N",lon:"138°E to 148°E",borders:[56,57,60,65,66],history:"The Bonin (Ogasawara) Islands, above all Chichi-jima, were a Japanese naval supply and communications base whose long-range radio stations made them a regular target once American carriers reached the area in mid-1944. That year all 6,886 civilian inhabitants were evacuated to the home islands. On 4 August 1944 aircraft of Task Group 58.1 caught Convoy No. 4804 heading north from Chichi-jima and sank all but one of its cargo ships; that evening American cruisers and destroyers sent to bombard the island overtook the survivors, and the destroyers Cogswell, Ingersoll and Knapp sank the escort Matsu about 50 miles north-west of Chichi-jima, with only six of her crew rescued. On 2 September 1944 Lieutenant (jg) George H. W. Bush of VT-51, flying from USS San Jacinto, was shot down attacking Chichi-jima; both his crewmen died, and the lifeguard submarine Finback picked him up after about four hours in his raft. Other airmen captured on the island were executed, and some partly eaten, in the Chichi-jima incident, for which four Japanese officers were later hanged. Never invaded, the chain's garrison of about 25,000 troops surrendered at the war's end.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chichijima","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_destroyer_Matsu_(1944)","https://history.navy.mil/research/histories/biographies-list/bios-b/bush-george-h-w.html"]},60:{name:"Western Pacific (between the Bonins and Wake)",area:"Open ocean east of the Bonins toward Marcus and Wake.",lat:"20°N to 32°N",lon:"161°E to 175°E",borders:[58,59,62,66,67],history:"No major naval actions are recorded in this stretch of open ocean east of the Bonins; Wake lies just to the south in adjacent waters, with Marcus Island toward the west. The one well-documented episode came at the start of the Battle of Midway. On 3 June 1942 a Midway-based PBY Catalina sighted the Japanese invasion transport group steaming east, roughly 500 to 700 miles west-south-west of Midway according to different accounts, a position in or near this zone. Army B-17s from Midway bombed the transports later that day without scoring hits, and in the early hours of 4 June PBY-5A amphibians made a night torpedo attack that hit the oiler Akebono Maru; it was the only successful American aerial torpedo attack of the battle. Otherwise these were empty waters lying between the isolated Japanese outposts on Wake and Marcus and the American base at Midway, crossed by occasional Japanese supply traffic and by American submarines. The climate is subtropical, with trade winds in the south and typhoons possible in late summer and autumn.",sources:["https://ibiblio.org/hyperwar/OnlineLibrary/photos/events/wwii-pac/midway/mid-1m.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Midway"]},61:{name:"North Pacific (north of Midway)",area:"Open ocean north-west of Midway.",lat:"33°N to 40°N",lon:"174°E to 169°W",borders:[40,41,58,62,63],history:"No major naval actions are recorded in this stretch of the North Pacific north-west of Midway, and no sinkings could be confidently placed here. It is a wide expanse of open ocean lying between the great-circle routes far to the north, which link Japan with the Aleutians and North America, and the Hawaiian chain to the south. Its significance in the war was as approach water. In June 1942 Admiral Nagumo's four-carrier striking force came in toward Midway from the north-west, with Yamamoto's battleships and cruisers trailing several hundred miles behind. At dawn on 4 June the carriers launched their strike from about 250 nautical miles north-west of the atoll, and the carrier battle itself was fought further south, in the waters around Midway. After the battle Midway became a forward base for American submarines, some of which crossed this part of the ocean on their way to patrol Japanese home waters. Conditions here are harsher than in the tropics further south: cool, often overcast, with frequent fog and winter gales generated by storms tracking eastward across the North Pacific.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_aircraft_carrier_Akagi","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Midway","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midway_Atoll"]},62:{name:"Midway",area:"Midway Atoll and surrounding waters at the western end of the Hawaiian chain.",lat:"26°N to 33°N",lon:"175°E to 168°W",borders:[58,60,61,63,67],history:"Midway Atoll gave its name to the decisive carrier battle of the Pacific war. On 7 December 1941 the destroyers Ushio and Sazanami shelled Sand Island, killing four men, among them First Lieutenant George H. Cannon, the first Marine awarded the Medal of Honor for actions in the Second World War; further attacks, including a submarine bombardment in February 1942, followed. In June 1942 Admiral Yamamoto sought to seize Midway and destroy the US Pacific Fleet's carriers, but American codebreakers allowed Enterprise, Hornet and Yorktown to wait undetected at Point Luck north-east of the atoll. On 4 June Nagumo launched 108 aircraft against Midway from about 250 nautical miles to the north-west, and American dive bombers then wrecked Akagi, Kaga and Soryu; Hiryu was crippled that afternoon. Akagi was scuttled on 5 June at 30°30′N 178°40′W, and her wreck was found there in 2019. The cruiser Mikuma was sunk during the pursuit to the west. Yorktown, crippled by Hiryu's aircraft, was torpedoed by submarine I-168 on 6 June, which also sank the destroyer Hammann; Yorktown capsized on 7 June. Japan lost 3,057 men and 248 aircraft; the United States 307 men. From July 1942 Midway served as a submarine base.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Bombardment_of_Midway","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Midway","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_aircraft_carrier_Akagi","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midway_Atoll"]},63:{name:"North Pacific (between Midway and Hawaii, north)",area:"Open ocean north-west of the main Hawaiian Islands.",lat:"26°N to 38°N",lon:"167°W to 152°W",borders:[41,42,61,62,64,68],history:"This open ocean north and north-west of the main Hawaiian Islands saw no battles of its own, but it was the approach route for the Pearl Harbor attack. Admiral Nagumo's six carriers, having sailed from Hitokappu Bay in the Kurils on 26 November 1941 across the stormy northern Pacific, ran south toward Oahu, and at about 06:10 on 7 December began launching their first wave roughly 200 miles from the island, near the boundary with Hawaiian waters; Opana radar detected the formation approaching from the north about 130 miles out. Six months later the roles were reversed. Enterprise and Hornet left Pearl Harbor on 28 May 1942 and Yorktown on 30 May, steaming north-west to rendezvous about 300 miles north-east of Midway at Point Luck; a Japanese submarine picket line meant to watch these waters was late getting into position, so the carriers passed undetected. Throughout the war these waters were crossed by naval traffic between Pearl Harbor and Midway. They lie on the northern fringe of the trade-wind belt, warm in summer but subject to winter storms.",sources:["https://www.nps.gov/perl/learn/historyculture/timeline.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attack_on_Pearl_Harbor","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Midway","https://ibiblio.org/hyperwar/OnlineLibrary/photos/events/wwii-pac/midway/mid-1h.htm"]},64:{name:"NE Pacific off California",area:"Off the California coast (San Francisco, Los Angeles, San Diego) and out toward Hawaii.",lat:"26°N to 40°N",lon:"150°W to 122°W",borders:[29,42,63,68,69,70],history:"Off California the war came close to shore in its first weeks. Japanese submarines deployed along the coast in December 1941: on 20 December I-17 torpedoed the tanker Emidio off Cape Mendocino, at the zone's northern edge; on 23 December I-21 sank the tanker Montebello off Cambria shortly after she left Avila; and on 24 December I-19 torpedoed the freighter Absaroka off Point Fermin near Los Angeles, killing one man. Several other ships escaped attacks. Further offshore, on the routes to Hawaii, I-26 sank the lumber ship Cynthia Olson, bound from Tacoma to Honolulu, on 7 December 1941 with all 35 aboard lost (her SOS gave about 33°42′N 145°29′W), and I-9 attacked the Matson freighter Lahaina on 11 December some 700 miles north-east of Oahu; she sank the next day. On 23 February 1942 I-17 shelled the oil installations at Ellwood near Santa Barbara, causing little damage but fuelling an invasion scare and, the next night, the false-alarm Battle of Los Angeles. San Francisco Bay and San Diego were major naval bases; on 17 July 1944 the Port Chicago explosion in the Bay killed 320 people, and on 16 July 1945 Indianapolis sailed from San Francisco carrying atomic bomb components.",sources:["https://historynet.com/japanese-submarines-prowl-the-us-pacific-coastline-in-1941/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bombardment_of_Ellwood","https://pacificwrecks.com/ship/ss/lahaina.html","https://maritimequest.com/daily_event_archive/2008/12_dec/07_ss_cynthia_olson.htm"]},65:{name:"Philippine Sea (north) & Volcano Islands - Iwo Jima",area:"South of Japan between the Ryukyus and the Bonins; its island most likely stands for Iwo Jima (the rulebook names Iwo Jima as a Japanese island territory).",lat:"20°N to 28°N",lon:"130°E to 145°E",borders:[38,55,56,59,66,91,92],history:"This northern part of the Philippine Sea is dominated by Iwo Jima in the Volcano Islands, whose airfields and radar stood on the B-29 route between the Marianas and Japan. From mid-June 1944, as the Marianas campaign opened, American aircraft and warships began raiding the island; on 4 July 1944 cruisers including Boston, Canberra and Santa Fe bombarded its airfields. After about nine months of intermittent air and naval bombardment, including only three days of concentrated naval fire before the landing, US Marines went ashore on 19 February 1945 from a fleet of some 450 ships. Japanese coastal guns had already hit nine LCI(G) gunboats on 17 February, sinking one. On 21 February kamikazes sank the escort carrier Bismarck Sea with the loss of 318 men and badly damaged the carrier Saratoga, which lost more than 120 killed. The flag was raised on Mount Suribachi on 23 February, and the island was declared secure on 26 March after fighting that cost the Americans more than 26,000 casualties and killed most of the roughly 21,000 Japanese defenders. Iwo Jima later received more than 2,200 B-29 landings.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Iwo_Jima","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Bismarck_Sea","https://ca-69boston.org/july-4-1944/"]},66:{name:"Wake Island",area:"Wake Island and surrounding central Pacific waters.",lat:"14°N to 22°N",lon:"158°E to 175°E",borders:[59,60,65,67,92],history:"Wake Island was the scene of one of the first and most celebrated American stands of the Pacific war. On 8 December 1941, hours after Pearl Harbor, Japanese bombers destroyed eight of the twelve Marine F4F Wildcats on the ground. On 11 December the first Japanese landing attempt was beaten off: Battery L's coastal guns on Wilkes Islet sank the destroyer Hayate and Wildcats sank the destroyer Kisaragi. A relief force built around Saratoga and the seaplane tender Tangier was recalled as the atoll fell; Japan had returned with the carriers Hiryu and Soryu and some 1,500 more troops, landing on 23 December and forcing a surrender. American losses included 49 Marines killed, and about 1,600 people, most of them civilian construction workers, were captured. Enterprise raided the Japanese garrison on 24 February 1942, and Wake was bombed periodically thereafter; cut off from supply, many of its garrison starved. After a US carrier raid in October 1943, the commander, Sakaibara, ordered the 98 American prisoners still on the island executed, a crime for which he was executed in 1947. The garrison surrendered aboard USS Levy on 4 September 1945.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Wake_Island","https://oceanexplorer.noaa.gov/okeanos/explorations/ex1606/background/hayate/welcome.html"]},67:{name:"Central Pacific (between Wake and Hawaii)",area:"Open ocean south-west of Midway and west of Hawaii (around Johnston).",lat:"15°N to 26°N",lon:"177°E to 168°W",borders:[60,62,66,68,94],history:"Most of this zone is empty ocean south-west of Midway, but it contains Johnston Atoll, a small American outpost whose wartime story is well documented. On 7 December 1941 the heavy cruiser Indianapolis was off Johnston carrying out a simulated bombardment when Pearl Harbor was attacked. On 15 December a Japanese submarine shelled the atoll, hitting several buildings including the power station without injuring anyone, and further shelling followed on 22 and 23 December. The atoll's runway had been completed by the time war broke out, and in December 1943 a Navy construction battalion lengthened it to 6,000 feet. Johnston served as a refuelling base for submarines and as a refuelling stop for American bombers crossing the Pacific, among them the B-29 Enola Gay, and by 1944 it was one of the busiest air transport terminals in the Pacific. No major sea battles were fought in these waters, which lie in the steady north-east trade winds between the Hawaiian chain and the Marshall Islands.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johnston_Atoll","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Indianapolis_(CA-35)"]},68:{name:"Hawaiian Islands",area:"The main Hawaiian Islands (Oahu/Pearl Harbor, Kauai, Maui, Hawaii) and surrounding waters.",lat:"15°N to 25°N",lon:"167°W to 146°W",borders:[63,64,67,69,95,98],history:"The Hawaiian Islands were where the Pacific war began. Shortly before 07:00 on 7 December 1941 the destroyer Ward sank a Japanese midget submarine off Pearl Harbor, and from 07:48 some 350 aircraft from six Japanese carriers attacked the fleet and airfields on Oahu. Arizona exploded with the loss of 1,177 men, Oklahoma capsized, and in all eighteen ships were sunk or run aground, including five battleships; 2,403 Americans were killed. A damaged Japanese aircraft crash-landed on Niihau, setting off the Niihau incident. In the following weeks Japanese submarines shelled Kahului on Maui on 15 December and Nawiliwili, Hilo and Kahului again on 30–31 December, causing little damage. On 4 March 1942, in Operation K, two Kawanishi H8K flying boats refuelled from submarines at French Frigate Shoals and dropped bombs harmlessly near Honolulu; American patrols then blocked a repeat attempt that might have located the US carriers before Midway. Pearl Harbor became the main base for the Pacific counteroffensive, though on 21 May 1944 the West Loch disaster destroyed six LSTs preparing for the Marianas invasion and killed about 163 men.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attack_on_Pearl_Harbor","https://imagesofoldhawaii.com/after-december-7-1941-submarines-continue-attack-on-hawai%CA%BBi/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_K","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Loch_disaster"]},69:{name:"Eastern Pacific (east of Hawaii)",area:"Open ocean between Hawaii and Baja California.",lat:"16°N to 25°N",lon:"144°W to 129°W",borders:[64,68,70,98],history:"No naval actions or sinkings could be found for this stretch of open ocean between Hawaii and Baja California. It is one of the emptiest parts of the North Pacific, with no islands, and it lay well away from the war's battle zones. The busiest wartime routes, between the California ports and Pearl Harbor, ran mostly to the north of it, where Japanese submarines sank several merchant ships in December 1941; those losses are described under the adjoining zones off California and around Hawaii. Ships steaming between the Panama Canal and Hawaii could pass through or near this area, and US Navy and merchant traffic heading west to the Pacific bases was the main wartime use of these waters. The region lies in the belt of the north-east trade winds, with generally moderate seas, warm temperatures and fair weather for much of the year, although eastern Pacific hurricanes forming off Mexico in summer and early autumn sometimes track westward across it toward Hawaii.",sources:["https://pacificwrecks.com/ship/ss/lahaina.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Cynthia_Olson"]},70:{name:"Off Baja California & Mexico",area:"Off Baja California and Mexico's Pacific coast.",lat:"17°N to 30°N",lon:"127°W to 100°W",borders:[64,69,97,98],history:"No significant sea battles were fought off Baja California and Mexico's Pacific coast, and the zone's wartime history is mainly one of coastal defence. After Pearl Harbor Mexico broke off relations with Japan and ordered Japanese residents away from the coast. A joint Mexican–US defence commission was set up by executive agreement on 2 January 1942, and former president Lázaro Cárdenas was recalled to command the Pacific zone. Mexican aircraft began daily patrols from Cedros Island northward, and gunboats protected American minefields. Mexico declared war in May 1942 after German U-boats sank the tankers Potrero del Llano and Faja de Oro, though those sinkings took place in the Gulf of Mexico and nearby Atlantic waters, not here. From June 1942 US Army early-warning radar stations operated on the Baja coast, run by Mexican troops from that August, and airfields were planned. Fears that Japan would use Baja California as a base or landing site proved unfounded: Cárdenas reported that no Japanese submarines or aircraft had used it, and no confirmed enemy attacks in these waters could be found.",sources:["https://bajabound.com/bajaadventures/bajatravel/wwii_in_baja","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico_in_World_War_II"]},71:{name:"Gulf of Mexico",area:"The Gulf of Mexico: US Gulf coast (Texas to Florida), the Mississippi Delta, Mexico's coast and the Yucatán Channel.",lat:"20°N to 29°N",lon:"96°W to 83°W",borders:[72,75],history:"The Gulf of Mexico became a U-boat hunting ground in 1942, when German submarines extended their offensive against American waters to the tankers sailing from Texas and Louisiana oil ports. Around 56 vessels were sunk in the Gulf in 1942–43, many of them tankers, most of them in 1942, when lit coastlines silhouetted ships at night and more than twenty U-boats operated there. On 21 May 1942 U-106 torpedoed the Mexican tanker Faja de Oro off Key West, killing ten; together with Potrero del Llano, sunk off Florida a week earlier, this pushed Mexico to declare war on the Axis. On 30 July 1942 U-166 sank the passenger ship Robert E. Lee about 45 miles south of the Mississippi Delta, and her escort, the submarine chaser PC-566, counter-attacked with depth charges. U-166, the only U-boat sunk in the Gulf, was long credited to a Coast Guard aircraft, but her wreck, found near the Lee in 2001, showed PC-566 had sunk her, and the Navy corrected the record in 2014. Blackouts, armed patrols and escorted sailings from late 1942 ended the heavy losses.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_submarine_U-166_(1941)","https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/1690.html","https://hnoc.org/publishing/first-draft/1942-german-submarines-brought-wwii-louisianas-shores","https://mbac.gmu.edu/mbac_wp/world-war-ii-wrecks/"]},72:{name:"Bahamas & Florida Straits",area:"Off south-east Florida, the Bahamas, the Florida Straits, northern Cuba and the Windward Passage north of Hispaniola.",lat:"20°N to 27°N",lon:"80°W to 65°W",borders:[43,46,71,73,75,76],history:"The Florida Straits and Bahamas channels carried a stream of tankers and freighters between the Gulf, the Caribbean and the US East Coast, and U-boats hunted them hard in 1942. In March 1942, during Operation Neuland, U-126 patrolled the Windward Passage, sinking ships in and around it, some within sight of Guantánamo Bay; among its victims, the freighter Mariana was lost with all 36 men. On 14 May 1942 U-564 under Reinhard Suhren torpedoed the Mexican tanker Potrero del Llano east of Cape Florida, killing 13; survivors were landed at Miami, and the sinking helped bring Mexico into the war. On 11 June U-157 sank the molasses tanker Hagan off Cuba's north coast, and two days later the Key West-based cutter USCGC Thetis destroyed U-157 with all 52 hands south-west of Key West. Convoys later cut losses, but on 13 May 1943 U-176 sank the tankers Nickeliner and Mambí from convoy NC-18 off northern Cuba. Two days later a US Navy Kingfisher aircraft marked U-176's position and the Cuban patrol boat CS-13 sank her with depth charges off Cayo Blanquizal, Cuba's only U-boat kill of the war.",sources:["https://www.uboat.net/allies/merchants/1648.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_submarine_U-157_(1941)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_submarine_U-176","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Neuland"]},73:{name:"Western Atlantic north-east of the Antilles",area:"Open ocean north-east of Puerto Rico and the Leeward Islands.",lat:"21°N to 26°N",lon:"64°W to 50°W",borders:[46,72,74,76,77],history:"No major naval battles were fought in this stretch of open ocean north-east of Puerto Rico and the Leeward Islands, but it lay across the shipping lanes linking the Caribbean with the US East Coast, used by ships arriving from the Indian Ocean and South Atlantic, and in 1942 long-range U-boats picked off unescorted merchantmen here. On 3 June 1942 U-172 under Carl Emmermann sank the American freighter City of Alma, carrying manganese ore from Bombay to Baltimore, about 400 miles north-east of San Juan; 29 of her 39 crew died. Later that month U-153 struck twice. On 25 June it sank the British motor ship Anglo-Canadian about 800 miles north-east of Antigua, using a spotlight to help survivors reach their boats and handing them water and cigarettes; 49 of 50 aboard eventually landed on St Kitts. On 29 June it sank the American freighter Ruth some 320 miles north-north-east of Barbuda, with 35 of her 39 men lost. These warm subtropical waters, bordering the Sargasso Sea, are generally calm outside the summer and autumn hurricane season.",sources:["https://www.uboat.net/allies/merchants/ship/1740.html","https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/ship/1850.html","https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/1870.html"]},74:{name:"Central Atlantic (west of the Canaries)",area:"Open ocean between the Sargasso Sea and the Canary Islands.",lat:"23°N to 31°N",lon:"48°W to 23°W",borders:[46,47,48,73,77,78],history:"This open Atlantic between the Sargasso Sea and the Canaries lay on the U-boats' long transit routes to the Caribbean, Brazil and the South Atlantic, and in 1943 it became the hunting ground of American escort-carrier groups. Aircraft from USS Bogue sank U-217 with all hands on 5 June 1943 and U-118 on 12 June, when 16 men survived. USS Core's aircraft followed with the supply 'milch cow' U-487 on 13 July, U-67 on 16 July (only three survivors) and U-185 on 24 August, while USS Card's planes destroyed U-847 with all 62 aboard on 27 August using Fido homing torpedoes. On 13 December 1943 Bogue's group sank U-172 after a 27-hour hunt involving aircraft and four destroyers. Earlier, in January 1943, the tanker convoy TM-1 from Trinidad to Gibraltar, escorted by only a destroyer and three corvettes, was mauled west of the Canaries by the Delphin wolf pack: on 8–9 January U-436, U-442 and other boats sank Oltenia II, RFA Albert L. Ellsworth, Empire Lytton, Minister Wedel and Norvik, and in all seven of the nine tankers were lost.",sources:["https://uboat.net/boats/u487.htm","https://uboat.net/boats/u847.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Bogue","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoy_TM_1"]},75:{name:"Western Caribbean",area:"Western Caribbean: southern Cuba, Jamaica, Central America and the Panama Canal's Caribbean entrance (Colón).",lat:"11°N to 20°N",lon:"85°W to 70°W",borders:[71,72,76,103],history:"The western Caribbean guarded the Atlantic approach to the Panama Canal at Colón, and U-boats raided its shipping from 1942. On 3 July 1942 U-161 crept into Puerto Limón, Costa Rica, and torpedoed the Panamanian freighter San Pablo alongside the brightly lit pier, killing 24, mostly local stevedores working in her holds. Escorts struck back: on 28 August 1942, while attacking convoy TAW-15 between Jamaica and Haiti, U-94 was depth-charged by a US Catalina and rammed by the Canadian corvette HMCS Oakville, whose boarding party got aboard before the scuttled boat sank; 19 Germans died and 26 were captured. In July 1943 US Navy Mariner flying boats of VP-32 sank U-759 south of Haiti on 15 July and U-359 east of Jamaica on 26 July, both with all hands. In early October 1943 U-214 laid 15 mines off the canal's Caribbean entrance, which were soon discovered and swept. That month the US submarine Dorado, sailing from New London to the canal, vanished; a Court of Inquiry judged she had probably been attacked in error by a US Mariner flying boat on 12 October.",sources:["https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/ship/1881.html","https://ww2db.com/event/today/08/28/1942","https://uboat.net/boats/u759.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Dorado_(SS-248)"]},76:{name:"Eastern Caribbean & Lesser Antilles",area:"Eastern Caribbean: Puerto Rico, the Lesser Antilles, Venezuela, Aruba/Curaçao, Trinidad and the Guianas coast.",lat:"4°N to 20°N",lon:"70°W to 54°W",borders:[72,73,75,77,104],history:"The eastern Caribbean, with the oil refineries of Aruba and Curaçao and the bauxite and oil traffic past Trinidad, was struck in Operation Neuland on 16 February 1942. U-156 torpedoed tankers off Aruba's Lago refinery, sinking Oranjestad, but its deck gun burst when fired with the muzzle plug still in, killing a gunner. U-67 slipped into Willemstad, Curaçao, though most of its torpedoes failed, while U-502 sank three shallow-draft tankers serving Lake Maracaibo, and surviving crews briefly refused to sail. On the night of 18–19 February U-161 entered Trinidad's Gulf of Paria and torpedoed British Consul and Mokihana at anchor, then on 10 March hit Lady Nelson and Umtata alongside at Castries, St Lucia, while U-129 sank bauxite freighters south-east of Trinidad. Vichy French warships at Martinique, including the carrier Béarn, were immobilised under US pressure in May 1942; the island joined the Free French in July 1943. From August 1942 the GAT convoys linked Guantánamo, Curaçao, Aruba and Trinidad. In 1943 US Mariners sank U-159 south-east of Haiti on 28 July, and U-615 north of Margarita Island on 7 August after the largest air hunt ever mounted for one U-boat.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Neuland","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_aircraft_carrier_Béarn","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_submarine_U-615","https://uboat.net/boats/u159.htm"]},77:{name:"Western tropical Atlantic",area:"Open ocean east of the Lesser Antilles and north of Brazil.",lat:"3°N to 23°N",lon:"53°W to 33°W",borders:[73,74,76,78,104],history:"No surface battles were fought in this broad belt of tropical Atlantic east of the Lesser Antilles, but it carried merchant ships to and from the Caribbean and Brazil, and U-boats and Japanese submarines crossed it on long-distance missions. On 27 June 1942 U-153 sank the unescorted American freighter Potlatch about 650 miles east of the Virgin Islands; her 47 survivors spent 26 days in one lifeboat before reaching the Bahamas. In July 1943 US Navy Catalinas of VP-94 sank U-590 north of Belém on 9 July with all 45 hands, and U-662 off the Amazon estuary on 21 July, leaving three survivors. Escort-carrier groups later hunted long-range boats here. Aircraft from USS Block Island sank U-1059, carrying torpedoes for the Far East, south-west of the Cape Verdes on 19 March 1944, and on 24 June 1944 Bogue's Avengers sank the Japanese submarine I-52, carrying about two tons of gold to Germany, two days after a night rendezvous with U-530; her wreck was found in 1995 at about 5,200 metres. On 30 September 1944 USS Fessenden sank U-1062, returning from Penang, with all hands.",sources:["https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/1856.html","https://uboat.net/boats/u662.htm","https://uboat.net/boats/u1059.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_submarine_I-52_(1942)"]},78:{name:"Central tropical Atlantic",area:"Open mid-ocean water between the Caribbean and the Cape Verde Islands.",lat:"10°N to 29°N",lon:"32°W to 21°W",borders:[48,74,77,79,104,105],history:"This stretch of open mid-ocean lies west of Portugal's neutral Cape Verde Islands and south-west of the Canaries, across the route of convoys sailing north from Freetown to Britain. On the night of 22 September 1941 U-103 attacked convoy SL-87 here, south-west of the Canaries, hitting Niceto de Larrinaga and Edward Blyden with two torpedoes each and sinking both; all 63 men aboard Edward Blyden survived and were landed at Londonderry. In 1944 American escort-carrier groups hunted U-boats in these waters. Aircraft from USS Block Island, firing a Fido homing torpedo, and then the destroyer Corry and destroyer escort Bronstein, sank U-801 west of the Cape Verdes on 17 March 1944, and 47 men were rescued. On 6 May 1944 the destroyer escort Buckley, working with Block Island's aircraft, fought a close-range gun action with U-66 and rammed her; 36 Germans survived. On 2 July 1944 an Avenger from USS Wake Island, using depth charges and a Fido torpedo, sank U-543 with all 58 hands south-west of the Canaries.",sources:["https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/1124.html","https://uboat.net/boats/u801.htm","https://uboat.net/boats/u66.htm","https://uboat.net/boats/u543.htm"]},79:{name:"Canary Islands & Moroccan coast",area:"Off Morocco (Casablanca) and Spanish Sahara, including the Canary Islands.",lat:"20°N to 35°N",lon:"20°W to 7°W",borders:[48,78,80,105],history:"The coast of French Morocco saw this zone's major action. On 8 November 1942, in Operation Torch, American forces landed around Casablanca and fought the Naval Battle of Casablanca: the battleship Massachusetts duelled with the unfinished French battleship Jean Bart, US warships beat back a sortie by the light cruiser Primauguet and French destroyers, and aircraft from USS Ranger attacked the harbour. The French lost Primauguet, four destroyers and several submarines, with 462 killed aboard their ships; the Americans lost 174 killed. After the ceasefire U-boats struck the crowded transport anchorage off the Moroccan coast: U-173 sank the troopship Joseph Hewes on 11 November, and U-130 sank Tasker H. Bliss, Hugh L. Scott and Edward Rutledge on 12 November, killing 74 servicemen. US destroyers sank U-173 off Casablanca on 16 November. Earlier, on 4 October 1941, the British anti-submarine trawler Lady Shirley sank U-111 south-west of Tenerife, and 44 of its crew survived. In January 1943 U-522 sank the tanker British Dominion of convoy TM-1 north-west of the Canaries. On 4 June 1944, off the West African coast, the USS Guadalcanal group captured U-505 intact; it is now displayed in Chicago.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naval_Battle_of_Casablanca","https://uboat.net/boats/u111.htm","https://uboat.net/boats/u505.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoy_TM_1"]},80:{name:"Western Mediterranean (south) & Sicilian Channel",area:"Southern western Med: Alboran Sea, Algerian coast (Oran, Algiers), Tunisia, the Sicilian Channel, Malta and the coast of Tripolitania.",lat:"35°N to 39°N",lon:"5°W to 15°E",borders:[49,50,51,79,81],history:"The southern western Mediterranean was the crossroads of the naval war around Malta. Besieged from June 1940 until November 1942, the island was bombed relentlessly; the carrier HMS Illustrious, crippled by German dive-bombers on 10 January 1941, was patched up in Grand Harbour. With Royal Navy submarines, Malta-based aircraft sank 108 Axis ships between June and September 1941, and in late 1941 the cruiser group Force K joined them. In Operation Pedestal (August 1942) U-73 sank the carrier HMS Eagle on 11 August, the cruisers Manchester and Cairo were lost, and only five of fourteen merchantmen arrived, the tanker Ohio being towed into Grand Harbour on 15 August. On 13 November 1941 U-81 torpedoed HMS Ark Royal east of Gibraltar; she sank next day with one man lost. Off Cape Bon on 13 December 1941 four destroyers led by HMS Sikh sank the Italian cruisers Alberico da Barbiano and Alberto di Giussano, killing over 800. On 8 November 1942 Operation Torch landed troops at Oran and Algiers; attempts to rush both harbours cost the sloops Walney and Hartland and the destroyer Broke, and over two days the Vichy French warships that sortied from Oran were sunk or driven ashore.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siege_of_Malta_(World_War_II)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Pedestal","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Cape_Bon_(1941)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Torch"]},81:{name:"Eastern Mediterranean (south)",area:"From the Gulf of Sirte past Cyrenaica (Benghazi, Tobruk) to Egypt (Alexandria, Port Said) and the Levant coast.",lat:"31°N to 36°N",lon:"15°E to 34°E",borders:[51,52,53,80,82],history:`The waters between the Gulf of Sirte and Alexandria were the Mediterranean Fleet's main battleground in the Desert War. From April 1941, during the siege of Tobruk, destroyers and small craft of the "Tobruk Ferry" ran men and supplies in by night under constant air attack; Admiral Cunningham reported 27 naval and seven merchant ships sunk, among them HMAS Waterhen (June 1941) and HMAS Parramatta, torpedoed by U-559 off Tobruk on 27 November 1941 with only 24 survivors. Two days earlier U-331 sank the battleship HMS Barham off the Egyptian coast; her magazine exploded and 862 men died. On the night of 18/19 December 1941 Italian frogmen of Decima MAS, launched from the submarine Scirè, crippled the battleships Queen Elizabeth and Valiant inside Alexandria harbour. In the Second Battle of Sirte on 22 March 1942 Philip Vian's cruisers and destroyers held off Admiral Iachino's squadron, which included the battleship Littorio, to shield Malta convoy MW 10, though air attack later destroyed most of its cargo. The disastrous raid on Tobruk, Operation Agreement (13–14 September 1942), cost the destroyers Sikh and Zulu and the cruiser Coventry.`,sources:["https://navy.gov.au/about-navy/history/history-milestones/tobruk-ferry-service","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMS_Barham_(04)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raid_on_Alexandria_(1941)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Battle_of_Sirte"]},82:{name:"Red Sea",area:"The Red Sea from Suez to Bab-el-Mandeb: Egypt, Sudan, Eritrea (Massawa) and western Arabia.",lat:"11°N to 26°N",lon:"35°E to 44°E",borders:[81,83],history:"The Red Sea was the Italian navy's isolated East African front. From Massawa the Red Sea Flotilla of seven destroyers, eight submarines and five MAS boats threatened British convoys to Suez but achieved little. The submarine Macallè was wrecked on 15 June 1940 after a toxic gas leak, and on 23 June Torricelli was sunk near Perim by HMS Kandahar, Kingston, Khartoum and the sloop Shoreham; Khartoum herself was lost the same day to an internal torpedo explosion. On 21 October 1940 the destroyer Francesco Nullo, attacking a convoy, was chased by HMS Leander and hit by HMS Kimberley, and was destroyed off Harmil Island. As British forces closed on Massawa, the raider Ramb I and the German freighter Coburg slipped out in February 1941; a last destroyer sortie against Port Sudan on 2–3 April ended with Sauro and Manin sunk by Swordfish from HMS Eagle and the rest scuttled. Massawa fell on 8 April after more than thirty vessels were scuttled to block its harbour, while four submarines escaped to Bordeaux. On 6 October 1941 a German He 111 bomber sank the freighter SS Thistlegorm at anchor in the northern Red Sea near Ras Muhammad, killing nine.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Sea_Flotilla","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMS_Khartoum_(F45)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Thistlegorm"]},83:{name:"Gulf of Aden",area:"Gulf of Aden and western Arabian Sea: Aden, British Somaliland, Italian Somaliland's north and Socotra.",lat:"9°N to 16°N",lon:"45°E to 59°E",borders:[82,84,85,119],history:"The Gulf of Aden guarded the southern entrance to the Red Sea and the British base at Aden. In the first days of Italy's war the submarine Galileo Galilei sank the Norwegian tanker James Stove about 12 miles south of Aden on 16 June 1940; on 19 June the armed trawler HMS Moonstone fought her on the surface, killing her commander, and she surrendered and was towed into Aden. In August 1940 Italian forces overran British Somaliland, and the Royal Navy lifted 7,140 troops and civilians out of Berbera by 18 August, the cruiser HMAS Hobart sailing last for Aden; on 17 August the guns of HMS Ceres had checked an Italian column at Bulhar. On 16 March 1941 Operation Appearance landed the Aden Striking Force at Berbera from the cruisers Glasgow and Caledon and smaller vessels, recapturing the colony against little resistance. Japanese submarines later hunted the approaches: I-29 sank the Gazcon, Haresfield and Ocean Honour at the mouth of the gulf in September 1942, and I-27 sank the Sambo, Athina Livanos and Nitsa there in November and December 1943.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_submarine_Galileo_Galilei","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_conquest_of_British_Somaliland","https://ww2db.com/event/timeline/place/Indian-Ocean/Gulf-of-Aden_","https://navyhistory.au/the-war-cruises-of-i27-part-1/5/"]},84:{name:"Persian Gulf, Gulf of Oman & northern Arabian Sea",area:"Persian Gulf (Abadan, Bushire, Bahrain), Gulf of Oman and the northern Arabian Sea off Baluchistan and Karachi.",lat:"20°N to 30°N",lon:"48°E to 70°E",borders:[83,85],history:"The Persian Gulf mattered for the oil of Abadan and, after 1941, as the sea gate of the Persian Corridor supply route to the Soviet Union. On 24 June 1940 the sloop HMS Falmouth sank the Italian submarine Galvani in the Gulf of Oman; 26 of her 57 crew died. On 25 August 1941, opening the Anglo-Soviet invasion of Iran, a British, Indian and Australian naval force under Commodore Cosmo Graham struck at dawn: HMS Shoreham sank the Iranian sloop Palang at Abadan, HMAS Yarra sank the sloop Babr at Khorramshahr, where Iran's naval chief Gholamali Bayandor was killed, and the armed merchant cruiser Kanimbla landed troops at Bandar Shahpur, where seven Axis merchant ships were seized and an eighth scuttled. Thereafter Allied freighters unloaded millions of tons of Lend-Lease cargo at Bandar Shahpur, Bushehr and Khorramshahr for overland shipment to the USSR. German Monsun U-boats reached these waters in 1943, and U-533 was sunk in the Gulf of Oman off Khor Fakkan on 16 October 1943 by a Bisley bomber of No. 244 Squadron RAF, with only one survivor.",sources:["https://regiamarina.net/r-smg-galvani/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anglo-Soviet_invasion_of_Iran","https://uboat.net/boats/u533.htm"]},85:{name:"Arabian Sea (south) & west India",area:"Off India's west coast (Bombay), the Laccadives and Maldives.",lat:"2°N to 20°N",lon:"60°E to 77°E",borders:[83,84,86,119,120],history:"The waters off India's west coast carried the convoys linking Bombay, Colombo, Aden and the Gulf, and both Japanese and German submarines hunted them. Japanese boats were active from early 1942; I-6, patrolling the Arabian Sea west of Bombay, sank the Clan Ross on 2 April and the Bahadur on 7 April 1942. German Monsun U-boats operating from Penang followed from late 1943: U-532 sank the Jalabala of convoy MB-50 in the Laccadive Sea west of Cape Comorin on 11 October 1943. The worst loss came on 12 February 1944, when I-27 torpedoed the troopship Khedive Ismail of convoy KR 8 in the One and a Half Degree Channel of the Maldives; 1,297 people died, including 77 women, and the destroyer HMS Petard, with HMS Paladin, then sank I-27. On 14 April 1944 the freighter Fort Stikine, carrying about 1,395 tons of explosives, caught fire and blew up in Bombay's Victoria Dock. Estimates of the dead range from about 800 to 1,300; thirteen ships were lost and some 80,000 people were left homeless.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_submarine_I-6","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Jalabala_(1927)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Khedive_Ismail","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1944_Bombay_explosion"]},86:{name:"Indian Ocean south of Ceylon",area:"South of Ceylon toward northern Sumatra (Addu Atoll lies near its western edge).",lat:"0°N to 8°N",lon:"73°E to 97°E",borders:[85,87,120,121,122],history:"South of Ceylon lay the Eastern Fleet's sea routes and its secret fuelling anchorage, Port T at Addu Atoll. When Japanese carriers raided Ceylon on Easter Sunday, 5 April 1942, their aircraft struck Colombo harbour, sinking the destroyer HMS Tenedos and wrecking the armed merchant cruiser Hector; that afternoon dive-bombers caught the heavy cruisers HMS Dorsetshire and Cornwall about 200 miles south-west of Ceylon and sank both, with 424 men lost. Admiral Somerville's fleet withdrew to refuel at Addu Atoll, arriving on 8 April. Later the Allies carried the war east toward northern Sumatra: aircraft from HMS Illustrious and USS Saratoga struck Sabang in Operation Cockpit on 19 April 1944, and on 25 July 1944, in Operation Crimson, the battleships Queen Elizabeth, Valiant, Renown and Richelieu bombarded Sabang's harbour. Just beyond, in the adjacent Malacca Strait, the submarine HMS Tally-Ho sank the Japanese cruiser Kuma off Penang on 11 January 1944, and early on 16 May 1945 five destroyers of the 26th Flotilla, including HMS Saumarez, sank the heavy cruiser Haguro about 45 miles south-west of Penang; estimates of Japanese dead range from about 750 to over 900.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_Ocean_raid","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Crimson","https://navalairhistory.com/2012/06/10/three-raids-at-the-beginning-of-the-end/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Malacca_Strait"]},87:{name:"Bay of Bengal",area:"Bay of Bengal: eastern India (Madras, Calcutta), Ceylon's east coast (Trincomalee), Burma and the Andamans.",lat:"5°N to 23°N",lon:"80°E to 98°E",borders:[86,122],history:"The Bay of Bengal was briefly a Japanese sea in the spring of 1942. Japanese forces landed unopposed at Port Blair on 23 March 1942, seizing the Andaman Islands and with them control of the Andaman Sea and the sea route to Burma. In early April Vice-Admiral Ozawa's Malaya Force, built around the light carrier Ryūjō, swept the shipping lanes off eastern India, sinking some 20 merchant ships in three days, most of them on 6 April. On 9 April Japanese carrier aircraft bombed the naval base at Trincomalee, damaging the monitor Erebus, then caught the carrier HMS Hermes, sailing without her aircraft, and the destroyer HMAS Vampire off Batticaloa on Ceylon's east coast. Hermes sank with 307 men and Vampire with eight; the corvette Hollyhock and several merchant ships were also lost. The tide turned along the Burmese coast in 1945: British forces landed at Akyab on 3 January and, after a bombardment by the battleship Queen Elizabeth, on Ramree Island on 21 January. On 1–2 May, in Operation Dracula, paratroops and then the 26th Indian Division landed at the mouth of the Rangoon River and occupied Rangoon, which the Japanese had already abandoned.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_Ocean_raid","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMS_Hermes_(95)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Ramree_Island","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Dracula"]},88:{name:"South China Sea (west) & Gulf of Siam",area:"Off French Indochina, Hainan and the Gulf of Siam (Thailand).",lat:"5°N to 23°N",lon:"100°E to 117°E",borders:[89,90,122,123],history:"The western South China Sea was Japan's springboard to the south. Japanese naval forces landed on Hainan on 9–11 February 1939, making Samah (Sanya) a base for operations against south China and French Indochina. On 17 January 1941, in the Franco-Thai War, the French cruiser Lamotte-Picquet and four avisos surprised the Royal Thai Navy at Ko Chang in the Gulf of Siam, sinking the torpedo boats Chonburi and Songkhla and leaving the coastal defence ship Thonburi burning and aground. At about 00:30 on 8 December 1941, before the Pearl Harbor attack began, Japanese troops landed at Kota Bharu in Malaya, with others at Pattani and Songkhla in Thailand; RAAF Hudsons left the transport Awazisan Maru burning. Force Z, the battleship Prince of Wales and the battlecruiser Repulse, sailed north to strike the invasion convoys but was sunk by Japanese aircraft on 10 December just to the south, off Kuantan, with 840 men lost. On 12 January 1945 the US Third Fleet's carriers raided the coast of Indochina, sinking 46 ships, including the cruiser Kashii and some 142,000 tons of merchant shipping.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Ko_Chang","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Kota_Bharu","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinking_of_Prince_of_Wales_and_Repulse","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_China_Sea_raid"]},89:{name:"Taiwan Strait & South China coast",area:"Formosa (Taiwan), the Taiwan Strait and the south China coast (Amoy, Hong Kong).",lat:"20°N to 28°N",lon:"117°E to 126°E",borders:[54,55,88,90,91],history:"The waters around Formosa (Taiwan) and the south China coast were in Japanese hands for almost the whole war. On 10–12 May 1938 the Imperial Japanese Navy's Fifth Fleet bombarded Amoy (Xiamen) and landed more than 2,000 troops, seizing the island to tighten the blockade of China. Japan attacked Hong Kong on 8 December 1941; the destroyers HMS Thanet and Scout slipped out for Singapore on 9 December, motor torpedo boats attacked Japanese troop boats crossing the harbour on 19 December, and the colony surrendered on Christmas Day. Later the Taiwan Strait became a hunting ground for American submarines. USS Tang, under Richard O'Kane, attacked convoys on 23–24 October 1944 near Turnabout Island before her own last torpedo circled back and sank her early on 25 October; 78 men died and the few survivors were captured. From 12 to 16 October 1944 Task Force 38 fought the Formosa air battle, destroying more than 300 Japanese aircraft, though the cruisers Canberra and Houston were torpedoed. In January 1945 Halsey's Third Fleet raided Takao, the Pescadores and Hong Kong, where on 16 January five large tankers and a naval oiler were sunk.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amoy_Operation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Hong_Kong","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Tang_(SS-306)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_China_Sea_raid"]},90:{name:"Philippines",area:"The Philippine archipelago and inner seas (Sibuyan, Sulu, Mindanao).",lat:"4°N to 19°N",lon:"118°E to 129°E",borders:[88,89,91,96,123,124],history:"The Philippines saw both an early Allied disaster and the decisive naval battle of the Pacific war. Japan's 14th Army landed in Lingayen Gulf on 22 December 1941. Corregidor, the island fortress guarding Manila Bay, fell after Japanese troops landed on the night of 5–6 May 1942; the submarine Spearfish had made the last evacuation run on 4 May. The Battle of Leyte Gulf, 23–26 October 1944, was fought largely in these inner seas. In the Palawan Passage the submarines Darter and Dace sank the heavy cruisers Atago and Maya. On 24 October carrier aircraft sank the giant battleship Musashi in the Sibuyan Sea, while a bomb hit doomed the light carrier USS Princeton. That night in Surigao Strait American battleships and destroyers destroyed Nishimura's force, sinking Fuso and Yamashiro. Off Samar on 25 October the escort carriers and destroyers of Taffy 3 held off Kurita's battleships, losing Gambier Bay, Hoel, Johnston and Samuel B. Roberts, and the first organised kamikaze attacks struck the escort carriers, sinking St. Lo. The US Sixth Army landed at Lingayen Gulf on 9 January 1945 while kamikazes hit many ships offshore.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Leyte_Gulf","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Corregidor","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Invasion_of_Lingayen_Gulf"]},91:{name:"Philippine Sea & Mariana Islands (Guam)",area:"East of Luzon; its island most likely stands for the Marianas (Guam, Saipan, Tinian).",lat:"11°N to 22°N",lon:"127°E to 148°E",borders:[55,65,89,90,93,96],history:"The Mariana Islands and the Philippine Sea saw the largest carrier battle in history. Guam, an American island surrounded by Japan's mandated Marianas, was bombed from 8 December 1941, when the minesweeper USS Penguin was lost, and surrendered on 10 December after Japanese troops came ashore that morning. The United States returned in June 1944. Marines landed on Saipan on 15 June, and Admiral Ozawa's Mobile Fleet came out to strike Spruance's Fifth Fleet. In the Battle of the Philippine Sea on 19–20 June, American fighters destroyed hundreds of Japanese aircraft in the 'Great Marianas Turkey Shoot', the submarines Albacore and Cavalla sank the carriers Taiho and Shokaku, and carrier aircraft sank Hiyo, though many returning American planes ran out of fuel in the dark. Saipan was secured by 9 July, Tinian (landed 24 July) by 1 August and Guam (landed 21 July) by 10 August, and their airfields became B-29 bases for the bombing of Japan. On 26 July 1945 USS Indianapolis delivered atomic bomb components to Tinian; four days later, bound from Guam for Leyte, she was torpedoed by I-58 and sank in about twelve minutes. Of 1,195 men aboard, only 316 survived.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Philippine_Sea","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Guam_(1941)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mariana_Islands_campaign","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Indianapolis_(CA-35)"]},92:{name:"Western Pacific north of the Marshalls",area:"Open ocean between the Marianas/Wake and the Marshall Islands.",lat:"8°N to 18°N",lon:"146°E to 176°E",borders:[65,66,93,94],history:"No major naval battles are recorded in this stretch of open ocean between the Marianas, Wake Island and the northern Marshalls. It is deep tropical water with only scattered atolls, swept by the north-east trade winds and lying in the western Pacific's typhoon belt. Until 1944 it lay inside Japan's defensive perimeter, between its bases in the Marianas, Wake and the Marshalls. The notable fighting happened on its edges. Just to the north, Wake Island's Marines repulsed a Japanese landing attempt on 11 December 1941, sinking the destroyers Hayate and Kisaragi, before the island fell on 23 December; American carriers began raiding Wake on 24 February 1942, and Japan held it until September 1945. On its south-eastern edge, Eniwetok in the northern Marshalls was captured on 17–23 February 1944 and became a forward naval base for attacks on the Marianas to the north-west. In early June 1944 Task Force 58 sailed from Majuro and an invasion fleet of more than 500 ships converged on Saipan, much of the route from the Marshalls crossing these waters to the landings of 15 June. After that the zone became a quiet rear area behind the advancing American front.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Wake_Island","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Eniwetok","https://warfarehistorynetwork.com/?p=21307"]},93:{name:"Western Pacific (north of the Carolines)",area:"Open ocean north of the Carolines and west of the Marshalls.",lat:"3°N to 14°N",lon:"144°E to 176°E",borders:[91,92,94,99,100],history:"Most of this zone is open ocean north of the Caroline Islands and west of the Marshalls, and no fleet battle was fought in it. Its importance came from Truk, Japan's main fleet anchorage in the central Carolines just to the south, and the shipping lanes running from there north to Saipan and Japan and east to the Marshalls. American submarines hunted along these approaches. On 19 November 1943, patrolling north of Truk to intercept Japanese forces reacting to the Tarawa invasion, USS Sculpin was crippled by the destroyer Yamagumo and scuttled at about 8°40′N 155°E. Captain John Cromwell, aboard as wolf-pack commander, chose to go down with the boat rather than risk revealing invasion plans under interrogation, and received a posthumous Medal of Honor; 42 of her crew were picked up, and twenty of them later died when the carrier Chuyo, carrying them to Japan, was torpedoed. In December 1943 USS Skate sank the cargo ship Terukawa Maru north of Truk, and on Christmas Day, about 180 miles north-east of Truk, she torpedoed the battleship Yamato, which was ferrying troops to Truk. Otherwise these were deep waters crossed mainly by Japanese convoys and patrolling submarines.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Sculpin_(SS-191)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Skate_(SS-305)","http://www.combinedfleet.com/yamato.htm"]},94:{name:"Central Pacific (south-west of Hawaii)",area:"Open ocean south-west of Hawaii, north-east of the Marshalls.",lat:"3°N to 14°N",lon:"178°E to 168°W",borders:[67,92,93,95,100],history:"No major naval actions are recorded in this empty stretch of the central Pacific between Hawaii and the Marshall and Gilbert Islands, and no notable sinkings inside it have been identified. It is deep ocean with almost no land, lying mostly in the belt of north-east trade winds, with the calmer equatorial doldrums toward its southern part. Its wartime importance was as a highway: the direct routes from Pearl Harbor to the Gilberts and Marshalls ran across it. On 1 February 1942 American carrier task forces under Halsey and Fletcher raided Japanese bases in the Marshalls and Gilberts, damaging the cruiser Katori and sinking three small warships, before withdrawing at once. In November 1943 the Makin invasion force, Task Force 52, left Pearl Harbor on 10 November for the Gilberts, where landings on Makin and Tarawa began on 20 November. The Majuro and Kwajalein landings in the Marshalls followed on 31 January 1944. Once those islands were taken, the front moved far to the west and this zone became a quiet rear area crossed by convoys, tankers and aircraft supporting the American advance across the central Pacific.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marshalls–Gilberts_raids","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Makin","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gilbert_and_Marshall_Islands_campaign"]},95:{name:"Central Pacific south of Hawaii",area:"Open ocean south/south-west of Hawaii; its small island probably stands for Johnston Atoll or Palmyra.",lat:"0°N to 16°N",lon:"172°W to 152°W",borders:[68,94,98,100,101,129],history:"No major naval battles were fought in these central Pacific waters south of Hawaii, but its scattered islands were garrisoned outposts on the air and sea routes from Hawaii to the South Pacific. On 7 December 1941 the cruiser USS Indianapolis was off Johnston Atoll, at the zone's north-western edge, conducting a practice bombardment when Pearl Harbor was attacked. A Japanese submarine shelled Johnston on 15 December, hitting the power station without casualties, and returned on 22 and 23 December, when coastal guns drove it off. On 24 December the submarine I-75 fired twelve rounds at Palmyra Atoll, hitting the Army engineers' dredge Sacramento in the lagoon before a 5-inch battery forced it to submerge and withdraw. Palmyra, a naval air station since August 1941, became a refuelling stop for transpacific aircraft and hosted six B-17s in January 1942. Christmas Island (Kiritimati) was garrisoned under American control and given its first airstrip, giving aircraft flying between Hawaii and the South Pacific a place to refuel and protecting the Hawaii–Australia supply route. By 1944 Johnston was one of the busiest air transport terminals in the Pacific, a stop for bombers including the B-29 Enola Gay.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johnston_Atoll","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palmyra_Atoll","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiritimati"]},96:{name:"Palau & western Carolines",area:"Palau, Yap and Ulithi, east of Mindanao.",lat:"4°N to 13°N",lon:"130°E to 142°E",borders:[90,91,99,124,125,126],history:"Palau and the western Carolines were Japanese mandated islands and an important forward base until 1944. On 30–31 March 1944, in Operation Desecrate One, eleven American carriers struck Palau, sinking or damaging 36 ships, among them the repair ship Akashi, while Avengers sowed mines in the harbour approaches, the first tactical use of carrier-laid mines in the Pacific. On 15 September 1944 US Marines landed on Peleliu to secure the flank of the Philippines invasion; the battle dragged on until 27 November and cost about 1,600 American and over 10,000 Japanese dead, and neighbouring Angaur was also taken. Ulithi Atoll was occupied unopposed on 23 September, and its vast lagoon became one of the war's greatest fleet anchorages, holding more than 700 ships at its peak as forces staged for Okinawa. Japan struck back. On 20 November 1944 a kaiten human torpedo, probably launched by the submarine I-47, sank the nearly fully loaded oiler USS Mississinewa at anchor, killing 63 of her crew. On 11 March 1945, in Operation Tan No. 2, a long-range kamikaze flown from southern Japan hit the carrier USS Randolph at Ulithi, killing a number of crewmen.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Desecrate_One","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Peleliu","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ulithi","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Mississinewa_(AO-59)"]},97:{name:"Eastern Pacific off southern Mexico",area:"Off southern Mexico and Central America's Pacific coast (Clipperton Island area).",lat:"3°N to 16°N",lon:"119°W to 96°W",borders:[70,98,102,103],history:"No major naval battles are recorded in this stretch of the eastern Pacific off southern Mexico and Central America. Its one well-documented wartime story is Clipperton Island, a remote French-owned atoll. After American warships confirmed in 1944 that it was uninhabited, USS Argus left San Francisco on 4 December 1944 with weather specialists, followed by USS LST-563 carrying equipment for a US Navy weather station. Landing proved difficult: LST-563 grounded on the reef on 21 December, the salvage ship USS Seize also grounded trying to free her, and the LST was stripped and abandoned in January 1945. About two dozen armed sailors then ran the station, launching weather balloons daily, while France protested; in February 1945 the US Navy carried a French officer, Lieutenant Louis Jampierre, out to inspect it. The last Americans left on 21 October 1945. Mexico entered the war against the Axis in mid-1942 after U-boats sank its tankers in the Gulf of Mexico, and took steps to strengthen surveillance of its Pacific coast, but no enemy warships are known to have fought here. Waters east of 100°W fell within the US Southeast Pacific Area command, headquartered at Balboa, Panama.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clipperton_Island","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico_in_World_War_II","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southeast_Pacific_Area"]},98:{name:"Eastern Pacific (south-east of Hawaii)",area:"Open ocean south-east of Hawaii.",lat:"3°N to 15°N",lon:"146°W to 121°W",borders:[68,69,70,95,97,101],history:"No major naval actions are recorded in this stretch of open ocean south-east of Hawaii, and no well-documented wartime sinkings could be confirmed within it. It is a remote expanse of deep water between the Hawaiian Islands and the Pacific coasts of Mexico and Central America, lying just north of the equator in the tropical trade-wind belt, with no islands of note and no wartime bases. Throughout the war it lay well behind the American front. Admiral Nimitz's Pacific Ocean Areas command controlled the waters around Hawaii, while the Southeast Pacific Area, headquartered at Balboa in Panama, was responsible for the approaches to the Americas east of 100°W, with cruisers such as USS Concord, Trenton and Detroit tasked with destroying any surface raiders that threatened American shipping. Japanese submarines shelled and reconnoitred the US West Coast, and in 1945 Japan planned, then abandoned, an air strike on the Panama Canal launched from I-400-class submarines; none of these operations is known to have brought fighting into this zone. It is best regarded as quiet rear-area ocean crossed occasionally by Allied ships.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southeast_Pacific_Area","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_submarines_in_the_Pacific_War","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I-400-class_submarine"]},99:{name:"Caroline Islands (Truk)",area:"The central/eastern Carolines - Truk (Chuuk), Ponape - north of New Guinea.",lat:"1°N to 10°N",lon:"143°E to 163°E",borders:[93,96,100,126,127],history:"The central Carolines were dominated by Truk (Chuuk) Lagoon, Japan's main base for operations against the Allies in New Guinea and the Solomons, often called the 'Japanese Pearl Harbor'; the giant battleships Yamato and Musashi lay there for months in 1943. American submarines hunted its approaches: on 25 December 1943 USS Skate torpedoed Yamato as she steamed towards Truk, letting in some 3,000 tons of water. Sensing danger, the Japanese began withdrawing heavy units in October 1943, accelerating in early February 1944. On 16 February Skate sank the light cruiser Agano, damaged earlier by USS Scamp, about 160 nautical miles north of Truk, and on 17–18 February 1944 Vice Admiral Marc Mitscher's fast carriers struck in Operation Hailstone. Aircraft and surface ships sank the cruiser Katori, the destroyer Maikaze, the ammunition ship Aikoku Maru (945 killed) and some 32 merchant ships, destroying around 250 aircraft; a night torpedo hit damaged the carrier USS Intrepid. Carrier aircraft returned on 29 April 1944, and British Pacific Fleet forces raided Truk in June 1945 (Operation Inmate). Bypassed and cut off, the garrison faced starvation until Japan's surrender in August 1945.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Hailstone","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuuk_Lagoon","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Skate_(SS-305)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_cruiser_Agano"]},100:{name:"Marshall Islands",area:"Island cluster east of the Carolines - most likely the Marshall Islands (Kwajalein, Eniwetok, Majuro).",lat:"4°N to 14°N",lon:"160°E to 173°E",borders:[93,94,95,99,127,128,129],history:"The Japanese-mandated Marshall Islands were the target of the first American carrier offensive of the war. On 1 February 1942 aircraft from USS Enterprise struck Kwajalein, Wotje and Taroa while cruisers and destroyers shelled Wotje and Taroa, and USS Yorktown's planes hit Jaluit and Mili; three small Japanese warships were sunk and the cruiser Katori damaged, while the heavy cruiser USS Chester took a bomb hit. Two years later the islands were seized in Operation Flintlock. Majuro was occupied without casualties on 31 January 1944 and became an advanced air and naval base, while between 31 January and 3 February the 4th Marine Division took Roi-Namur and the Army's 7th Infantry Division captured Kwajalein Island. Some 7,800 Japanese defenders were killed against about 350 Americans; at Roi-Namur a satchel charge detonated a torpedo-warhead magazine, killing 20 Marines. Success at Kwajalein let Admiral Nimitz bring forward Operation Catchpole: on 17–23 February 1944 Marines and soldiers captured Engebi, Eniwetok and Parry islands in Eniwetok Atoll, killing about 3,400 Japanese. Eniwetok then became a forward base for the US Navy's later advances across the Pacific.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marshalls%E2%80%93Gilberts_raids","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Kwajalein","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Eniwetok"]},101:{name:"Equatorial Pacific (east-central)",area:"Open ocean on the equator south of Hawaii and east of the Line Islands.",lat:"7°S to 2°N",lon:"145°W to 120°W",borders:[95,98,102,129,130],history:"No major naval actions are recorded in this stretch of the equatorial Pacific south of Hawaii and east of the Line Islands, and no well-documented wartime sinkings could be confirmed within it. It is deep, open ocean straddling the equator, far from significant land: the Line Islands lie to the west and the Marquesas, in French Polynesia, lie just beyond its southern edge. It sits within the tropical trade-wind belt and the westward-flowing equatorial currents. During the war it was a remote Allied rear area. The United States held the waters to the north around Hawaii, and to the south-west it sent Operation Bobcat, an expedition of nine ships and nearly 7,000 soldiers, to build an oil depot, airstrip, seaplane base and South Pacific supply base at Bora Bora in the Society Islands. Allied vessels moving between the Americas and the South Pacific crossed this broad region of ocean, but Japanese surface raiders and submarines are not known to have operated or fought here, and Japan's planned air attack on the Panama Canal from I-400-class submarines was abandoned in 1945. The zone is best treated as quiet open ocean.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Bobcat","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_submarines_in_the_Pacific_War","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I-400-class_submarine"]},102:{name:"Equatorial Pacific (east)",area:"Open ocean on the equator west of the Galápagos.",lat:"8°S to 2°N",lon:"118°W to 95°W",borders:[97,101,103,106,131],history:"No major naval actions are recorded in this stretch of open equatorial ocean west of the Galápagos Islands, and no well-documented wartime sinkings could be confirmed within it. It is deep, empty ocean straddling the equator, hundreds of kilometres from the nearest land, swept westward by the South Equatorial Current. Its strategic interest came from what lay to the east. The Pacific approaches to the Panama Canal were guarded by the US Southeast Pacific Area command, headquartered at Balboa, whose operating area extended west to 100°W and so took in the eastern part of this zone; its light cruisers were tasked with destroying surface raiders that threatened American shipping. From April 1942 the US Army Air Forces' Sixth Air Force operated Seymour Island Airfield on Baltra in the Galápagos, flying P-40 Warhawks and B-24 Liberators to guard the South American coast and the canal against Japanese submarines. That threat never materialised: Japan's plan to bomb the canal's Gatun locks with Seiran aircraft launched from I-400-class submarines was cancelled in mid-1945 in favour of an attack on Ulithi, which itself never took place. The zone therefore remained a quiet patrol and transit area throughout the war.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southeast_Pacific_Area","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seymour_Airport","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I-400-class_submarine"]},103:{name:"Pacific off Panama, Colombia & Ecuador",area:"Pacific approaches to the Panama Canal, the Gulf of Panama, the Colombian/Ecuadorian coast and the Galápagos.",lat:"4°S to 14°N",lon:"94°W to 79°W",borders:[75,97,102,106],history:"These Pacific approaches to the Panama Canal were among the most closely guarded waters in the Americas, though they saw little combat. The US Southeast Pacific Area command, headquartered at Balboa at the canal's Pacific entrance, kept light cruisers such as USS Concord, Trenton and Detroit ready to hunt surface raiders threatening American shipping. From 1942 the Sixth Air Force flew P-40s and B-24 Liberators from Seymour Island Airfield on Baltra in the Galápagos, with a detachment at Salinas in Ecuador in 1942–43, to protect the canal and the South American coast against Japanese submarines. The threat proved hypothetical: Japan's plan to strike the canal's Gatun locks with Seiran bombers carried by I-400-class submarines was cancelled in mid-1945. The zone's only real naval clash came before Pearl Harbor, in the Ecuadorian–Peruvian War. On 25 July 1941 the Peruvian destroyer Almirante Villar exchanged fire for about 21 minutes with the Ecuadorian gunboat Abdón Calderón near the Jambelí Channel in the Gulf of Guayaquil, without decisive result. Peruvian submarines then scouted the channel, Peruvian warships including the cruiser Coronel Bolognesi bombarded Punta Jambelí and Puerto Bolívar on 29 July, and by late August Peru was blockading Guayaquil, Ecuador's main port.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southeast_Pacific_Area","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seymour_Airport","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I-400-class_submarine","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecuadorian%E2%80%93Peruvian_War"]},104:{name:"Off north-east Brazil",area:"Off Brazil's north-east bulge (Natal, Recife) and the equatorial Atlantic (St Peter and St Paul Rocks, Fernando de Noronha).",lat:"11°S to 5°N",lon:"46°W to 27°W",borders:[76,77,78,105,107],history:"The waters off Brazil's north-eastern bulge became a key Allied anti-submarine battleground. In August 1942 U-507 sank five Brazilian coasters, including Baependy, just to the south off Sergipe and Bahia, with about 600 casualties; Brazil declared a state of belligerency on 22 August. Natal's air base, nicknamed the 'Trampoline of Victory', and the US South Atlantic Force headquarters at Recife, with a support base on Fernando de Noronha, made the region a hub for air patrols. A VP-83 Catalina sank U-164 north-west of Fortaleza on 6 January 1943, and U-507 itself was sunk off Ceará a week later. On 15 April 1943 two Natal-based Catalinas sank the Italian submarine Archimede; a single survivor reached the Amazon coast by raft after 29 days. U-128 was sunk south of Recife on 17 May 1943 by Mariner aircraft and the destroyers Moffett and Jouett, U-598 off Natal on 23 July by Liberators, and U-591 off Recife on 30 July by a Ventura. The zone's worst disaster came after Germany's surrender: on 4 July 1945 the Brazilian cruiser Bahia, serving as a plane guard near St Peter and St Paul Rocks, sank within minutes after a gunnery accident, with over 300 lost.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazil_in_World_War_II","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_submarine_Archimede_(1939)","https://uboat.net/boats/u128.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazilian_cruiser_Bahia"]},105:{name:"Cape Verde & Senegal",area:"Off Senegal (Dakar) and the Cape Verde Islands.",lat:"1°N to 20°N",lon:"26°W to 17°W",borders:[78,79,104,107,108],history:"The Vichy French naval base at Dakar made this zone a flashpoint in 1940. At dawn on 8 July 1940, during Operation Catapult, Swordfish torpedo bombers from HMS Hermes hit the battleship Richelieu in Dakar harbour, flooding her with about 2,400 tons of water. On 23–25 September 1940 a British, Free French and Australian force under Admiral John Cunningham and General Charles de Gaulle tried to bring Dakar over to the Free French in Operation Menace. Governor Pierre Boisson's defences held: the battleship HMS Resolution was torpedoed by the submarine Bévéziers and had to be towed to Cape Town, and HMS Barham was hit by shore batteries, while the Vichy submarines Persée and Ajax were sunk, the destroyer L'Audacieux was set ablaze by HMAS Australia and beached, and Richelieu was hit by British shellfire. Off the neutral Portuguese Cape Verde Islands, Ultra decrypts sent the submarine HMS Clyde to Tarrafal Bay, Santo Antão, where U-68 and U-111 met on 27–28 September 1941; Clyde's torpedoes missed, but U-67 was damaged in a collision. After the Allied landings in North Africa in November 1942, Richelieu came under Free French control and sailed from Dakar for New York on 30 January 1943.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Dakar","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_battleship_Richelieu","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Action_in_Tarrafal_Bay"]},106:{name:"Pacific off Peru & northern Chile",area:"Off Peru and northern Chile (Callao, Antofagasta).",lat:"34°S to 6°S",lon:"94°W to 73°W",borders:[102,103,111,131,139],history:"No major naval battles were fought off Peru and northern Chile, but these waters saw a well-documented episode of the Allied blockade of German shipping. Four German merchant ships, Hermonthis, München, Leipzig and Monserrate, lay at Callao. The Canadian armed merchant cruiser HMCS Prince Henry refuelled there on 24 March 1941 and then waited outside Peruvian territorial waters. When München and Hermonthis put to sea on 1 April 1941, Prince Henry fired a warning shot across München's bow; her crew set her on fire and abandoned ship, and the Peruvian cruiser BAP Almirante Grau later sank the burning hulk by gunfire, while her crew, reaching shore at Casma, were arrested. Hermonthis was found burning and abandoned the same day; a boarding party could not control the fire, and Prince Henry sank her with gunfire on 2 April. Leipzig and Monserrate were scuttled at Callao; Monserrate was later refloated and served the Peruvian Navy as Callao. For the rest of the war the zone saw no recorded fighting. Peru and Chile stayed out of combat, and the nearest Allied military presence was the US Sixth Air Force detachment at Talara, on Peru's northern coast just beyond the zone.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMCS_Prince_Henry","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_shipwrecks_in_April_1941","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seymour_Airport"]},107:{name:"South Atlantic off Brazil & Uruguay",area:"Off south-eastern Brazil (Rio, Santos) and Uruguay/River Plate approaches.",lat:"38°S to 5°S",lon:"53°W to 25°W",borders:[104,105,108,109,112],history:"This zone's defining event was the Battle of the River Plate. On 13 December 1939, at about 34°S 49°W off the estuary, Commodore Henry Harwood's cruisers HMS Exeter, HMS Ajax and HMNZS Achilles engaged the German Panzerschiff Admiral Graf Spee, which had been sinking merchant ships in the South Atlantic and Indian Ocean. The British and New Zealand cruisers lost 72 killed and the Germans 36, but the damaged Graf Spee broke off and made for neutral Montevideo. Allowed only 72 hours there, Captain Hans Langsdorff scuttled her on 17 December and shot himself two days later. A year later, on 5 December 1940, the German raider Thor fought the armed merchant cruiser HMS Carnarvon Castle south-east of Rio de Janeiro, hitting her eight times and forcing her to retreat to Montevideo for repairs with six dead and 32 wounded. After Brazil joined the Allies, US and Brazilian aircraft hunted U-boats off its southern coast. A US Navy Mariner sank U-513 south-east of São Francisco do Sul on 19 July 1943, killing 46, and on 31 July U-199 was sunk east of Rio de Janeiro by a US Mariner and Brazilian Catalina and Hudson aircraft; 12 of her crew survived.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_River_Plate","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_auxiliary_cruiser_Thor","https://uboat.net/boats/u513.htm","https://uboat.net/boats/u199.htm"]},108:{name:"Gulf of Guinea (west)",area:"Off Sierra Leone (Freetown), Liberia and the Gold Coast.",lat:"12°S to 11°N",lon:"24°W to 13°W",borders:[105,107,109,110],history:"Freetown in Sierra Leone was the South Atlantic's key convoy port. From September 1939 merchant ships from South America, Africa and the Indian Ocean gathered in its sheltered anchorage to form the SL convoys to Liverpool, escorted at first by armed merchant cruisers or South Atlantic Station cruisers based there. On 22 November 1941, acting on decrypted signals, the cruiser HMS Devonshire caught the German raider Atlantis refuelling U-126 north of Ascension Island and sank her; U-126 rescued about 300 survivors. Ascension Island, just to the south (zone 109), later became a secret American airbase, and the September 1942 Laconia incident unfolded in the waters around it. On 30 April–1 May 1943, U-515 sank seven ships of convoy TS-37, bound from Takoradi to Freetown, as it neared Freetown.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SL_convoys","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_auxiliary_cruiser_Atlantis","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laconia_incident","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoy_TS_37"]},109:{name:"Central South Atlantic (Ascension)",area:"Mid-ocean South Atlantic around Ascension Island.",lat:"27°S to 5°S",lon:"27°W to 12°W",borders:[107,108,110,112,113,114],history:"The waters around Ascension Island saw one of the war's most notorious incidents. On 12 September 1942 U-156 under Werner Hartenstein torpedoed the liner Laconia about 110 nautical miles north-north-east of the island; she was carrying some 1,800 Italian prisoners, and roughly 1,650 to 1,750 people died. Hartenstein began a rescue, but on 16 September a US B-24 Liberator flying from Ascension attacked U-156 during the rescue, prompting Dönitz's Laconia Order of 17 September forbidding U-boat rescues. Ascension itself became an Allied anti-submarine base after US Army engineers built Wideawake airfield in 1942. Earlier, on 9 December 1941, U-124 approached Georgetown on the surface and withdrew when the Fort Bedford battery opened fire. North of the island, HMS Devonshire sank the German raider Atlantis on 22 November 1941 as she resupplied U-126. US warships also intercepted German blockade runners in these waters: Karin was scuttled on 10 March 1943 as a boarding party from USS Eberle came aboard, and Weserland, Rio Grande and Burgenland were sunk or scuttled in early January 1944 after interception by USS Somers, Omaha and Jouett.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laconia_incident","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ascension_Island","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_auxiliary_cruiser_Atlantis","https://ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-BA-Appendix/Appendix-10.html"]},110:{name:"Gulf of Guinea (east) & Congo coast",area:"Bight of Benin/Biafra (Lagos), Fernando Po, São Tomé, Gabon, the Congo mouth and northern Angola.",lat:"12°S to 3°N",lon:"10°W to 10°E",borders:[108,109,114],history:"The eastern Gulf of Guinea saw Frenchmen fight Frenchmen at sea. In the Battle of Gabon in November 1940, Free French forces with British support moved against Vichy-held Libreville: on 8 November the Vichy submarine Poncelet, damaged by depth charges from HMS Devonshire's Walrus aircraft, was scuttled off Port-Gentil, and on 9 November the Free French aviso Savorgnan de Brazza disabled and set on fire the Vichy sloop Bougainville. Libreville fell on 10 November and Port-Gentil surrendered on 12 November. On 14 January 1942 Operation Postmaster, a raid by SOE and the Small Scale Raiding Force under Gus March-Phillipps, cut out the Italian merchant ship Duchessa d'Aosta, the German tug Likomba and the barge Bibundi from neutral Spanish Santa Isabel on Fernando Po and towed them out with the tugs Vulcan and Nuneaton, bound for Lagos. Lagos served as a convoy port, and U-boats reached these waters in 1943: on 9 July U-508 sank Manchester Citizen of convoy ST-71 about 60 miles south-west of Lagos, killing 29, and on 18 July she sank the passenger ship Incomati some 200 miles south of Lagos, with all but one of 223 aboard rescued.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Gabon","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Postmaster","https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/2996.html","https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/3015.html"]},111:{name:"South-east Pacific off southern Chile",area:"Off southern Chile toward Cape Horn.",lat:"70°S to 36°S",lon:"93°W to 65°W",borders:[106,112,139,149],history:"No major naval battles or sinkings are recorded in this remote stretch of the south-east Pacific off southern Chile and towards Cape Horn. Chile stayed neutral for most of the war; it only declared war on Japan on 13 April 1945, the last country to join the Allies, and took no military action. The best-documented wartime episode here is the escape of the German freighter Erlangen. Sheltering at the sub-Antarctic Auckland Islands south of New Zealand, she left Carnley Harbour on 7 October 1939 with about 240 tonnes of cut wood added to her fuel, and crossed the Southern Ocean to Chile, anchoring off Ancud on 11 December and at Puerto Montt the next day. She later tried to run for Germany and was scuttled off the River Plate in July 1941 when intercepted by HMS Newcastle. Otherwise these waters, swept by the westerlies and among the stormiest on earth, were crossed by shipping passing between the Atlantic and Pacific via the Strait of Magellan and Cape Horn, far from the main naval theatres.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Erlangen_(1929)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chile_during_World_War_II"]},112:{name:"South Atlantic off Argentina & Falklands",area:"Off Argentina, the River Plate estuary's southern side, the Falkland Islands and Drake Passage/Cape Horn.",lat:"69°S to 34°S",lon:"65°W to 31°W",borders:[107,109,111,113],history:"The Battle of the River Plate, the first major naval battle of the war, was fought on 13 December 1939 in the neighbouring waters off the Plate estuary (zone 107), but this zone's Falkland Islands played a direct part in it. HMS Exeter, badly damaged by the German panzerschiff Admiral Graf Spee, withdrew to the Falkland Islands for emergency repairs at Stanley, while the cruiser HMS Cumberland, refitting at the Falklands, sailed to join the squadron, arriving late on 14 December. Later in the war the Falklands were the springboard for Operation Tabarin, a secret British expedition to assert sovereignty over the Falkland Islands Dependencies amid Argentine and Chilean counterclaims and concern that German raiders might shelter in Antarctic anchorages. Its party reached Port Stanley on 26 January 1944, sailed on 29 January aboard William Scoresby and Fitzroy, and set up bases on Deception Island on 3 February and at Port Lockroy on 11 February 1944.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_River_Plate","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Tabarin"]},113:{name:"South Atlantic (Tristan da Cunha)",area:"Open South Atlantic between South America and southern Africa.",lat:"60°S to 22°S",lon:"32°W to 14°W",borders:[109,112,114,115],history:"This open stretch of the South Atlantic was a hunting and replenishment ground for German surface raiders. Its best-known action came on 27 September 1942, at about 24°44'S 21°50'W, when the American Liberty ship Stephen Hopkins, sailing from Cape Town for Surinam, met the raider Stier and her supply ship Tannenfels in poor visibility. Though outgunned, the Liberty ship's crew fought back so effectively that Stier was wrecked and had to be scuttled; Stephen Hopkins also sank, with 42 of her people killed, and 15 survivors drifted for about a month before reaching Brazil. Earlier, Stier had spent some six weeks cruising around Tristan da Cunha and Gough Island looking for shipping, and in August 1942 the raider Michel refuelled from the tanker Charlotte Schliemann east of Tristan da Cunha. The British responded by commissioning remote Tristan da Cunha as the shore station HMS Atlantic Isle, a weather and signals intelligence post monitoring U-boat radio traffic and South Atlantic shipping. Otherwise these waters, crossed by the westerlies, lay off the main routes between South America and the Cape.",sources:["https://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-11.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinking_of_the_SS_Stephen_Hopkins","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tristan_da_Cunha"]},114:{name:"Off Angola & South-West Africa (St Helena)",area:"Off Angola and South-West Africa, including St Helena.",lat:"26°S to 13°S",lon:"11°W to 12°E",borders:[109,110,113,115],history:"The shipping lanes past St Helena and off Angola and South-West Africa saw raiders and U-boats throughout the war. The Admiral Graf Spee sank the British cargo liner Doric Star at about 19°15'S 5°05'E on 2 December 1939 and the Tairoa further south the next day; both crews survived as prisoners. On 22 October 1941 U-68 torpedoed the fleet oiler RFA Darkdale at anchor off Jamestown, St Helena. On 6 November 1942 the same boat sank the liner City of Cairo about 480 miles south of St Helena; 107 of her 302 people died, many in lifeboats that drifted for weeks, one boat for 51 days before reaching Brazil. Later that month, on 29 November 1942, the liner Dunedin Star ran aground on the Skeleton Coast of South-West Africa. All her passengers and crew survived after a long rescue involving the minesweeper HMSAS Nerine, South African Air Force Venturas and an overland police convoy from Windhoek, but the rescue tug Sir Charles Elliot was wrecked and two of her crew died. Throughout, these waters lay on the main route between Britain and the Cape.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_shipwrecks_in_December_1939","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_submarine_U-68_(1940)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_City_of_Cairo","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MV_Dunedin_Star"]},115:{name:"South Atlantic off the Cape",area:"Off South Africa's west coast and the Cape of Good Hope (Cape Town, Saldanha Bay).",lat:"49°S to 26°S",lon:"13°W to 20°E",borders:[113,114,116],history:"The approaches to the Cape of Good Hope, on the vital route round Africa to the Middle East and India, were mined by German raiders and attacked by U-boats. The raider Atlantis laid 92 mines off Cape Agulhas on 10 May 1940, and the raider Doggerbank laid more off South Africa in March and April 1942. On 1 December 1941 the supply ship Python, replenishing U-boats far out in the open South Atlantic, was surprised by HMS Dorsetshire and scuttled; her crew and the survivors of Atlantis aboard were carried home by U-boats and Italian submarines. The heaviest blow came in October 1942, when the first five U-boats of a long-range group reached the waters off Cape Town on 7 October. Finding few targets there, they spread along the coast, and by the end of the month they had sunk 24 ships of 161,121 tons off South Africa. Among them was the liner Orcades, sunk by U-172 on 10 October about 220 nautical miles south-west of Cape Town with the loss of 45 lives. Cape Town remained a key port of call on this route throughout the war.",sources:["https://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-11.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Orcades_(1937)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_auxiliary_cruiser_Atlantis","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_ship_Doggerbank"]},116:{name:"Mozambique Channel & SE Africa",area:"Mozambique Channel and the coast from Tanganyika/Zanzibar past Mozambique to Natal (Durban) and Port Elizabeth.",lat:"42°S to 2°S",lon:"23°E to 49°E",borders:[115,117,118,119],history:"The Mozambique Channel and the coast down to Natal formed the main Allied route to Egypt, India and the Persian Gulf. On 5 May 1942 Operation Ironclad landed British troops at Courrier Bay and Ambararata Bay to seize Vichy-held Diego Suarez in northern Madagascar; carrier aircraft sank the armed merchant cruiser Bougainville and the submarines Bévéziers and Le Héros. At the end of May a Japanese midget submarine launched by I-20 crept into Diego Suarez harbour, damaging the battleship HMS Ramillies and sinking the tanker British Loyalty. Japanese submarines of the 8th Submarine Squadron then sank some 20 merchant ships in the channel by the end of June 1942. British forces landed at Majunga on 10 September 1942 and at Tuléar on 29 September. U-boats arrived in late 1942: on 28 November U-177 sank the troopship Nova Scotia off Natal, killing 858 of the 1,052 aboard, most of them Italian internees; 194 were rescued by the Portuguese sloop Afonso de Albuquerque. On 3–4 March 1943 U-160 struck convoy DN-21 south of Port St Johns, sinking four ships and damaging two.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Madagascar","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/8th_Submarine_Squadron_(Imperial_Japanese_Navy)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Nova_Scotia_(1926)","https://uboat.net/boats/successes/u160.html"]},117:{name:"Seychelles & Mascarenes",area:"Western Indian Ocean east of Madagascar: Seychelles, Réunion, Mauritius.",lat:"21°S to 4°S",lon:"50°E to 62°E",borders:[116,118,119,140],history:"The islands east of Madagascar lay on Indian Ocean convoy and independent shipping routes and drew U-boats in 1943–44. Réunion, held by Vichy France, was taken for the Free French on the night of 27–28 November 1942, when the destroyer Léopard landed about 80 troops who seized Saint-Denis; a coastal battery exchanged fire with the ship, men died on both sides, and the last Vichy holdouts surrendered on 30 November. In mid-1943 German long-range U-boats operating from refuelling points in the southern Indian Ocean hunted off Mauritius. On 2 July 1943 U-181 under Wolfgang Lüth sank the steamer Hoihow at 19°30'S 55°30'E, about 105 nautical miles from Mauritius, after a ten-hour chase; 145 of the 149 aboard were lost. On 7 August 1943 the same boat sank Umvuma, dispersed from convoy DN-54, south-west of Port Louis; 89 of 111 aboard were rescued and landed at Port Louis. Near the Seychelles, on 12 August 1944, the frigate HMS Findhorn and the Indian sloop HMIS Godavari depth-charged and sank U-198 with all 66 hands.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_destroyer_L%C3%A9opard","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Hoihow","https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/ship/3040.html","https://uboat.net/boats/u198.htm"]},118:{name:"South of Madagascar",area:"Open Indian Ocean south and south-east of Madagascar.",lat:"39°S to 23°S",lon:"46°E to 62°E",borders:[116,117,140],history:"The open ocean south and south-east of Madagascar was far from any battlefront but became a meeting place for Axis submarines. The most famous event came in late April 1943, when, by uboat.net's account, the German U-180 met the Japanese submarine I-29 about 180 miles south-east of Madagascar (other accounts say off Mozambique) and, after a meeting of over twelve hours prolonged by bad weather, transferred the Indian nationalist leader Subhas Chandra Bose and his adjutant Abid Hasan; it is the only known civilian transfer between submarines of different navies in the war. In June 1943 the German tanker Charlotte Schliemann refuelled and reprovisioned a group of long-range U-boats about 600 miles south of Mauritius, after which U-177 patrolled south of Madagascar. On 5 August 1943 U-177, using its Bachstelze autogyro kite to spot targets, sank the Greek steamer Efthalia Mari east of southern Madagascar, the only sinking credited to a Bachstelze sighting; one man died. Nearby to the west, Catalinas sank U-197 with all hands south of Madagascar on 20 August 1943. Otherwise this was a stretch of open, often stormy ocean crossed by independently routed merchant ships between South Africa and the Middle East, India and Australia.",sources:["https://www.uboat.net/ops/monsun2.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_submarine_I-29","https://uboat.net/allies/merchants/ship/3036.html","https://uboat.net/boats/u197.htm"]},119:{name:"Western Indian Ocean (equatorial) & Chagos",area:"Open ocean east of Kenya/Italian Somaliland toward the Maldives, including the Chagos Archipelago (Diego Garcia).",lat:"15°S to 8°N",lon:"52°E to 81°E",borders:[83,85,116,117,120,140],history:"The equatorial western Indian Ocean, crossed by routes linking East Africa, Aden, India and Ceylon, saw some of the worst disasters and atrocities of the naval war. On 12 February 1944 the Japanese submarine I-27 torpedoed the troopship Khedive Ismail south-west of the Maldives' One and a Half Degree Channel; 1,297 of the 1,511 aboard died, and the destroyers HMS Paladin and HMS Petard then sank I-27. Later that month I-37 sank the tanker British Chivalry at 00°50'S 68°00'E on 22 February and machine-gunned survivors, then sank the cargo ship Sutlej on 26 February, again firing on the boats; 50 of her 73 people died. On 26 March 1944 I-8 sank the Dutch Tjisalak at 2°30'S 78°40'E and massacred about 98 survivors taken aboard. Earlier, on 27 February 1941, HMNZS Leander caught the Italian auxiliary cruiser Ramb I near the equator west of the Maldives and her crew scuttled her; survivors were landed at Addu Atoll, where U-183 torpedoed the oil hulk British Loyalty on 9 March 1944. In the Chagos Archipelago the British opened RAF Station Diego Garcia in 1942, an advanced base for flying boats of Nos. 205 and 240 Squadrons hunting submarines and raiders.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_shipwrecks_in_February_1944","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_shipwrecks_in_March_1944","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_auxiliary_cruiser_Ramb_I","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diego_Garcia"]},120:{name:"Central Indian Ocean (Cocos)",area:"Open central-eastern Indian Ocean toward the Cocos (Keeling) Islands.",lat:"17°S to 4°N",lon:"82°E to 99°E",borders:[85,86,119,121,140,141],history:"Few naval actions are recorded in the open central Indian Ocean, which was crossed chiefly by the Allied route between Fremantle, Colombo and Aden. Its main wartime foothold was the Cocos (Keeling) Islands, whose Direction Island cable station and direction-finding post made them valuable. On the night of 8–9 May 1942, fifteen men of the Ceylon Defence Force garrison mutinied and tried to seize the gun battery; the rising was crushed and three men, including its leader Gratien Fernando, were later executed. Japan never tried to occupy the islands, but sent reconnaissance aircraft roughly monthly, and on 25 December 1942 the submarine I-166 shelled them without causing damage. On 11 November 1942, in waters south-west of the Cocos Islands, the Indian corvette HMIS Bengal and the Dutch tanker Ondina fought the Japanese raiders Hokoku Maru and Aikoku Maru; Hokoku Maru blew up and sank, and both Allied ships survived. Later in the war airstrips were built on the Cocos, and RAF squadrons, including Nos. 99 and 356 arriving on West Island in July 1945, used them to strike targets in South-East Asia.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cocos_(Keeling)_Islands","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMIS_Bengal","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%C5%8Dkoku_Maru"]},121:{name:"Indian Ocean off Sumatra & Java",area:"South-west of Sumatra and south of Java, incl. Christmas Island.",lat:"13°S to 1°N",lon:"101°E to 108°E",borders:[86,120,122,132,141],history:"Japan's conquest of Java in early 1942 made these waters deadly for Allied ships. On the night of 28 February–1 March the cruisers USS Houston and HMAS Perth, trying to escape through the Sunda Strait, ran into the Japanese invasion convoy for western Java and its escorts and were both sunk, with the loss of about 1,071 men. The Dutch destroyer Evertsen, following later, was caught by Japanese destroyers and run aground in flames. South of Java the old seaplane tender USS Langley, ferrying 32 P-40 fighters, was bombed on 27 February about 75 miles south of Tjilatjap and scuttled by her escorts; the oiler USS Pecos, carrying many of her survivors, and the destroyer USS Edsall were sunk soon afterwards. On Christmas Island, Punjabi gunners mutinied in March 1942 and killed their British officer and four British soldiers; the Japanese landed unopposed on 31 March, and next day USS Seawolf torpedoed the cruiser Naka offshore. USS Searaven sank the freighter Nissei Maru at the island's wharf on 17 November 1942. On 18 September 1944 the British submarine HMS Tradewind torpedoed the prisoner transport Junyo Maru off western Sumatra, killing more than 5,000 Allied prisoners and Javanese labourers.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Sunda_Strait","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Langley_(CV-1)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Christmas_Island","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jun%27y%C5%8D_Maru"]},122:{name:"Strait of Malacca",area:"Strait of Malacca between Malaya and Sumatra, Singapore approaches and the Andaman Sea's south.",lat:"2°S to 9°N",lon:"95°E to 105°E",borders:[86,87,88,121,123],history:"The Strait of Malacca and the approaches to Singapore saw the Allied collapse of 1942 and a long submarine war afterwards. As Singapore fell, evacuation ships fleeing south were bombed off Bangka Island; the Vyner Brooke, carrying Australian Army nurses and wounded servicemen, was sunk on 14 February 1942, and on 16 February Japanese soldiers massacred 21 of the nurses on Radji Beach, Bangka, leaving Vivian Bullwinkel the only nurse to survive. In September 1943 Operation Jaywick, sailing in the disguised fishing boat Krait, used limpet mines to sink or damage several ships in Singapore harbour. From July 1943 German U-boats of the Monsun group began operating from Penang, and Italian transport submarines reached Singapore and Penang. British submarines hunted the strait: HMS Tally-Ho sank the cruiser Kuma off Penang on 11 January 1944, and HMS Trenchant sank the heavy cruiser Ashigara in the Bangka Strait on 8 June 1945, drowning over 1,200 troops. On 16 May 1945 Captain Manley Power's 26th Destroyer Flotilla, led by HMS Saumarez, sank the cruiser Haguro off Penang, with about 900 Japanese killed. In the war's final weeks the British midget submarine XE3 crippled the cruiser Takao inside Singapore harbour.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Malacca_Strait","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_cruiser_Ashigara","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bangka_Island_massacre","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monsun_Gruppe"]},123:{name:"Borneo & Java Sea (north)",area:"Around Borneo: Makassar Strait, Celebes Sea south, Java Sea north, Balikpapan and Tarakan.",lat:"6°S to 8°N",lon:"109°E to 121°E",borders:[88,90,122,124,133],history:"The waters around Borneo held the oil that drew Japan south. Tarakan was invaded on 11 January 1942, and in the early hours of 24 January four old US destroyers, John D. Ford, Pope, Parrott and Paul Jones, attacked the invasion convoy off Balikpapan and sank four transports, the US Navy's first surface engagement in South-East Asia since 1898, though the town fell next day. The Allied striking force that tried to stop the invasion convoys heading south toward Java was destroyed in the Battles of the Java Sea on 27 February and 1 March 1942, fought off Java's north coast (zone 132). Later Brunei Bay became a fleet anchorage, and on 22 October 1944 Vice Admiral Kurita's Centre Force sortied from there for the Battle of Leyte Gulf. In 1945 Australian forces returned in the Operation Oboe landings: at Tarakan on 1 May, Labuan and Brunei Bay on 10 June, and Balikpapan on 1 July.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Balikpapan_(1942)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Java_Sea","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Battle_of_the_Java_Sea","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borneo_campaign"]},124:{name:"Celebes & Moluccas",area:"Celebes (Sulawesi), Halmahera, the Molucca Sea and Ambon.",lat:"5°S to 5°N",lon:"120°E to 131°E",borders:[90,96,123,125,133,134],history:"Celebes and the Moluccas lay on Japan's eastern route into the Indies in early 1942. On 11 January Japanese naval landing forces sailing from Davao seized Manado in northern Celebes, backed by a naval paratroop drop on Langoan airfield. Kendari fell on 24 January and became a major air base; the US seaplane tender Childs, sent to deliver aviation fuel, narrowly escaped Japanese destroyers and aircraft there that day. Carrier aircraft from Hiryu and Soryu struck Ambon on 24 January, and between 30 January and 3 February the island's Australian Gull Force and Dutch defenders were overwhelmed. The Japanese minesweeper W-9 struck a Dutch mine in Ambon Bay, and partly in revenge about 300 Australian and Dutch prisoners were massacred at Laha. On 19 February land-based bombers from Ambon and Kendari joined the carrier raid on Darwin. The tide turned on 15 September 1944, when a US and Australian task force of some 57,000 personnel landed on Morotai, off Halmahera, against about 500 defenders. Morotai's airfields supported the liberation of the Philippines and the 1945 Borneo landings, and on 9 September 1945 General Thomas Blamey accepted the surrender of the Japanese Second Army there.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Ambon","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Manado","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Morotai","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Kendari"]},125:{name:"Western New Guinea",area:"North-western New Guinea (Vogelkop) and Geelvink Bay.",lat:"5°S to 4°N",lon:"131°E to 139°E",borders:[96,124,126,134],history:"North-western New Guinea was the final stretch of General MacArthur's 1944 advance along the coast toward the Philippines. The US 41st Infantry Division landed on Biak, at the mouth of Geelvink Bay, on 27 May 1944 to seize its airfield sites. Japan responded with Operation Kon, an attempt to run about 2,500 troops from Mindanao to Biak: the first sortie, escorted by a force including the battleship Fuso, turned back after a false report of an American carrier; the second was driven off by American and Australian warships on 8 June; and a third, built around the battleships Yamato and Musashi, was abandoned when the Americans invaded the Marianas. Biak cost the Japanese some 4,700 killed. On 2 July the 158th Regimental Combat Team landed on Noemfoor, followed on 3 and 4 July by drops of the 503rd Parachute Infantry, and the island was declared secure on 7 July. On 30 July 1944, in Operation Typhoon, US troops landed unopposed at Sansapor on the Vogelkop Peninsula to build airfields, and follow-up landings cut off the Japanese garrison at Manokwari. Sansapor was the last of MacArthur's landings on New Guinea before he turned toward Morotai and the Philippines.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Biak","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Noemfoor","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Sansapor"]},126:{name:"Eastern New Guinea & Bismarck Sea",area:"North-eastern New Guinea, the Bismarck Sea, New Britain (Rabaul) and the Admiralties.",lat:"12°S to 2°N",lon:"140°E to 155°E",borders:[96,99,125,127,134,136],history:"The Bismarck Sea and the north coast of New Guinea were contested for much of the war. Japanese troops captured Rabaul on 23 January 1942, overwhelming the Australian Lark Force, and Simpson Harbour became Japan's main base in the region. In August and September 1942 a Japanese naval landing force was defeated at Milne Bay, on New Guinea's eastern tip, and withdrawn by sea; it is often described as the first battle in which Allied troops decisively defeated Japanese land forces. On 2–4 March 1943, in the Battle of the Bismarck Sea, Allied aircraft destroyed a Rabaul–Lae convoy, sinking all eight transports and four of its eight destroyers; some 2,890 Japanese were killed and only about 1,200 troops reached Lae. Australia's 9th Division landed near Lae on 4 September 1943 from ships of the US VII Amphibious Force. US carrier strikes on Rabaul on 5 and 11 November 1943 damaged several cruisers and drove most Japanese warships back to Truk. The landing on Los Negros on 29 February 1944 began the capture of the Admiralty Islands, where Seeadler Harbour became a major fleet base, completing the isolation of Rabaul, whose large garrison was left to wither until the surrender.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Bismarck_Sea","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Rabaul_(1942)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bombing_of_Rabaul_(November_1943)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Admiralty_Islands_campaign"]},127:{name:"Solomon Islands",area:"The Solomons chain (Bougainville, New Georgia, Guadalcanal).",lat:"16°S to 4°S",lon:"157°E to 178°E",borders:[99,100,126,128,136],history:"The Solomons were the scene of the Pacific war's longest naval struggle. Japan occupied Tulagi in May 1942, and on 7 August US Marines landed on Guadalcanal. Two nights later, at the Battle of Savo Island, Vice Admiral Mikawa's cruisers sank USS Astoria, Quincy and Vincennes and HMAS Canberra, killing 1,077 Allied sailors. Months of night actions followed, including Cape Esperance, Tassafaronga and the Naval Battle of Guadalcanal of 12–15 November 1942, in which the battleships Hiei and Kirishima were sunk, Rear Admirals Callaghan and Scott were killed and the cruisers Atlanta and Juneau were lost. More than 50 vessels came to rest in what became known as Ironbottom Sound. About 150 miles south-east of San Cristobal the submarine I-19 sank the carrier USS Wasp on 15 September 1942, and in the Battle of the Santa Cruz Islands on 26 October, fought north of those islands, the carrier Hornet was lost. Japan evacuated Guadalcanal in early 1943. The Allies then pushed up the chain, landing on New Georgia on 30 June 1943 and fighting at Kula Gulf, then landing on Bougainville on 1 November 1943, with the Battle of Empress Augusta Bay fought that night.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Savo_Island","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naval_Battle_of_Guadalcanal","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Santa_Cruz_Islands","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Georgia_campaign"]},128:{name:"Ellice Islands & Samoa area",area:"Open ocean east of the Solomons and Santa Cruz Islands toward the Ellice Islands and Samoa.",lat:"20°S to 5°S",lon:"172°E to 168°W",borders:[100,127,129,136,137],history:"This broad stretch of ocean east of the Solomons saw little fighting, and no major naval battles are recorded here, but it carried the vital sea and air route between the United States and Australia and New Zealand. A Japanese submarine shelled the US naval station at Pago Pago, Tutuila, on 11 January 1942, firing about fifteen rounds that did minor damage; it was the only enemy attack on American Samoa. A Marine brigade of some 5,600 men arrived nine days later, and Tutuila became a garrison and the South Pacific's largest jungle-training site. At Wallis, the Free French aviso Chevreuil ended the island's Vichy loyalty on 27 May 1942, and US Marines landed the following day to build an airfield for heavy bombers. On 2 October 1942 US Marines occupied Funafuti in the Ellice Islands, followed by Nanumea and Nukufetau in August 1943. Funafuti, raided about ten times by Japanese aircraft in 1943, became a forward airfield and fleet anchorage whose lagoon could hold up to 100 ships; its B-24s bombed Nauru and Tarawa in April 1943, and it served as a key base for the November 1943 invasion of the Gilberts.",sources:["https://www.nps.gov/articles/samoawwii.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wallis_and_Futuna_in_World_War_II","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naval_Base_Funafuti","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Funafuti_Airfield"]},129:{name:"Gilbert Islands (probable) & equatorial Pacific",area:"A NW–SE island chain south-east of the Marshalls; most likely the Gilbert Islands (Tarawa, Makin), possibly also the Phoenix/Line Islands.",lat:"12°S to 4°N",lon:"170°E to 155°W",borders:[95,100,101,128,130,137,138],history:"The Gilbert Islands were Japan's south-eastern outpost and the first objective of the US Central Pacific drive. Japanese forces seized Makin on 10 December 1941 and garrisoned Tarawa from September 1942. On 17–18 August 1942 Lieutenant Colonel Evans Carlson's 2nd Raider Battalion, carried by the submarines Nautilus and Argonaut, raided Makin; nine Marines left behind were later executed on Kwajalein, and Japan responded by strengthening its central Pacific defences. Operation Galvanic followed on 20 November 1943. On Betio, Tarawa, a neap tide left landing craft stranded on the reef, and in four days of fighting about 1,000 Marines and nearly all of the roughly 4,500 defenders died, lessons that reshaped American amphibious doctrine. The Army's 27th Division took Butaritari, Makin, by 23 November, but on 24 November the submarine I-175 sank the escort carrier USS Liscome Bay off Makin with the loss of about 700 men, among them Doris Miller. Farther east, the US airfield on Canton Island in the Phoenix group, a staging point for strikes on the Gilberts, was shelled by Japanese submarines in late 1942 and bombed in March 1943, with only slight damage.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Tarawa","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Makin_Island_raid","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Liscome_Bay","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canton_Island"]},130:{name:"Cook Islands & eastern Polynesia",area:"Its island cluster is the board's Cook Islands territory (south-east of the Gilberts); the zone also spans eastern Polynesia toward Tahiti.",lat:"25°S to 8°S",lon:"165°W to 140°W",borders:[101,129,131,138,139],history:"No naval battles are recorded in this remote stretch of the South Pacific; its war was one of bases and supply routes far behind the front. French Oceania, centred on Tahiti, rallied to de Gaulle's Free France on 2 September 1940 in a local coup that the pro-Vichy governor could not resist, and its volunteers went on to serve in the Bataillon du Pacifique, which fought at Bir Hakeim. A US convoy that sailed from Charleston on 27 January 1942 reached Bora Bora on 17 February under Operation Bobcat, landing several thousand troops and Seabees to build a fuel depot of about 225,000 barrels, coastal defences, an airstrip and a seaplane base; from mid-1942 one or two ships a week refuelled there on the way to the South-West Pacific. In the Cook Islands, US troops arrived on Penrhyn (Tongareva) in November 1942 and built an airfield, while New Zealand and contract workers completed Amuri Field on Aitutaki that month, where about 850 US Army personnel were briefly stationed. These airfields formed links in the air route toward Australia. None of these bases ever saw combat, and the Bora Bora base closed in June 1946.",sources:["https://ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USN/Building_Bases/bases-24.html","https://maisondelaculture.pf/n12-2-septembre-1940-le-ralliement-des-efo-a-la-france-libre-bibliographie-et-films-documentaires","https://pacificwrecks.com/location/cook_penrhyn.html","https://tracesofwar.com/sights/150444/Amuri-Field-Airstrip-Aitutaki.htm"]},131:{name:"South-east Pacific (west of Peru)",area:"Open ocean far west of Peru.",lat:"29°S to 9°S",lon:"117°W to 95°W",borders:[102,106,130,139],history:"No major naval actions are recorded in this remote stretch of the south-east Pacific during the Second World War. Its only land is Chilean: Easter Island (Rapa Nui) and the tiny, uninhabited Sala y Gómez. In the war years Chile governed Easter Island, while most of it was leased as a sheep farm to the Williamson-Balfour Company, an arrangement that lasted until 1953, when the government cancelled the lease and handed the island's administration to the Chilean Navy. Chile stayed neutral for much of the war, came under heavy United States pressure, and declared war on Japan only on 13 April 1945, without taking military action. The nearest raider activity was in adjacent waters: in July 1941 the German auxiliary cruiser Komet was resupplied by the freighter Anneliese Essberger near the Tuamotu Archipelago, far to the west, and in August 1941 she sank the British freighters Australind and Devon and captured the Dutch Kota Nopan in the waters around the Galápagos Islands, well to the north. The zone lies in the belt of the south-east trade winds, far from the main wartime shipping lanes.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Easter_Island","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chile_during_World_War_II","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_auxiliary_cruiser_Komet","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_shipwrecks_in_August_1941"]},132:{name:"Java & Java Sea",area:"Java, the Sunda Strait and the Java Sea's south.",lat:"3°S to 11°S",lon:"105°E to 115°E",borders:[121,133,141,142],history:"Java, the Sunda Strait and the southern Java Sea saw the destruction of the Allied ABDA fleet in early 1942. On 27 February Rear Admiral Karel Doorman's cruisers and destroyers met Takeo Takagi's force covering the Japanese invasion convoys in the Battle of the Java Sea; the Dutch cruisers De Ruyter and Java and the destroyers Kortenaer, HMS Electra and HMS Jupiter were sunk, Doorman died with his flagship, and about 2,300 Allied sailors were killed. On the night of 28 February–1 March USS Houston and HMAS Perth ran into the Japanese force landing the 16th Army at Bantam Bay in the Sunda Strait; both cruisers were sunk, with 693 of Houston's 1,061 men and 353 of Perth's 681 killed by the US Navy's count, and most survivors captured, while several Japanese transports were sunk or driven aground, largely by Japanese torpedoes. On 1 March the damaged HMS Exeter, HMS Encounter and USS Pope were caught and sunk in the Second Battle of the Java Sea. South of Java, the carrier USS Langley, ferrying 32 P-40 fighters, was bombed and scuttled about 75 nautical miles south of Tjilatjap on 27 February. Allied forces on Java surrendered on 9 March 1942.",sources:["https://history.navy.mil/browse-by-topic/wars-conflicts-and-operations/world-war-ii/1942/java-sunda/last-battle.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Java_Sea","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Sunda_Strait","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Langley_(CV-1)"]},133:{name:"Lesser Sundas & Timor",area:"Bali to Timor (Flores, Sumba), the Flores Sea and the Banda Sea's south.",lat:"5°S to 12°S",lon:"115°E to 125°E",borders:[123,124,132,134,142],history:"The Lesser Sundas and the Bali and Flores seas lay on the eastern flank of the Japanese advance on Java. On 4 February 1942, in the Battle of the Flores Sea (also called the Battle of Makassar Strait), Japanese land-based bombers caught Doorman's ABDA cruisers in the Bali Sea south of the Kangean Islands, badly damaging USS Marblehead and knocking out USS Houston's after turret; about 70 Allied sailors were killed. Japanese troops landed on Bali on 18–19 February, and on the night of 19/20 February, in the Battle of Badung Strait, four Japanese destroyers drove off a larger Dutch-American force: the Dutch destroyer Piet Hein was sunk, the cruiser Tromp badly damaged and USS Stewart damaged, while the destroyer Michishio was crippled. That same night Japanese troops landed at Dili and in Dutch Timor, and about 300 naval paratroopers dropped near Usua, east of Kupang; the Australian Sparrow Force surrendered on 23 February, though some men escaped east to fight on as guerrillas. A reinforcement convoy from Darwin, escorted by USS Houston, USS Peary, HMAS Swan and HMAS Warrego, had already been turned back by air attack in the Timor Sea, south-east of the island, on 16 February.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Makassar_Strait","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Badung_Strait","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Timor"]},134:{name:"Arafura & Timor Seas",area:"Between northern Australia (Darwin) and New Guinea: Timor Sea, Arafura Sea, Gulf of Carpentaria, Torres Strait.",lat:"16°S to 6°S",lon:"125°E to 154°E",borders:[124,125,126,133,135,136],history:"The Timor and Arafura seas formed Australia's northern front line. On 19 February 1942 aircraft from the carriers Akagi, Kaga, Hiryū and Sōryū, followed by land-based bombers, struck Darwin in the first and largest of more than 100 Japanese air raids on Australia; the destroyer USS Peary, the transport Meigs, the ammunition ship Neptuna, Zealandia, Mauna Loa, British Motorist and other vessels were sunk, and at least 236 people died, with most historians putting the toll at about 250 or more. A month earlier, on 20 January, the Japanese submarine I-124, which had laid mines off Darwin, was sunk with all 80 men after depth-charge attacks led by the corvette HMAS Deloraine. Across the Timor Sea, small warships supplied the Australian guerrillas still fighting on Timor. The destroyer HMAS Voyager ran aground at Betano while landing troops in September 1942, was abandoned after air attack and destroyed with demolition charges. On 1 December 1942 the corvette HMAS Armidale, carrying Dutch reinforcements to Betano, was sunk by Japanese aircraft with heavy loss of life; wounded Ordinary Seaman Teddy Sheean kept firing his Oerlikon gun as she went down and was posthumously awarded the Victoria Cross in 2020.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bombing_of_Darwin","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_submarine_I-124","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMAS_Armidale_(J240)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Timor"]},135:{name:"Coral Sea",area:"Off Queensland: the Coral Sea, Great Barrier Reef and Louisiades.",lat:"26°S to 15°S",lon:"146°E to 158°E",borders:[134,136,146,147],history:"The Coral Sea gave its name to the first battle fought between aircraft carriers. In May 1942 Japan's Operation MO aimed to seize Port Moresby and Tulagi, and Rear Admiral Frank Jack Fletcher's US carrier forces, supported by an Australian–American cruiser force, moved to stop it. On 7 May American aircraft sank the light carrier Shōhō north-east of Misima in the Louisiades, while Japanese carrier aircraft sank the destroyer USS Sims and crippled the oiler Neosho, which was scuttled on 11 May after 123 survivors were taken off. On 8 May the carrier forces exchanged strikes: Shōkaku was damaged and USS Lexington, critically damaged, was scuttled that evening by the destroyer Phelps at 15°20′S 155°30′E, with about 216 of her crew killed. The fleets never sighted each other. A tactical Japanese success, the battle was a strategic Allied victory: the Port Moresby invasion force was recalled and Shōkaku and Zuikaku missed Midway. Later, on 14 May 1943, a Japanese submarine, probably I-177, torpedoed the hospital ship AHS Centaur about 30 nautical miles east of Moreton Island, just south of this zone; 268 of the 332 aboard died.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Coral_Sea","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Lexington_(CV-2)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Neosho_(AO-23)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AHS_Centaur"]},136:{name:"New Caledonia, New Hebrides & Fiji",area:"Melanesia east of the Coral Sea.",lat:"25°S to 14°S",lon:"156°E to 178°W",borders:[126,127,128,134,135,137,147],history:"New Caledonia, the New Hebrides and Fiji became rear bases for the Allied South Pacific campaign. New Caledonia rallied to Free France in 1940: on 19 September Henri Sautot reached Nouméa to take over as governor, with the Australian cruiser HMAS Adelaide in harbour facing the Vichy sloop Dumont d'Urville, which eventually stood down and carried pro-Vichy officials away to Indochina. The US Army's Americal Division later garrisoned New Caledonia, and Nouméa became a major Allied naval base. From 18 October 1942 Vice Admiral William Halsey directed the South Pacific Area from the flagship Argonne in Nouméa harbour. Espiritu Santo in the New Hebrides became a key staging and heavy-bomber base, but its defensive American minefields claimed two US ships. The destroyer USS Tucker struck a mine in the Segond Channel on 4 August 1942, losing three men, and on 26 October 1942 the troopship SS President Coolidge, carrying about 5,000 troops, hit two mines and sank. Only two men died, among them Captain Elwood Euart, who went back to rescue soldiers trapped below; the lost cargo included critical equipment and the entire US stock of the anti-malarial quinine.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rallying_of_New_Caledonia_to_Free_France","https://ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Guadalcanal/USA-P-Guadalcanal-7.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Tucker_(DD-374)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_President_Coolidge"]},137:{name:"South Pacific (Tonga & Cook Islands)",area:"South Pacific east of Fiji (Tonga/Cook area).",lat:"37°S to 21°S",lon:"176°W to 154°W",borders:[128,129,136,138,147,148],history:"No major naval battles are recorded in the open South Pacific east of Fiji, but Tonga played a notable supporting role. United States forces first landed at Nuku'alofa on Tongatapu on 10 May 1942, and Seabees of the 1st Construction Battalion built an airfield and main wharf; at its height about 10,000 Americans were on the island, including the Army's 147th Infantry, and the 1st Marine Division briefly staged through Tonga on its way to Guadalcanal. The anchorage served the fleet: the carrier USS Wasp arrived there on 18 July 1942 for several days of turbine repairs, and on 6 September 1942 the new battleship USS South Dakota struck an uncharted reef in the Lahai Passage after leaving Nuku'alofa, damaging some 150 feet of hull plating; the repair ship Vestal patched her before she sailed for Pearl Harbor. By July 1944 only 31 Americans remained. In late 1940 the German raiders Orion and Komet hunted the Panama–New Zealand shipping routes that crossed this region; their largest victim, the liner Rangitane, was sunk on 27 November 1940 roughly 300 miles east of New Zealand, near or just beyond the zone's south-western edge, with 16 lives lost.",sources:["https://pacificwrecks.com/location/tonga_tongatapu.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_South_Dakota_(BB-57)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Wasp_(CV-7)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RMS_Rangitane"]},138:{name:"South Pacific (Austral Islands)",area:"Open South Pacific south of French Polynesia.",lat:"43°S to 26°S",lon:"152°W to 124°W",borders:[129,130,137,139,148,149],history:"No significant naval actions are recorded in this remote stretch of the South Pacific south of French Polynesia, which takes in Rapa, the southernmost inhabited island of the Austral group, and wide open ocean south of Pitcairn. The islands belonged to French Oceania, which rallied to General de Gaulle's Free France in September 1940, keeping the area on the Allied side. The main wartime installation in French Polynesia lay well to the north, outside this zone: from 14 February 1942 United States forces in Operation Bobcat built a fuelling and supply base on Bora Bora with about 5,000 personnel, which refuelled 181 ships before closing in 1946 without ever seeing combat. German raiders did pass through the wider region. In late 1940 the auxiliary cruisers Orion and Komet cruised the shipping routes between Panama and New Zealand, and in July 1941 Komet was resupplied by the freighter Anneliese Essberger near the Tuamotu Archipelago, north of this zone. No attacks by them are recorded within these waters. The zone lies between the south-east trade winds and the stormier westerlies further south, far from the main theatres of the Pacific war and crossed by few shipping lanes.",sources:["https://welcome-tahiti.com/en/operation-bobcat/","https://pacificeagles.net/german-raiders-in-the-pacific/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_auxiliary_cruiser_Komet"]},139:{name:"South-east Pacific (west of Chile)",area:"Open ocean west of central Chile.",lat:"48°S to 30°S",lon:"122°W to 95°W",borders:[106,111,130,131,138,149],history:"No naval actions are recorded in this stretch of open ocean west of central Chile during the Second World War. It lies well offshore, with the Juan Fernández Islands and the Chilean coast to the east, in the belt of the westerlies, where gales are frequent in its southern part, and wartime shipping crossed it only sparsely. Chile remained neutral for much of the war, partly from fear that submarine attacks would damage its trade, and declared war on Japan only on 13 April 1945, without taking military action. Chilean losses at sea, such as the steamer Toltén sunk off New Jersey with all 27 crew in March 1942, occurred far away in the Atlantic. German auxiliary cruisers used the Cape Horn passage between the Atlantic and Pacific: Orion entered the Pacific round the Horn in May 1940 on her way to New Zealand waters, and Komet set course for Cape Horn on her way home in the autumn of 1941. Their exact tracks are not well documented, and no attacks by either ship are recorded in these waters. The zone was therefore a quiet backwater, far from the main Pacific theatres and from the convoy routes of the Atlantic.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chile_during_World_War_II","https://pacificeagles.net/german-raiders-in-the-pacific/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_auxiliary_cruiser_Komet"]},140:{name:"Southern Indian Ocean",area:"Southern Indian Ocean east of Madagascar toward the open ocean.",lat:"38°S to 18°S",lon:"64°E to 95°E",borders:[117,118,119,120,141,143],history:"This remote stretch of the southern Indian Ocean, east of Mauritius and Rodrigues, saw no surface battles, but its emptiness made it a refuelling ground for Germany's long-range Monsun U-boats. The supply tanker Charlotte Schliemann had fuelled six U-boats further west in June 1943; sailing again from Batavia in January 1944, she refuelled U-boats in late January, one of them near 24°S 68°E by a US intelligence account. In February 1944, before the next boats could be supplied, she was intercepted by the British destroyer HMS Relentless and scuttled at about 23°S 75°E; Relentless took some survivors prisoner and U-532 picked up others. Her replacement, the tanker Brake, left Singapore around 29 February and began fuelling U-boats on 11–12 March 1944 near 32°S 74°E, though bad weather hampered the work. On 12 March she was found by a British force including the escort carrier HMS Battler, the cruisers Suffolk and Newcastle and the destroyers Roebuck and Quadrant, and went down at about 32°S 72°E; accounts differ on whether Roebuck's gunfire or scuttling by her crew sank her. U-168 rescued the survivors. Both U-boat supply tankers in these waters had been lost within about six weeks.",sources:["https://ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-BA-Appendix/Appendix-10.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_shipwrecks_in_February_1944","https://naval-history.net/xGM-Chrono-10DD-53R-HMS_Roebuck.htm","https://uboat.net/ops/monsun3.htm"]},141:{name:"Eastern Indian Ocean (west of Australia)",area:"Open Indian Ocean west of North-West Cape.",lat:"28°S to 14°S",lon:"98°E to 107°E",borders:[120,121,132,140,142,143,144],history:"This stretch of the eastern Indian Ocean, west of North-West Cape and south of Christmas Island, saw its worst losses as Java fell. On 1 March 1942 dive-bombers from the Japanese carriers Akagi, Kaga, Hiryu and Soryu sank the US Navy oiler USS Pecos at about 14°27'S, 106°11'E, on the zone's northern edge. Pecos had been ordered to Australia carrying several hundred survivors from the aircraft tender USS Langley and other ships; the destroyer USS Whipple rescued about 232 men, but more than 400 died. The same day Japanese battleships, cruisers and carrier dive-bombers sank the destroyer USS Edsall roughly 200 miles south-east of Christmas Island, close to the zone's northern edge; only eight men were picked up, as prisoners. The duel between HMAS Sydney and the raider Kormoran in November 1941 was fought in neighbouring waters to the east, off Shark Bay. Later these waters lay beneath the Allied air route between Perth and Ceylon: from June 1943 to July 1945 Qantas Catalinas flew the 'Double Sunrise' service from the Swan River to Lake Koggala, making 271 Indian Ocean crossings without loss.",sources:["https://uboat.net/allies/warships/ship/8074.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Pecos_(AO-6)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Edsall_(DD-219)","https://www.airforce.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-07/FINAL%20HH-AF%20KA%20Double%20Sunrise.pdf"]},142:{name:"Off north-west Australia",area:"Off Western Australia's north-west coast (Exmouth Gulf, Broome).",lat:"25°S to 17°S",lon:"107°E to 120°E",borders:[132,133,141,144],history:"Western Australia's north-west coast was the front door to the Netherlands East Indies, and its waters saw Australia's worst naval disaster. On 19 November 1941, about 106 nautical miles off Dirk Hartog Island near Shark Bay, the cruiser HMAS Sydney and the German raider Kormoran destroyed each other; all 645 of Sydney's crew died, while 318 of Kormoran's 399 men survived. As Java fell, Broome became a staging point for evacuation flights, and on 3 March 1942 nine Zero fighters from Kupang, Timor, strafed Roebuck Bay and Broome airfield, destroying at least 22 Allied aircraft, including 15 flying boats, several still carrying Dutch refugees; at least 88 people died. That morning, far offshore, the Japanese submarine I-1 torpedoed and shelled the Dutch ship Siantar some 250 miles north-west of Shark Bay, killing 21. Exmouth Gulf later became an advanced US Navy submarine base, Operation Potshot, served by the tender USS Pelias; Japanese bombers raided it without effect on 20 and 21 May 1943. In September 1943 the Operation Jaywick raid on Japanese shipping in Singapore Harbour was launched from Potshot.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinking_of_HMAS_Sydney","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attack_on_Broome","http://www.combinedfleet.com/I-1.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Potshot"]},143:{name:"Southern Indian Ocean (east)",area:"Open ocean far south-west of Australia.",lat:"44°S to 28°S",lon:"95°E to 105°E",borders:[140,141,144],history:"No battles are recorded in this remote stretch of the southern Indian Ocean far south-west of Australia, but it saw one of the last U-boat successes of the war. On 6 February 1945 the German submarine U-862, returning to Batavia after a patrol around Australia and New Zealand, torpedoed the American Liberty ship Peter Silvester at about 34°19'S, 99°37'E, some 900 miles west of Cape Leeuwin. She was carrying US Army supplies and 317 mules bound for Burma. About 33 men died; survivors were picked up over the following weeks by ships including USS Corpus Christi and the escort carrier HMS Activity, and the last fifteen were rescued by USS Rock on 9 March, after about a month adrift. One lifeboat was never found. She was the last ship sunk by a U-boat in the Indian Ocean. Otherwise these are open waters on the northern fringe of the Roaring Forties, swept by strong westerly winds and heavy swell. When German U-boats were expected off Western Australia late in the war, westbound Allied shipping was ordered to pass at least 250 miles south of Cape Leeuwin, well out into the open ocean.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Peter_Silvester","https://seapower.navy.gov.au/node/4943"]},144:{name:"Off south-west Australia",area:"Off Western Australia's south-west coast (Fremantle/Perth, Albany).",lat:"49°S to 27°S",lon:"105°E to 118°E",borders:[141,142,143,145],history:"Fremantle, the port of Perth, was the hub of this zone's war. From March 1942 to August 1945 it was the largest Allied submarine base in the Southern Hemisphere: more than 170 American, British and Dutch submarines operated from it, making 416 war patrols into enemy-held waters. An anti-submarine boom guarded the harbour from December 1940, and giant troopships such as Queen Mary and Queen Elizabeth anchored in Gage Roads. Japanese submarines arrived as Java fell: on 2 and 3 March 1942 I-3 surfaced and fired on the steamer Narbada about 90 miles west-north-west of Fremantle, and on 3 March chased the armed steamer Tongariro about 90 miles south-west of Rottnest Island, but each time return fire drove her off without a direct hit. In late 1944 Germany planned a U-boat offensive off this coast; Allied codebreakers learned of it, westbound shipping was routed at least 250 miles south of Cape Leeuwin, and of the boats sent only U-862 arrived, reaching Cape Leeuwin on 28 November 1944 before moving east into the Great Australian Bight. The loss of HMAS Sydney in November 1941 occurred further north, off Shark Bay.",sources:["https://museum.wa.gov.au/secret-fleets-fremantles-world-war-ii-submarine-base-0","https://fremantleports.com.au/the-port/history-and-heritage/wartime","http://www.combinedfleet.com/I-3.htm","https://seapower.navy.gov.au/node/4943"]},145:{name:"Great Australian Bight",area:"The Great Australian Bight off South Australia.",lat:"53°S to 34°S",lon:"120°E to 140°E",borders:[144,146],history:"The Great Australian Bight saw few battles, but German raiders and a U-boat both reached it. In late 1940 the raider Pinguin laid mines in the approaches to Adelaide and Port Lincoln, in Spencer Gulf, Investigator Strait near Kangaroo Island and Gulf St Vincent, finishing the Investigator Strait field on 7 November. On 7 December 1940 the British freighter Hertford was badly damaged by a mine near Liguanea Island at the entrance to Spencer Gulf and repaired at Port Lincoln before being towed to Adelaide. In July 1941 a drifting mine came ashore at Beachport and exploded, killing two men. In late November 1944 U-862, under Heinrich Timm, rounded Cape Leeuwin and spent about a week searching the Bight without success. On 9 December 1944 she attacked the Greek steamer Ilissos off Cape Jaffa, about 130 miles south-east of Adelaide, but rough seas and the ship's return fire forced her to break off; the corvettes Burnie and Maryborough hunted for her in vain. Otherwise the Bight carried coastal shipping between Western Australia and the eastern states, including iron ore ships sailing from Whyalla in Spencer Gulf to the steelworks at Newcastle.",sources:["https://mail.ozatwar.com/ozatwar/germanmines.htm","https://seapower.navy.gov.au/node/4943","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Iron_Crown"]},146:{name:"Tasman Sea (west) & Bass Strait",area:"Off south-east Australia (Sydney, Melbourne), Bass Strait and Tasmania.",lat:"52°S to 30°S",lon:"142°E to 160°E",borders:[135,145,147],history:"South-east Australia's waters saw the heaviest enemy action off the Australian coast. In late 1940 German raiders mined the approaches to Bass Strait, the converted tanker Passat laying fields off Cape Otway and Wilsons Promontory: the British Cambridge sank off Wilsons Promontory on 7 November and the American City of Rayville off Cape Otway a day or two later, the first United States merchant ship lost in the war. On 5 December a raider's mine sank the freighter Nimbin off Norah Head, New South Wales, with seven dead. On 20 November 1940 the minesweeper HMAS Goorangai was cut in two by the liner Duntroon at the mouth of Port Phillip, the navy's first loss of the war, with all 24 aboard. On the night of 31 May 1942 three Japanese midget submarines entered Sydney Harbour; a torpedo aimed at the cruiser USS Chicago sank the depot ship HMAS Kuttabul, killing 21. I-24 shelled Sydney and I-21 Newcastle on 8 June, and I-27 sank the freighter Iron Crown on 4 June with heavy loss of life. I-21 returned in early 1943, sinking three more ships. On 25 December 1944 U-862 sank the Liberty ship Robert J. Walker off Moruya.",sources:["https://mail.ozatwar.com/ozatwar/germanmines.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMAS_Goorangai","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attack_on_Sydney_Harbour","https://seapower.navy.gov.au/node/4943"]},147:{name:"New Zealand & Tasman Sea (east)",area:"New Zealand and the eastern Tasman Sea.",lat:"55°S to 23°S",lon:"161°E to 173°W",borders:[135,136,137,146,148],history:"New Zealand's waters were raided by German surface ships rather than fought over by fleets. On the night of 13–14 June 1940 the auxiliary cruiser Orion laid mines off Auckland, and on 19 June one sank the liner RMS Niagara off Whangarei, carrying British gold bound for the United States. On 20 August 1940 Orion sank the freighter Turakina nearly 500 km off the Taranaki coast after the first gun battle ever fought in the Tasman Sea; 36 of her crew died. Orion and Komet then sank the small steamer Holmwood off the Chatham Islands on 25 November and the liner Rangitane about 550 km off East Cape on 27 November, killing 16. On 14 May 1941 the minesweeper HMNZS Puriri struck a mine while sweeping in the Hauraki Gulf and sank, with five dead. In March 1942 a floatplane from the submarine I-25 flew over Wellington and Auckland, and in May an aircraft from I-21 overflew Auckland. In January 1945 U-862 cruised down the North Island's east coast, firing a single torpedo that missed a small merchant ship near Napier on 16 January.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axis_naval_activity_in_New_Zealand_waters","https://nzhistory.govt.nz/turakina-sunk-by-german-raider-in-tasman-sea","https://nzhistory.govt.nz/page/liner-sunk-german-raiders","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_auxiliary_cruiser_Orion"]},148:{name:"Southern Pacific (south-east of New Zealand)",area:"Open ocean south-east of New Zealand.",lat:"61°S to 37°S",lon:"170°W to 135°W",borders:[137,138,147,149],history:"No major naval actions are recorded in this vast stretch of the South Pacific south-east of New Zealand, which lay far from the war's main battlefields. The nearest raider attacks took place in adjacent New Zealand waters to the west, where in November 1940 the German auxiliary cruisers Orion and Komet sank the small steamer Holmwood off the Chatham Islands and then the liner Rangitane about 550 km east of New Zealand, killing 16 people. Komet later set course for Cape Horn on her homeward voyage in late 1941, so German raiders may have passed through these latitudes, but their exact tracks are not well documented and no encounters are recorded here. Nor are any Allied merchant losses recorded within the zone. The region spans the Roaring Forties and Furious Fifties, where persistent westerly gales, heavy swell and, in its southern reaches, icebergs make it one of the stormiest oceans on earth. For ships and aircraft alike these were dangerous, empty waters, and Allied naval and air forces based in New Zealand were the nearest military presence.",sources:["https://nzhistory.govt.nz/page/liner-sunk-german-raiders","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_auxiliary_cruiser_Komet","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axis_naval_activity_in_New_Zealand_waters"]},149:{name:"Southern Pacific (far south-east)",area:"Open ocean toward Antarctica west of South America.",lat:"67°S to 46°S",lon:"133°W to 95°W",borders:[111,138,139,148],history:"No naval actions are recorded in this remote corner of the South Pacific, which stretches from the Southern Ocean toward the Antarctic ice edge, far west of southern Chile. It lay well away from wartime convoy routes: ships sailing between Australia or New Zealand and the Panama Canal passed far to the north, and those using the Strait of Magellan or rounding Cape Horn did so far to the east. The great-circle track between New Zealand and Cape Horn does cross these high latitudes, and German auxiliary cruisers could use the Cape Horn passage to move between oceans; Komet, for example, set course for the Horn on her voyage home in late 1941, but no encounters are recorded here. The nearest notable German raider sinking in the eastern South Pacific was the Norwegian tanker India, shelled and set on fire by Michel on 11 September 1943 west of Easter Island, far to the north. This is an ocean of the Furious Fifties and Screaming Sixties, with near-constant westerly gales, enormous swells, fog and drifting icebergs, and in winter the sea ice approaches its southern edge.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_auxiliary_cruiser_Komet","https://www.warsailors.com/singleships/india.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_auxiliary_cruiser_Michel"]}};export{e as default};
